/* ML Academy · THE EXPOSITION (English)
   Same keys as anlatim.js: 'lessonId:stepNo'. Read before touching the model. */
var ANLATIM_EN = {
  'veri:1': '<p>Machine learning starts with a table. That sounds ordinary, but the whole field rests on it: <b>we show the world to a model as a table</b>. However complex a problem is, if it cannot be put into that shape the model cannot see it.</p><p>A table has two directions and they say different things. The <b>rows</b> tell you who was measured and the <b>columns</b> what was measured. In a table of students every row is a student and every column a piece of information about that student.</p><p>That distinction looks trivial but it directly determines what a model can learn. A model looks for a <b>pattern</b> across the rows: "when this column is large, is that one large too?" For the question to be askable at all, the table has to be built properly first.</p>',
  'veri:2': '<p>The columns of a table are not equal. Some are information we <b>give</b> the model and one is the answer we <b>ask</b> of it.</p><p>What we give is called a <b>feature</b> and is usually written x. What we ask for is called the <b>label</b>, written y. If you are predicting the price of a house, the floor area, the number of rooms and the district are features; the price is the label.</p><p>You make that split, not the data. Ask a different question of the same table and a different column becomes the label. So <b>the label is a property of the question, not of the data</b>.</p><p>And the critical point: the label is the thing you do <b>not</b> have at prediction time. If you have it there is nothing to predict. That sentence looks simple, but in the data leakage lesson it turns out to be the source of a whole class of errors.</p>',
  'veri:3': '<p>The columns told you what was measured. The <b>rows tell you who</b>. Every row is a single observation: one student, one transaction, one patient, one photograph.</p><p>A model cannot learn from a single row. One row only says "this student worked 6 hours and scored 66"; that is a fact, not a rule. A rule only appears when you look at <b>many rows at once</b>: "as study hours grow the score grows."</p><p>This is why the size of a dataset is not an incidental detail. Every new row wipes a little more noise off the rule you are looking for. A tendency found in ten rows may be luck; one found in a thousand is probably real.</p>',
  'veri:4': '<p>A table is a correct representation but a bad one for the human eye. Look at ten rows and you will not see the relationship; put the same ten rows on a plot and it appears at a glance.</p><p>The transformation is simple: <b>every row becomes a point</b>. You put a feature on the horizontal axis and the label on the vertical one. Now "as study hours grow the score grows" is no longer a sentence but a <b>rise</b> on your screen.</p><p>Almost every plot you see in this course rests on that transformation. And a model does the same thing: it looks at the points and searches for a rule that runs through them.</p>',
  'veri:5': '<p>The word "model" is vague in everyday speech. In machine learning its definition is very narrow: <b>a model is a function that turns an input into an output.</b> It contains a few adjustable numbers, and learning means adjusting them.</p><p>In its simplest form it is a line: <code>y = w·x + b</code>. Here x is the input, y the prediction and <b>w and b are the model\'s knowledge</b>. Saying the model "learned" means those two numbers changed to fit the data. There is no other magic.</p><p>A huge language model has the same structure; it just has hundreds of billions of numbers instead of two. This is why the answer to "how does AI learn" is the same at every scale, and the next lesson shows exactly that.</p>',

  'ezber:1': '<p>How do you tell whether a model works? The first answer that comes to mind is wrong: "does it give the right answer on the data I gave it?"</p><p>That criterion is wrong because it cannot tell <b>memorising</b> the data from <b>understanding</b> it. A program that writes your table into memory verbatim and a program that finds the underlying rule both look perfect on that data.</p><p>The two approaches sit side by side here. One remembers every student individually, the other extracts the rule "as study hours grow the score grows". Both look at the same data and both appear to work.</p><p>The only place you can see the difference is a student <b>neither of them has seen</b>. The whole lesson rests on that.</p>',
  'ezber:2': '<p>Testing a model on the data it learned from is like giving a student an exam made of exactly the questions they revised. They get a high mark, and the mark proves nothing.</p><p>A memorising model always gets full marks on that exam, because it has seen every question before and stored the answer. A rule learning model also scores highly, but it does not have to be perfect; a rule need not pass exactly through every point.</p><p>The inversion here matters: <b>the model that looks better on the training data can be the worse model.</b> Because a perfect fit is usually a sign of memorisation rather than understanding.</p><p>This is why machine learning has one rule with no exceptions: <b>test a model on data it did not see while learning.</b></p>',
  'ezber:3': '<p>Now the real question: what will the model say about a student it has never seen?</p><p>The memorising model does not have that student. All it can do is find the closest record it has and return that answer. Sometimes it works, mostly it does not, and nobody can explain why it did or did not.</p><p>The rule learning model applies its formula. Never having seen the new student is not a problem, because it learned a <b>rule</b>, not a <b>list</b>.</p><p>That difference has a name: <b>generalisation</b>, the ability to behave correctly on unseen data. It is the only thing worth measuring in machine learning. Success on the training data means nothing next to it.</p>',
  'ezber:4': '<p>Both failures have standard names and both are dangerous.</p><p><b>Overfitting:</b> the model took the data too seriously and mistook the noise for the rule. Perfect in training, bad on new data. The symptom is clear: a low training error and a high test error.</p><p><b>Underfitting:</b> the model did not take the data seriously at all; it is not flexible enough to catch the rule. This time it is bad in both training and testing.</p><p>These are the two ends of one dial, and <b>there is a sweet spot in the middle</b>. Much of this course is about ways of finding it: penalty terms, cross validation, early stopping. They all answer the same question: how flexible should the model be?</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:1': '<p>The previous lesson gave you the definition of a model: a function that turns an input into an output, with a few adjustable numbers inside. Now we come to adjusting those numbers.</p><p>The model here is a line: <code>y = w·x + b</code>. It has two numbers. <b>w is the slope</b>: how much y changes when x grows by one unit. <b>b is the intercept</b>: where the line crosses the vertical axis. Change these two and the line rotates and shifts; it can do nothing else.</p><p>"Learning" means bringing those two numbers to the values that best fit the data. For now <b>you</b> will do it, by hand. The point is to feel how hard the job is: with two numbers trial and error is possible. A language model has billions of numbers and there is no such thing as adjusting them by hand. The rest of this lesson builds the method that automates what you are about to do manually.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:2': '<p>To improve something automatically you first have to be able to <b>measure</b> it. "This line fits the data well" is an impression; you cannot give a computer an impression, you have to give it a number.</p><p>The measurement is built like this. For every point, the difference between the model\'s prediction and the true value is called the <b>residual</b>. Some residuals are positive and some negative; add them directly and they cancel out, making a bad model look good. So each one is <b>squared</b> first: that removes the sign problem and punishes large errors disproportionately.</p><p>The mean of those squares is the <b>mean squared error</b> (MSE). It is a single number and the smaller it is the better the model. Measures like this are called <b>loss functions</b>, and without one the word "learning" has no definition: a system that does not know what to improve can learn nothing.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:3': '<p>You can now compute a single number for every pair (w, b). That means: <b>there is a map of all possible models.</b></p><p>Picture it: w on the horizontal axis, b on the vertical one, and the height of every point is that model\'s loss. A landscape appears. The high ground is bad models and the hollows are good ones. What you are looking for is <b>the lowest point</b>.</p><p>This view redefines the whole field: <b>learning is searching a landscape for its lowest point.</b> Training a model is not a discovery but a search problem.</p><p>There is one catch. The only reason I can draw this map is that there are two parameters. With three you cannot draw it and with a million you cannot even imagine it. In real training <b>the map is invisible.</b> The next step is entirely about that constraint.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:4': '<p>You cannot see the map. So what can you see? <b>The slope at the point you are standing on.</b></p><p>Imagine being on a mountain in fog. You do not know where the valley is, but you can feel which way the ground under your feet tilts. If you want to go down, what to do is clear: take a step in the steepest downhill direction, then look again.</p><p>In mathematics that slope is called the <b>derivative</b>. Because there are two parameters there are two slopes: how much the loss changes in the w direction and in the b direction. The vector formed by those two is called the <b>gradient</b>, and the gradient points in the direction of steepest <b>ASCENT</b>.</p><p>This is why you go in the <b>opposite</b> direction to go down. That is where the method gets its name: gradient descent.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:5': '<p>Now let us take a single step apart. Every step consists of the same three jobs:</p><p><b>1 · Measure.</b> Predict with the current w and b and compute the error. <b>2 · Find the direction.</b> Compute the slope of the loss in the w and b directions. <b>3 · Take a step.</b> Move each parameter a small amount against its own slope.</p><p>The "small amount" in the third step is a multiplier called the <b>learning rate</b> (η). The slope only tells you the <b>direction</b>, not how far to go. That decision is yours.</p><p>The whole thing fits on one line: <code>θ ← θ − η·∇L</code>. θ is the parameters, ∇L the gradient and η the learning rate. That line will come back again and again in this course, because almost every model trained today is trained with it.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:6': '<p>A single step barely changes the model. Its power is in repetition.</p><p>Do the same three jobs over and over and the model slowly descends to the floor of the valley. What to notice here is that <b>the speed is not uniform</b>: at the start the slope is steep, so the steps are long and progress is fast. As you approach the bottom the slope shrinks, the steps shorten and the model starts crawling.</p><p>That slowdown is not a flaw but the nature of the method: the step size is proportional to the slope, and the slope goes to zero at the bottom. It is annoying in practice though, and it is exactly why modern optimisers (Momentum, Adam) exist; you will meet them on Track 2.</p><p>The real message here: <b>there is no single clever move in learning.</b> There is the same dumb step repeated thousands of times.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:7': '<p>The learning rate η is not something the model learns. <b>You choose it.</b> Settings like this are called <b>hyperparameters</b>, and much of a good result in machine learning comes from choosing them well.</p><p>If η is chosen badly one of two things happens, and both are bad.</p><p><b>Too small:</b> the direction is right but the steps are tiny. The model is on the right path, it just never arrives. In a real project that means GPU days burned for nothing.</p><p><b>Too large:</b> every step overshoots the valley onto the opposite slope, and higher up at that. The error grows every round, the numbers overflow and you see <code>loss: nan</code> on your screen. Instead of learning, the model moves away.</p><p>The healthy range in between is not wide and it varies by problem. This is why η is the first thing tuned before training starts.</p>',
  'nasil-ogrenir:8': '<p>Everything you learned comes together in one line here, and the algorithm will actually run.</p><p>Three things to watch. <b>The sign:</b> the gradient points uphill, so it has to be a minus; make it a plus and the model climbs the hill (gradient ascent) and the error grows. <b>The multiplication:</b> the step size is the learning rate times the slope; divide instead and the step scales inversely and the model blows up. <b>The order:</b> the slope is computed first, then the parameter is updated.</p><p>It will run even if you write it wrong, and you will see the result. The fastest way to learn is to see with your own eyes what the wrong answer produces.</p>',

  'ezberleme:1': '<p>The previous lesson set a rule: test a model on data it has not seen. This lesson makes that rule workable.</p><p>The method is simple and non-negotiable: split the data in two from the start. The <b>training set</b> is the part the model sees in order to learn. The <b>test set</b> stays locked; the model never sees it during training.</p><p>Why split at the very beginning? Because once a model has seen a piece of data, that data stops being an exam. Show a student the exam question in advance and the exam is void; the same applies here.</p><p>In this lesson the model will see 10 of the 14 points and never see the other 4. All the measurement happens on those 4.</p>',
  'ezberleme:2': '<p>You also choose how flexible the model will be. Here the flexibility is set by the <b>polynomial degree</b>.</p><p>Degree 1 is a line: it can only rotate and shift. Degree 2 is a parabola and can bend once. As the degree grows the curve can make more bends, that is get closer to the points.</p><p>At the extreme this happens: with a high enough degree the curve <b>passes exactly through every training point</b>. The training error goes to zero. That looks like a success.</p><p>The one thing to understand here: <b>the training error always falls as model complexity rises.</b> That is not an observation but a mathematical necessity; whatever a less flexible model can do, a more flexible one can already do. So the training error cannot be a measure of quality.</p>',
  'ezberleme:3': '<p>Now we look at the points we kept hidden. The model never saw them, so this is a real exam.</p><p>What you will see: while the training error falls, the test error <b>starts to rise</b> past a certain point. That is, the more flexible the model becomes the worse it predicts.</p><p>The reason is that the data contains two things at once. There is a <b>rule</b> (the thing we are after) and there is <b>noise</b> (measurement error, chance, individual variation). A flexible model cannot tell them apart; to sit exactly on the points it learns the noise as well. And noise by definition does not repeat: in new data it is completely different.</p><p>This is called <b>overfitting</b>, and the only way to catch it is to measure on data the model has not seen.</p>',
  'ezberleme:4': '<p>Put the two curves side by side and you get the most recognisable plot in machine learning.</p><p>The <b>training error</b> falls monotonically with complexity. The <b>test error</b> falls first, passes through a minimum, then rises. A <b>U</b> appears, and what you are looking for is the bottom of that U.</p><p>The region on the left is <b>underfitting</b>: the model is not flexible enough to catch the rule and is bad in both training and testing. The region on the right is <b>overfitting</b>: the model learned the noise too, and is perfect in training and bad in testing. The balance between them is the most fundamental trade-off in the field, and on Track 1 it will be decomposed numerically under the name <b>bias-variance</b>.</p><p>The practical consequence is one sentence: "100% on the training set" is a warning sign, not something to boast about.</p>',
  'algoritma:1': '<p>An algorithm is a precise sequence of steps for doing a job. Like a recipe: what to do in what order is fixed, there is no room for interpretation, and the same input gives the same result every time.</p><p>This lesson is about sorting, but sorting is not the goal in itself. <b>You sort in order to make searching cheap.</b></p><p>Here is the difference. Search an unsorted list and all you can do is look from beginning to end; with n elements you spend n steps on average. If the list is sorted you look at the middle element, work out which half your target is in, and eliminate the other half in one move. Every look halves the search space, so <b>log₂n</b> steps are enough.</p><p>That is not "a bit faster". On a billion records it is the difference between a billion steps and thirty. This is why the choice of algorithm is usually more decisive than the hardware.</p>',
  'algoritma:2': '<p>You can sort eight shuffled boxes at a glance. A computer cannot, because <b>it does not see them all at once.</b></p><p>A computer has only two moves: <b>compare</b> two elements and <b>swap</b> two elements. It can do nothing else. There is no instruction saying "put the big one at the end"; even finding the big one takes a series of comparisons.</p><p>So "sort" is not an algorithm, it is a <b>wish</b>. An algorithm is the thing that says, step by step, how to fulfil that wish using only compare and swap.</p><p>That is the essence of programming: translating what a human does at a glance into the primitive moves a machine can actually make.</p>',
  'algoritma:3': '<p>Bubble sort repeats a single rule, and that rule is one sentence: <b>look at a neighbouring pair, swap them if they are out of order, move one to the right.</b></p><p>When you reach the end of the list a <b>pass</b> is complete. Then you start again from the beginning. That is all.</p><p>Watch for this: at no point does the algorithm see the whole list. At any moment it looks at just two neighbours. And yet it produces the right result. In the next step you will see that this is not a coincidence but a necessity.</p><p>The name comes from that: on every pass the large numbers <b>bubble</b> towards the end like bubbles rising to the surface.</p>',
  'algoritma:4': '<p>To "understand" an algorithm is not to memorise its steps. It is <b>to be able to show why it produces the right answer.</b></p><p>Bubble sort\'s proof fits in one sentence: during a pass the largest remaining number is carried one step right at every comparison, because it is larger than every element it meets. So <b>at the end of every pass the largest remaining number is definitely in place.</b></p><p>From that it follows directly: after k passes the last k elements are certainly correct, and after n passes the whole list is sorted. That is not an observation but a guarantee; it holds for any list you give it.</p><p>The cost follows from the same reasoning. Every pass scans the whole list and there are n passes, so the number of comparisons grows roughly with n². That fact matters later: n² growth is acceptable at a thousand elements and not at a million.</p>',
  'algoritma:5': '<p>Everything you have seen so far was a <b>classical algorithm</b>: a human writes the rule and the computer applies it. The rule is fixed, provable, and gives the same answer to the same input every time.</p><p>Machine learning differs in exactly one place: <b>the human does not write the rule.</b> The human supplies examples and a learning procedure, and the computer extracts the rule from the data itself.</p><p>That change has a price. In a classical algorithm you can show line by line why the output is what it is; in machine learning you cannot, because the rule consists of numbers nobody wrote. And the output is probabilistic rather than certain.</p><p>What you get in return is this: <b>you can solve problems whose rule you cannot write.</b> You cannot spell out "is there a cat in this photo" step by step, but you can show tens of thousands of examples. The rest of the course is built on that trade.</p>',

  'siniflandirma:1': '<p>So far the model predicted a <b>number</b>: an exam score, a house price. That is called <b>regression</b>. Now we will predict a <b>category</b>: pass or fail, spam or not. That is called <b>classification</b>.</p><p>The difference is not just the type of the output; the <b>geometry</b> of the job changes too. In regression the model looks for a curve that runs <b>through</b> the points. In classification it looks for a line that <b>separates</b> them.</p><p>That separating line is called the <b>decision boundary</b>. The model assigns a label by looking at which side of the boundary a point falls on. So classification is essentially the job of <b>dividing space</b>.</p><p>Different models divide it differently: logistic regression draws a straight boundary, decision trees make staircases perpendicular to the axes, neural networks can draw any curve they like. Tracks 1 and 2 are largely lessons about those differences.</p>',
  'siniflandirma:2': '<p>A model does not really say "spam" directly. It first produces a <b>probability</b>: "this is 83% likely to be spam". The label comes from applying a <b>threshold</b> to that probability, usually 0.5.</p><p>Where the probability comes from matters: from the <b>distance to the decision boundary</b>. For a point sitting right on the boundary the model is undecided and the probability is near 0.5. The further from the boundary, the more confident the model and the closer the probability to the extremes.</p><p>The real lesson here: <b>the threshold is not part of the model, it is your decision.</b> You can move it without changing the model at all and the behaviour changes completely.</p><p>And it is not a technical setting but a <b>cost decision</b>. Lower the threshold and you catch more but raise more false alarms. Which is more expensive depends on the problem: in a spam filter throwing away a legitimate email is severe, in cancer screening missing a patient is severe. The next lesson is about measuring exactly that.</p>',

  'metrikler:1': '<p>Accuracy is the most intuitive measure: the share of correct predictions among all predictions. And for exactly that reason it is the most misleading one.</p><p>The problem appears with <b>imbalanced data</b>: fraud, a rare disease, a manufacturing defect. In these problems the thing you are looking for is a tiny minority of the data.</p><p>Say 97% of transactions are clean. A program that learns nothing and calls every transaction "clean" gets 97% accuracy. That number looks good, but the program has not caught a single fraud. So <b>accuracy here measures the base rate, not the model.</b></p><p>A practical rule: when you see an accuracy figure the first thing to ask is not how good the model is but <b>what the class distribution is</b>. Accuracy cannot be read without knowing the distribution.</p>',
  'metrikler:2': '<p>Accuracy throws every error into the same bag. But there are two different errors and their costs differ greatly.</p><p><b>A false positive:</b> the model said "fraud" and it was not. An innocent customer was blocked. <b>A false negative:</b> the model said "clean" and it was fraud. You missed it.</p><p>Two measures separate these. <b>Precision</b>: of the things the model called positive, how many really were? That is, how many of your alarms are real. <b>Recall</b>: of the real positives, how many did you manage to catch?</p><p>The critical point: these two <b>work against each other</b>. Lower the threshold and you catch more (recall rises) but raise more false alarms (precision falls). You cannot raise both at once; you can only move the whole trade-off to a better place by genuinely improving the model.</p><p>Which one to prioritise is not a technical question: it depends on which error costs more.</p>',
  'metrikler:3': '<p>Precision and recall depend on a single <b>threshold</b>. Change the threshold and both change. So how good is the model itself, independently of the threshold?</p><p>The <b>ROC curve</b> answers that. It walks through every possible threshold and marks two numbers for each: the share caught and the false alarm rate. A curve appears, and that curve looks at the model\'s <b>discriminative power</b> rather than at one threshold.</p><p>The <b>AUC</b> is the area under that curve. Its intuitive meaning: pick a random positive and a random negative example, and the AUC is the probability that the model gives the positive one the higher score. 0.5 is a coin flip and 1.0 is perfect separation.</p><p>With one warning: because the AUC collapses everything into a single number it <b>hides which region you will operate in</b>. On imbalanced data the PR curve gives a more honest picture. And whether a small gap between two AUCs is real is decided by a statistical test, not by eye.</p>',
  'bolme:1': '<p>The previous lesson split the data in two: training and test. In a real project that is not enough, because there is one more job in between.</p><p>When you train a model you choose not only its parameters but also its <b>settings</b>: the polynomial degree, the learning rate, the tree depth. You have to try those settings somewhere. Try them on the test set and the test set stops being an honest measurement.</p><p>So the data is split in three and each part has exactly one job:</p><p><b>Training</b> → learn. The model extracts its parameters from this data.<br><b>Validation</b> → choose. You compare settings and models here.<br><b>Test</b> → measure. Only at the very end, once.</p><p>The rule is strict: <b>the more times you looked at the test set, the more optimistic your result is.</b> The moment you look at it and change your model, the test set quietly turns into a validation set and loses its honesty.</p>',
  'bolme:2': '<p>A single split has one weakness: <b>it depends on luck.</b> With little data, which examples land in the test set can change the result considerably. If the easy examples land there the model looks better than it is; if the hard ones do, worse.</p><p><b>k-fold cross validation</b> removes that. The data is split into k parts. On every round one part is the test and the rest is training. After k rounds every example has been tested exactly once.</p><p>The gain is not only a fairer average: instead of a single number you get <b>a distribution of k numbers</b>. So you also see how volatile the result is. Only with that distribution can you argue about whether the gap between two models is real.</p><p>One warning: random splitting is not right for every dataset. In a <b>time series</b> you end up seeing the future and predicting the past. In <b>grouped data</b> (several records of the same patient, say) if the same group lands in both training and test, the model recognises the person rather than the rule. Both are leakage.</p>',

  'sizinti:1': '<p>Data leakage is the most expensive and the quietest mistake in machine learning. The model looks wonderful and collapses in production.</p><p>Its definition is one sentence: <b>giving the model information that will not be available at prediction time.</b></p><p>Why is it hard to catch? Because its symptom is not a bad result but <b>a very good one</b>. The model has already seen the answer by an indirect route. When the accuracy comes out higher than you expected, the right reaction is suspicion rather than delight.</p><p>The three most common forms: <b>columns that are filled in after the event</b> (was the transaction refunded, was the case sent for manual review), <b>features derived from the target</b>, and <b>preprocessing fitted on all the data</b> (fitting the scaler before splitting leaks the test set\'s mean into the model).</p>',
  'sizinti:2': '<p>The only reliable test for leakage is not technical but <b>temporal</b>. Ask one question about every feature:</p><p><b>"Will this information be in my hands at the moment I make the prediction?"</b></p><p>If the answer is no it is leakage. If the answer is "I am not sure" it counts as leakage too; uncertainty does not get the benefit of the doubt.</p><p>The column name sometimes gives a clue but is never enough. The real question is <b>when that value was written</b>. A column with the same name may be filled before the prediction in one system and after it in another. You only find that out by asking the team that produces the data.</p><p>And remember the trade: when you drop a leaking feature your accuracy falls. That fall is not a loss but <b>a return to reality</b>. The cost of a model that collapses in production is far higher than those few points.</p>',

  'mat-matris:1': '<p>Learning a matrix as a table of numbers is like knowing the chess pieces without knowing the game. <b>A matrix is an operation:</b> a rule that moves every point in space to another point.</p><p>The four numbers of a 2×2 matrix say what to do to the plane: rotate, stretch, shear. Put a shape through that transformation and it moves and changes form.</p><p>The <b>determinant</b> is the one-number summary of that operation: <b>by what factor it multiplies the area.</b> That is not a metaphor but a measurable equality; divide the new area by the old one and you get the absolute value of the determinant.</p><p>Its sign carries information too: a negative determinant means the transformation flipped the shape over like a mirror.</p>',
  'mat-matris:2': '<p>Something special happens when the determinant is zero: <b>the area falls to zero.</b> The shape is crushed onto a line, or even a point.</p><p>What that means is that the transformation <b>destroyed a dimension</b>. Different inputs land on the same output. And once they have, you cannot say which one they came from, because the information has been erased irrecoverably.</p><p>The algebraic counterpart: if the determinant is zero <b>the matrix has no inverse</b>. The undo operation is undefined.</p><p>The counterpart on the data side is very concrete: if two columns are multiples of one another (say "metres" and "centimetres") the determinant of that data matrix is zero. The model cannot tell the two columns apart and the question "how much does each contribute" has no single answer. The ridge lesson on Track 1 addresses exactly this problem.</p>',
  'mat-matris:3': '<p>Multiplying two matrices means applying two operations <b>one after the other</b>. First one, then the other.</p><p>From that it follows directly: <b>the order matters.</b> Rotating and then stretching does not give the same result as stretching and then rotating. It is the same in daily life: putting your socks on before your shoes is not the same as the reverse.</p><p>So in matrix multiplication <b>AB is generally not equal to BA</b>. The commutativity you are used to with numbers does not hold here, and that is not a flaw but the nature of the operation.</p><p>The determinants, on the other hand, do multiply: det(AB) = det(A)·det(B). So the order changes the result but <b>not by what factor the area grows</b>; two stretches produce the same total growth whichever order they are applied in.</p>',
  'mat-matris:4': '<p>Now the link to AI. <b>A neural network layer is, by definition, a matrix multiplication.</b></p><p>In a layer every neuron multiplies all the inputs by its own weights and sums them. Instead of writing that out for every neuron, you put the weights in a matrix and the whole layer becomes a single multiplication.</p><p>That has two consequences. First, the mathematics of deep learning is really linear algebra. Second and more practical: all the multiplications inside a matrix product are <b>independent of each other</b>, so they can be done at the same time.</p><p>This is why GPUs made AI possible. A GPU is not fast at one operation; it does thousands of independent operations at once. And a matrix multiplication is exactly that kind of job.</p>',

  'mat-olasilik:1': '<p>The sentence "the probability of this coin landing tails is 0.5" says nothing about a single toss. It makes no promise; it states <b>a long run rate</b>.</p><p>In a small number of trials the observed rate can deviate noticeably from the true probability. As the number of trials grows the deviation shrinks, and that shrinking has a speed: the measurement error falls roughly with <b>1/√N</b>.</p><p>The practical meaning of that formula is annoying: to <b>halve</b> the error you have to <b>quadruple</b> the number of samples. To cut it to a tenth, multiply by a hundred.</p><p>This is why numbers from small test sets cannot be trusted, and why model comparisons come with confidence intervals. The same rule will be the answer to "how many examples are enough" in the evaluation lesson on Track 4.</p>',
  'mat-olasilik:2': '<p>A test being good does <b>not on its own determine</b> what its result means. This is the statistical fact that runs most against intuition.</p><p>The quality of a test is measured with two numbers. <b>Sensitivity</b>: what share of the ill it calls positive. <b>Specificity</b>: what share of the healthy it correctly calls negative. Say both are 99%, so a very good test.</p><p>Now the real question: your test came out positive, what is the probability that you are actually ill?</p><p>The answer depends not on the quality of the test but also on <b>how common the disease is</b>. If the disease is rare there are so many healthy people that the false positives coming from a small percentage of them swamp the number of genuinely ill ones. The result: most of those testing positive are actually healthy.</p><p>That calculation is in front of you in this step. As you look at the numbers, watch this: the test never changes, only the world does.</p>',
  'mat-olasilik:3': '<p>The counterpart of the same calculation in machine learning is exact; only the names change.</p><p>The test\'s <b>sensitivity</b> = the model\'s <b>recall</b>. The test\'s <b>specificity</b> = one minus the false alarm rate. And the question "it came out positive, is it really ill" is <b>precision</b>.</p><p>What follows matters: <b>precision is not a property of the model.</b> It is a joint property of the model and the world. Move the same model to an environment where the event is rarer and its precision falls, even though you changed nothing.</p><p>The practical consequence: a model\'s precision figure from one setting is not valid in another. If the base rate changed, the number changed too.</p>',
  'mat-olasilik:4': '<p>When the base rate is low the design itself changes. Intuition misleads here, because everybody tries to raise the sensitivity first.</p><p>But with a rare event the real bottleneck is not sensitivity but <b>specificity</b>. The reason is numerical: there are very many negative examples, so even a small improvement in the false alarm rate cuts the total number of alarms greatly. Raising the sensitivity, on the other hand, can only pay off over the already small number of positives.</p><p>The extreme case: even if you take the sensitivity to 100%, that is even if you miss no case at all, the precision can stay low because of the false alarms.</p><p>This is why two moves are standard on rare event problems: raise the threshold and improve the model in the direction of specificity. The numbers in this step measure the return on both routes side by side.</p>',
  'neden-simdi:1': '<p>The standard answer to "why did AI take off now?" is three words: data, compute, algorithms. It is true but empty, because it does not say how much each contributed.</p><p>This lesson makes that question <b>measurable</b>. The setup is an experiment: one problem, a fixed test set and three separate levers.</p><p><b>Data:</b> how many examples we train on. <b>Compute:</b> how many rounds we train for. <b>Algorithm:</b> which model family we use.</p><p>First a <b>baseline</b> is established: a raw model, few examples, few rounds. Every number from here on is read against that baseline. And the rule of the experiment: <b>only one thing</b> changes at a time, so it is never unclear what caused a difference.</p>',
  'neden-simdi:2': '<p>Now we pull the levers one at a time. Only one grows while the other two stay fixed.</p><p>The expectation would be that raising the data a lot should lower the error noticeably. What you see in this step does not match that expectation, and the reason matters.</p><p><b>A linear model can only ever draw a line, however much data you give it.</b> If the relationship you are after is a curve, extra data does not help you catch it; it just pins down the same wrong line more precisely. On Track 1 we will call this <b>bias</b>: the error that comes from the model\'s own constraint and does not close with data.</p><p>The same logic holds for compute. Training a converged model for more rounds gains nothing; it has already gone as far as it can.</p><p>The conclusion: <b>each lever pays off only as far as the state of the others allows.</b></p>',
  'neden-simdi:3': '<p>Now we pull all three at once. The number here is neither the sum nor the product of the individual gains; it is larger than both.</p><p>The reason: the levers <b>unlock each other</b>. A flexible model can only use its capacity with enough data, otherwise it memorises. And enough data can only be processed with enough training rounds. Without all three at once, none of them shows its potential.</p><p>That is why "which one matters most" is the wrong question. The levers are not independent; <b>the effect of one depends on the state of the others.</b></p><p>It is also why AI took off in the last decade: large datasets, GPUs and architectures like the transformer became available <b>in the same period</b>. Had one been missing, the other two could not have produced today\'s result.</p>',
  'neden-simdi:4': '<p>The most important part of an experiment is saying what it does <b>not</b> show.</p><p>This experiment was run on one problem, at a small scale, with a chosen model family. The ratios that come out of it <b>belong to this setup</b>; another problem gives other numbers. What it demonstrates is not the numbers themselves but the <b>mechanism</b>: the levers unlock each other.</p><p>A second limit: no lever pays off forever. Past a certain point neither more data nor more rounds gains anything; the noise floor has been reached. You will see that numerically in the bias-variance lesson on Track 1 and in the scaling laws on Track 3.</p><p>And a caveat: the "algorithm" lever here was an architectural change. In real life an algorithmic improvement is usually harder and does not always give such a clean gain.</p>',

  'arama-uzayi:1': '<p>There is a standard way of explaining a puzzle to a computer: writing it as <b>states</b> and <b>transitions</b>.</p><p>A <b>state</b> is the complete snapshot of the world at that moment. In the water jug puzzle a state is the pair "how many litres in jug A, how many in jug B". A <b>transition</b> is an allowed move that takes you from one state to another: fill, empty, pour from one into the other.</p><p>With that writing the problem turns into a <b>graph</b>: the nodes are states and the edges are moves. And solving the puzzle reduces to searching for a path from one node to another.</p><p>One thing to note: not all combinations of a state can actually <b>occur</b>. Some states the rules do not allow cannot be reached by any sequence of moves. So the search space is smaller than the number of combinations you would count on paper.</p>',
  'arama-uzayi:2': '<p>Once the graph is built the question is: how do we find the <b>shortest</b> path to the goal?</p><p><b>Breadth-first search</b> (BFS) guarantees it. The method: first all the states reachable from the start in one move, then those reachable in two, then three. That is, the search widens <b>layer by layer</b>.</p><p>The proof of the guarantee follows from that ordering: if you found the goal in layer d, then had a shorter path existed it would already have been seen in an earlier layer. So the first path you find is the shortest.</p><p>It has one condition: this guarantee holds <b>when every move costs the same</b>. If the moves have different costs BFS is not enough; there you need Dijkstra or A*, and you will meet them on Track 1.</p>',
  'arama-uzayi:3': '<p>One detail determines the cost of the search: <b>are you noting down the states you have seen?</b></p><p>If you do not, you reach the same state again and again by different routes and expand it every time. The search now walks a <b>tree</b> rather than a graph, and that tree grows exponentially with depth.</p><p>Keeping the seen states in a set cuts that off: the second time you see a state you skip it. The work is then bounded by the <b>number of states</b> in the space, not by how many routes lead to them.</p><p>That one extra line turns an exponential search into a finite one. And the idea is not confined to this lesson: dynamic programming, memoisation and caching are different names for the same principle. <b>Do not do the same work twice.</b></p>',
  'arama-uzayi:4': '<p>Now the limit of search algorithms. <b>No algorithm can find an answer that is not in the space.</b></p><p>In the water jug puzzle some target amounts cannot be obtained by any sequence of moves. The reason is not the search but the <b>arithmetic</b>: every move you can make amounts to adding and subtracting whole multiples of the jug capacities, so every amount you can reach has to be a multiple of the <b>greatest common divisor</b> of the two capacities.</p><p>If the target is not one of those multiples there is no solution. The search could run forever and not find it, because what it is looking for is not in the space.</p><p>The engineering lesson: when a problem cannot be solved, the first question is not "is there a better algorithm". First ask <b>whether the solution is in the search space at all</b>. That distinction saves months of wasted effort.</p>',

  'kombinatorik:1': '<p>The most misleading intuition in computer science is this: "slower" and "impossible" are taken to be on the same scale. They are not.</p><p>An algorithm\'s cost is measured by how it grows as the input grows. <b>Polynomial</b> growth (n², n³) is manageable: double the input and the work grows by a few times. <b>Exponential</b> growth (2ⁿ) is not: add <b>one</b> to the input and the work <b>doubles</b>. <b>Factorial</b> growth (n!) is worse still: add one and the work grows n times.</p><p>That difference is not one of degree but of <b>kind</b>. On small inputs all three look fast; as the input grows two stay usable and one becomes physically impossible.</p><p>The numbers in this step put all three on the same axis. You will see there that the difference is not "a bit".</p>',
  'kombinatorik:2': '<p>Now you will feel the explosion in your own browser rather than in a table.</p><p>The problem is the <b>travelling salesman</b>: find the shortest tour that visits every city once and returns to the start. The brute force solution is perfect: try every tour and pick the shortest. It guarantees the right answer.</p><p>The problem is the count. Add one city and the number of tours to try grows <b>n times</b>. So adding a few cities does not mean "it takes a bit longer", it means "it never finishes".</p><p>The reason this page stops at a certain number of cities is not a theoretical choice: add a few more and the browser really does freeze. That is how you feel the combinatorial explosion.</p>',
  'kombinatorik:3': '<p>The natural reflex: "if computers get faster the problem goes away." This step measures that reflex.</p><p>The difference is <b>where</b> the speedup goes. With polynomial growth a faster machine <b>multiplies</b> the size you can solve. With exponential growth it only <b>adds</b> a few units to it.</p><p>The reason: with exponential growth every new unit already doubles the work. However much you speed the machine up, that speedup is a fixed multiplier, and a fixed multiplier only buys a fixed number of units.</p><p>The practical consequence is clear: <b>buying hardware is not a solution to an exponential problem.</b> Waiting a few years for a faster machine comes to the same thing.</p>',
  'kombinatorik:4': '<p>Hardware cannot change the growth class. <b>An algorithm can.</b> That is what rescues you.</p><p>On the travelling salesman, brute force tries every tour. A better approach (Held-Karp) stores the intermediate results instead of recomputing them again and again: the "do not do the same work twice" idea from the previous lesson. That shrinks the exponent and noticeably grows the size you can solve.</p><p>But to be honest: <b>Held-Karp is exponential too.</b> It is a more slowly growing exponential, that is all. So it does not make the problem easy, it only pushes the wall further out.</p><p>In the real world the usual way of coping with exponential problems is this: give up on finding the exact optimum and settle for a <b>good enough</b> solution. The A* and hyperparameter search lessons on Track 1 are examples of that trade.</p>',
  'knn:1': '<p>Every model you have seen so far went through a <b>training</b> phase: it looked at the data and adjusted its parameters. k-NN does none of that.</p><p>k-NN\'s "training" amounts to putting the data into memory. No computation, no parameters, no loss function. All the work is deferred to <b>prediction time</b>: when a new question arrives it looks at all the data, finds the <b>nearest k neighbours</b> to the query point, and answers with whichever class is in the majority.</p><p>This approach is called <b>lazy learning</b>. The assumption behind it is one sentence: <b>points that are close together have the same label.</b> When that assumption holds, k-NN works surprisingly well.</p><p>The price is in the cost. Other models are expensive in training and cheap at prediction; k-NN is the reverse. Every prediction means scanning all the data, so as the data grows every question gets more expensive.</p>',
  'knn:2': '<p>k says how many neighbours to consult. And it is the model\'s <b>only setting</b>.</p><p><b>If k is small</b> the model looks at very few neighbours. The decision boundary becomes wiggly and a single mislabelled point creates an island around itself. So it is exposed to noise: this is what overfitting looks like in k-NN.</p><p><b>If k is large</b> the model averages over a wide neighbourhood. The boundary smooths out, the noise is erased, but so are the genuine details. At the extreme, when k equals the whole dataset, the model gives everyone the same answer.</p><p>So k is the k-NN name of a familiar dial: the <b>flexibility dial</b>. It does the same job as λ in ridge, the depth in a tree and C in an SVM, and the right value is chosen from the data by cross validation.</p>',

  'lojistik:1': '<p>It has "regression" in its name but its job is classification. The name comes from the first half of what it does.</p><p>The model works in three steps. <b>First:</b> exactly as in linear regression it computes a score, <code>z = w·x + b</code>. That score can be any number from minus to plus infinity. <b>Second:</b> it passes the score through a <b>sigmoid</b>, which squashes any number into the range 0 to 1, that is turns the score into a probability. <b>Third:</b> in training the loss used is <b>cross entropy</b>.</p><p>Its structure implies a constraint: because the score is linear, <b>the decision boundary is always straight</b>. On data that needs a curved boundary logistic regression falls short.</p><p>But the same constraint is the source of its strength: every feature has a coefficient and that coefficient can be read directly. You can say "when this variable rises, the probability of the outcome moves this way". In most models that sentence cannot be formed.</p>',
  'lojistik:2': '<p>Logistic regression dates from the 1950s. Why is it still used in the middle of the deep learning era?</p><p>Because <b>choosing a model is not an accuracy race.</b> In a real project there are at least four more criteria.</p><p><b>Interpretability:</b> the coefficients are readable and the decision can be justified. In fields like credit and health that is a legal requirement. <b>Calibration:</b> the probabilities it produces are close to the real rates; when it says "70%" then roughly 7 of 10 cases really are. Many flexible models do not do that. <b>Data appetite:</b> it works with few examples, because it has few parameters to learn. <b>Cost:</b> it is cheap to train and to run, and simple to maintain.</p><p>The practical consequence: on most tabular problems logistic regression is <b>the first baseline to build</b>. A more complex model earns its place only if it can be shown statistically to beat that baseline.</p>',

  'agac:1': '<p>A decision tree works by splitting the data in two over and over. At every node it asks a single question, "is this feature above this threshold?", and separates the data into two branches.</p><p>The real question is: <b>which question should it ask?</b> The tree\'s answer rests on a measurable criterion.</p><p>The number that measures how mixed a group is is called <b>impurity</b>; its most common form is <b>Gini</b>. If a group is entirely one class the Gini is zero (perfectly pure); if the classes are half and half it is at its highest (maximally mixed).</p><p>The tree tries every candidate threshold and computes: <b>the parent\'s impurity minus the weighted impurity of the children.</b> That difference is called the <b>gain</b>, and the tree picks the threshold that makes the gain largest.</p><p>One detail matters: this choice is <b>greedy</b>. The tree does not ask "will this split help me later", it just takes the best one available now. Finding the globally optimal tree is NP-hard, which is why everybody is greedy.</p>',
  'agac:2': '<p>Every split of the tree rests on a single feature and a single threshold. The geometric consequence: <b>every cut is perpendicular to an axis.</b></p><p>So a tree cannot draw a diagonal line. It can only approximate a diagonal boundary with a <b>staircase</b>: small vertical and horizontal steps.</p><p>As the depth grows the steps get finer and the approximation improves. But at no depth do you get a true diagonal; you only get a finer staircase.</p><p>That has two practical consequences. Trees are <b>insensitive to scaling</b>: change a column from metres to kilometres and the threshold scales too, leaving the tree the same. On the other hand they are <b>sensitive to rotation</b>: rotating the data changes the problem completely as far as the tree is concerned. Linear models and SVMs do not have that problem.</p>',
  'agac:3': '<p>A single tree has two fundamental weaknesses and both come from its structure.</p><p><b>Instability.</b> Because the tree is built greedily the root split is decisive; if the root changes, everything below it changes. Remove a few points from the data and retrain and a completely different tree can come out. This is called <b>high variance</b>: the model is overly sensitive to small changes in the training data.</p><p><b>The staircase artefact.</b> The decision regions have sharp corners. If the true boundary is smooth the tree can never catch it smoothly.</p><p>That weakness gave birth to the two most successful ideas in machine learning, and both rest on the same intuition: <b>do not trust one tree, use many.</b></p><p><b>Bagging</b> grows trees in parallel and independently of each other and lets them vote; it lowers variance. <b>Boosting</b> grows trees in sequence, each targeting the previous one\'s error; it lowers bias. The next two lessons are those.</p>',

  'soft-split:1': '<p>This lesson follows one question: why can decision trees not be trained with gradient descent?</p><p>The answer is hidden in the tree\'s smallest piece. Every node asks a single question: "is x greater than the threshold t?" If yes the example goes to the right branch, if no to the left. There is nothing in between; an example 0.01 to the left of the threshold goes entirely left and one 0.01 to the right goes entirely right.</p><p>Mathematically that is a <b>step function</b>. And the derivative of a step function is <b>zero everywhere</b> (and undefined at the threshold).</p><p>Recall gradient descent\'s only question: "which way should I move the parameter?" It takes the answer from the derivative. Here the answer it gets is: <i>no direction, it makes no difference.</i></p><p>This is why classical trees are built by greedy search rather than gradient descent. The consequence: trees are interpretable but <b>not end to end learnable</b>; they cannot be plugged into the same pipeline as neural networks.</p>',
  'soft-split:2': '<p>The idea that solves it amounts to a single change: <b>replace the step with a sigmoid.</b></p><p>The gate no longer gives a sharp yes/no but a <b>weight</b> between 0 and 1. Every example goes to both branches, just with different weights.</p><p>The sharpness of the sigmoid is set by the <b>temperature</b> (T) and it works like a dial. <b>As T approaches zero</b> the sigmoid turns into a step; you get the classical decision tree back, with maximum interpretability. <b>When T is large</b> the gate softens right out; the model becomes more flexible, more like a neural network.</p><p>The real gain here is not having to choose. It is not a pick between a classical tree and a neural network but <b>a continuous dial on which you can stop at any point in between</b>.</p>',
  'soft-split:3': '<p>Softening is not an aesthetic preference; it has a mathematical consequence.</p><p>A sigmoid is <b>differentiable</b> and its derivative is never exactly zero. It is largest near the threshold, where the decision is most delicate, and shrinks as you move away without ever vanishing.</p><p>What that means: the question "should I move the threshold left or right?" now has an answer. That is, <b>the tree became trainable by backpropagation.</b></p><p>And this is not only about the threshold. Which feature to look at, the leaf values, the temperature: all of it can be learned at the same time. Instead of being built greedily node by node, the whole tree is <b>optimised end to end</b>.</p>',
  'soft-split:4': '<p>Now the concrete consequence of the difference. The same tree structure, the same leaf values; the only difference is whether the gate is hard or soft.</p><p><b>In a hard tree</b> the example goes to a single leaf and the prediction is that leaf\'s value. No value in between can be produced. Crossing from one side of the threshold to the other, the prediction <b>jumps</b>.</p><p><b>In a soft tree</b> both branches flow with a weight and the prediction is the <b>weighted average</b> of the leaves. Around the threshold the transition is smooth.</p><p>This is not an academic detail. In fields like credit scoring "customers at the threshold" is a known problem: a very small difference in the input creates a large jump in the decision, and that is hard to explain to a customer. A soft gate removes exactly that jump.</p>',
  'soft-split:5': '<p>The step where you prove you understood: you will complete the code for the soft gate and run it.</p><p>The gate has to satisfy three things. <b>The output has to be a weight</b>, that is between 0 and 1; this is why a sigmoid is needed and ReLU will not do, because it is unbounded. <b>The threshold has to be subtracted</b>, not added; the expression x − t is what makes the gate turn at t. <b>The temperature has to be the divisor</b>; dividing by T sets the sharpness of the slope, while multiplying by it or dividing by x makes the gate meaningless.</p><p>It will run even if you write it wrong and you will see the result. These three lines are the core of a real library (neural-trees).</p>',
  'soft-tree:1': '<p>You saw the idea in the previous lesson. This one actually trains it and puts it side by side with a classical tree.</p><p>To see where the difference comes from, look at the gate. CART\'s gate looks at a single feature: "is x > t?" That is why its cuts are perpendicular to the axes. A soft tree\'s gate looks at <b>a weighted sum of all the features</b>: σ((w₁x + w₂y + b)/T). That is a linear expression, and a linear expression <b>can cut diagonally</b>.</p><p>So the gain does not come from "softness" alone. The gate being linear removes CART\'s staircase constraint entirely.</p><p>In this step you will see the boundaries and the parameter counts of the two models on the same data. What to watch is not only the accuracy but <b>with how many parameters</b> it was reached.</p>',
  'soft-tree:2': '<p>One end of the temperature dial is not free, and this step measures the price.</p><p>As T shrinks the model gets sharper, that is more interpretable. But at the same time it becomes untrainable. The reason is mathematical.</p><p>The derivative of a sigmoid is proportional to σ(1−σ). As T shrinks, σ <b>saturates</b> at 0 or 1; once saturated, σ(1−σ) goes to zero and <b>the gradient vanishes</b>. The 1/T factor in the formula tries to compensate, but the saturation grows faster.</p><p>So the sentence "T → 0 brings the classical tree back" has a sequel: it brings back the classical tree\'s <b>untrainability</b> too. The dial is continuous and there is a real price at both ends.</p><p>The practical fix is called <b>annealing</b>: start with a high T so the gradients flow and a roughly correct gate is found, then lower T step by step to sharpen it. The same idea is used in knowledge distillation and in methods like Gumbel-Softmax.</p>',
  'soft-tree:3': '<p>The circle closes in this step. You have two numbers and there appears to be a gap between them. The question: <b>is that gap real?</b></p><p>Recall the lesson from Track 0. These numbers came from a single training run and were measured on all the data; that is a <i>training accuracy</i>. It is not enough for a claim of superiority.</p><p>The protocol for an honest comparison: split the data with <b>5×2cv</b>, train both models on every fold with the same split, collect the 10 paired differences and apply the <b>Alpaydın 5×2cv F-test</b>. If p is small the difference is significant; if not, the correct sentence is "could not be shown".</p><p>And the real lesson here: finding a pretty visual, a model that runs and a high number is easy. What is hard is being able to show that the number is <b>real</b>. That trio, <b>build, compare, prove</b>, repeats with every model from here on.</p>',

  'orman:1': '<p>The previous lesson concluded that a single tree is interpretable but unstable. <b>Bagging</b> fixes that instability, and the idea comes from statistics.</p><p>The average of independent measurements is less volatile than a single measurement. The same holds for models: average models that make independent errors and the errors partly cancel out. That is, <b>averaging lowers variance.</b></p><p>The critical word is <b>independent</b>. Run the same algorithm on the same data a hundred times and you get a hundred identical trees, and averaging gains nothing. The trees have to be made different.</p><p>Random Forest does that with two kinds of randomness. <b>Bootstrap:</b> every tree is trained on a random sample of the data drawn with replacement. <b>Random features:</b> only a subset of the features is tried at every split.</p><p>One consequence matters: adding trees <b>does not cause overfitting</b>. The number of trees lowers variance, it does not raise capacity. The risk of overfitting comes from the <b>depth</b> of the trees, not their number.</p>',

  'boosting:1': '<p>Random Forest grew its trees in parallel and independently. Boosting does the opposite: it grows them <b>in sequence</b>, and every tree targets the error the previous ones left behind.</p><p>The loop does one thing per round. First the <b>residual</b> is computed: the truth minus the current prediction. Then <b>a new stump</b> (a very shallow tree) is fitted to that residual. Then the stump\'s output is added to the current prediction with a small multiplier. And again.</p><p>That small multiplier is the <b>learning rate</b> and it is critical. It is kept small in order to stop each stump from sitting exactly on the residual; if it does, it learns the noise too.</p><p>What is striking: <b>no stump is any good on its own.</b> Each one is just a threshold and two constants. But stack them and the error melts away fast. That is the whole of boosting.</p><p>There is a price: boosting will certainly memorise if it does not stop. This is why <b>boosting is never used without early stopping.</b></p>',
  'boosting:2': '<p>Both methods use "many trees" but they solve <b>completely different problems</b>.</p><p><b>Random Forest is parallel.</b> The trees are independent and can be trained at once. Each tree is deep, that is individually low bias and high variance. Averaging lowers <b>variance</b>. Adding a tree never hurts.</p><p><b>Boosting is sequential.</b> The trees depend on each other and must be trained in order. Each tree is shallow, that is a "weak learner" with high bias and low variance. Correcting errors on top of each other lowers <b>bias</b>. Past a point, adding a tree hurts.</p><p>In practice on tabular data the boosting family (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) usually beats Random Forest. But RF is one of the rare models that gives a reasonable result <i>with no tuning at all</i>. So the standard order is: build a baseline with RF first, then try to beat it with boosting, and test the difference statistically.</p>',

  'svm:1': '<p>In the classification lesson you found a line. But there are <b>infinitely many</b> lines separating two classes. Which one is best?</p><p>The SVM\'s answer is simple and strong: <b>the one that stays furthest from both classes.</b> That gap between the two classes is called the <b>margin</b>, and an SVM picks the line that makes it widest.</p><p>The intuition: if the margin is wide, a new point can drift a little either way without changing class. So a wide margin is safer generalisation.</p><p>The elegant part of the solution is this: only the points sitting on or inside the margin determine the boundary. They are called the <b>support vectors</b>. Delete every other point and the model does not change at all. The practical consequence: a million rows of data can produce an extremely compact model with a few hundred support vectors.</p><p><b>C</b> is the penalty given to error: a small C means a wide margin with tolerance for mistakes, a large C a narrow margin with a risk of overfitting. So C is the SVM name of the other flexibility dials you have seen on this track.</p>',
  'svm:2': '<p>The boundary an SVM draws is straight. So what if the data cannot be separated by a straight line?</p><p>The idea: carry the data into <b>a higher dimension</b> and it may become linearly separable. With one class in the middle and the other at both ends no single point can separate them; but invent a second dimension x² and a line is enough.</p><p>The problem: in real problems the dimension needed can be very high, even infinite. Carrying the data there is impossible.</p><p>The <b>kernel trick</b> comes in here. The SVM\'s optimisation needs the data points only through their <b>inner products</b>. And some functions can compute the value of the inner product in the high dimension <b>without ever going there</b>. So the high dimension is never actually built.</p><p>The price is not in the dimension but in the <b>number of examples</b>: because the kernel matrix is n×n the cost grows fast with the size of the data. This is why an SVM stops being practical on big data, and tree ensembles and deep learning take over there.</p>',

  'kumeleme:1': '<p>In every lesson so far there was a <b>right answer</b>: a y for every x. That is called <b>supervised learning</b>. In this lesson there are no labels.</p><p>All you have is points, and the question is: are there groups in the data, and if so which? That is called <b>unsupervised learning</b>, and k-means is its best known example.</p><p>The algorithm is surprisingly simple; it repeats two steps. <b>Assign:</b> attach every point to its nearest centre. <b>Update:</b> move every centre to the exact middle of the points attached to it. Then assign again, update again.</p><p>The algorithm stops when the centres stop moving. No teacher, no labels, not even a loss function in the classical sense; and yet if the data really is grouped, k-means finds those groups.</p><p>This family is heavily used in practice: customer segmentation, anomaly detection, document grouping.</p>',
  'kumeleme:2': '<p>k-means has no guarantee, and this step shows exactly that.</p><p>The algorithm starts by placing the centres somewhere. Where it places them determines the result. If the start is bad the algorithm can get stuck in a <b>local optimum</b>: by its own criterion it converged, stopped and returned a result; but the groups it found are wrong.</p><p>The crucial part: <b>the algorithm does not know that this is wrong.</b> In supervised learning the test set tells you "you got it wrong". Here there is nobody to say it, because there is no such thing as a right answer.</p><p>That is the general price of unsupervised learning, and in practice it is managed in three ways: running the algorithm <b>with many different starts</b> and taking the best (<code>n_init</code> in scikit-learn), using the <b>k-means++</b> initialisation that deliberately places centres far apart, and testing the result against domain knowledge.</p><p>Keep this in mind: <b>an algorithm saying "I converged" does not mean "I found the right answer".</b></p>',

  'boyut:1': '<p>Is a dataset with twenty columns really twenty dimensional? Usually not. If the columns are related to each other, the information is really carried in far fewer directions.</p><p><b>PCA</b> finds those directions. It asks one question: <b>in which direction does the data spread most?</b> That direction is the first principal component (PC1). Then it looks for the most spread direction among those perpendicular to PC1, and so on.</p><p>The mathematical counterpart: the components are the <b>eigenvectors of the covariance matrix</b> and each one\'s <b>eigenvalue</b> is the variance in that direction. The components are sorted from largest to smallest.</p><p>Two properties matter. The components are <b>perpendicular</b> to each other, so PCA rotates the data; the new axes still form a perpendicular coordinate system. And the components are <b>mixtures</b> of the original features, which is why they are considered "uninterpretable": they buy speed and noise reduction and cost interpretability.</p>',
  'boyut:2': '<p>The answer to "how many dimensions should I go down to?" is hidden in the eigenvalues: the share of variance carried by each component is known.</p><p>Three criteria are used in practice. <b>A variance threshold:</b> take as many components as it takes to keep a given share (say 95%) of the total variance. <b>The elbow:</b> pick the point where the eigenvalue curve bends and starts to flatten. <b>Downstream performance:</b> see which number of components the actual model works best with.</p><p>There are two critical warnings. <b>PCA is unsupervised:</b> it never looks at the label. It can easily discard a direction with small variance that is critical for separating the classes. The supervised alternative is LDA, and you will meet it later.</p><p><b>PCA is fitted on the training set only.</b> Fit it on all the data and split afterwards and the test set\'s information leaks into the model; that is exactly the trap from the leakage lesson. The fix is to use a <code>Pipeline</code>.</p><p>And because PCA looks at variance it is meaningless without scaling: a column with large units captures all the components on its own.</p>',
  'boyut:3': '<p>t-SNE and UMAP are often put in the same box as PCA but they do <b>a completely different job</b>.</p><p>PCA is <b>linear</b>: it rotates the data and cuts. That makes it fast, deterministic and invertible; you can unpack the data you compressed.</p><p>The aim of t-SNE and UMAP is not to preserve variance but to preserve <b>neighbourhood relations</b>: points close together in high dimensions should stay close in two. The price is heavy: they are not deterministic (a different picture on every run), not invertible, and most importantly <b>the distances between clusters and the sizes of clusters are meaningless</b>.</p><p>The most common mistake is using that output as model input. These are <b>visualisation tools</b>, not feature extractors.</p><p>The right usage: inspecting an embedding space by eye, checking whether clusters really separate, hunting for label errors. And the only reliable thing you can read off a UMAP plot is this: <b>does it separate or not.</b></p>',
  'regresyon:1': '<p>On Track 0 we found the line with gradient descent: step by step, over thousands of repetitions. There was actually no need for that at all.</p><p>The squared error is a <b>smooth, single-bottomed</b> function of the parameters. At the minimum of such a function the derivative is zero. Set the derivative to zero and solve, and you get the answer directly; no search needed.</p><p>The result is called the <b>normal equation</b>: <code>w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</code>. One step, no setting like a learning rate, and it gives the exact solution.</p><p>So why is gradient descent still used? Because the XᵀX matrix in the formula is p×p (p = the number of features) and <b>inverting it grows with p³</b>. At a few thousand features that computation gets heavy; at a million it is impossible. Gradient descent, by contrast, scans the data once per step and never inverts a matrix.</p>',
  'regresyon:2': '<p>The closed form always exists but is not always <b>meaningful</b>.</p><p>As two features approach being copies of one another, the XᵀX matrix starts to become <b>singular</b>: its determinant falls to zero and inverting it turns into a division by zero.</p><p>The number that measures how bad this is is called the <b>condition number</b>. Its meaning: by what factor a small change in the input grows in the output. If the condition number is large the solution is numerically unreliable.</p><p>The practical counterpart: two datasets drawn from the same process give very different coefficients. The model is unstable. This is exactly the numerical meaning of the sentence "correlated features make unpenalised regression unstable" from the ridge lesson.</p>',
  'regresyon:3': '<p>The fix is surprisingly simple: add a small number to the diagonal of the matrix. <code>w = (XᵀX + λI)⁻¹Xᵀy</code>.</p><p>That addition stops the matrix being singular and lowers the condition number; the solution becomes numerically stable.</p><p>The nice part: this formula <b>is ridge regression itself</b>. So ridge does two jobs at once. Statistically it lowers the variance, numerically it produces an invertible matrix. Two points of view, one formula.</p><p>One consequence: ridge works <b>even when there are more features than examples</b>. In that case XᵀX is certainly singular, but XᵀX + λI is not.</p><p>A practical note: real libraries never actually invert the matrix when computing this; they use a QR or SVD decomposition, because those are more stable on ill-conditioned matrices.</p>',
  'regresyon:4': '<p>There are two solution routes and the choice depends on the problem.</p><p><b>The normal equation:</b> one step, no tuning, an exact solution. But building XᵀX and inverting it gets expensive fast with the number of features. With few features it is the perfect choice.</p><p><b>Gradient descent:</b> every step scales linearly with the number of examples and features, it does not have to hold all the data in memory (it works with mini batches), and on sparse data it only visits the non-zero entries. It also works unchanged on non-linear models. The price is choosing a learning rate and watching convergence.</p><p>The threshold is roughly a few thousand features. And correlated features cause trouble on both routes: as slow convergence in gradient descent and as a bad condition number in the normal equation. The cure for both is the same: a penalty term.</p>',

  'ridge:1': '<p>In real data the columns are not independent of each other. "Monthly income" and "annual income", "floor area" and "number of rooms" sit next to each other in the same table.</p><p>If two columns carry almost the same information, unpenalised regression faces a dilemma: it cannot decide which one to give the weight to. Giving one 4 and the other 0 produces almost the same prediction as giving each 2. The model chooses <b>arbitrarily</b> between them.</p><p>The consequence: change the data a little and the coefficients swing completely. The model may be good at prediction but <b>its coefficients are meaningless</b>; its answer to "which variable matters how much" is unreliable.</p><p>And as the coefficients grow the model also becomes more sensitive to noise. The cure for both problems is the same, and that is what this lesson is about.</p>',
  'ridge:2': '<p>Ridge adds a single term to the objective of least squares:</p><p style="text-align:center"><b>RSS + λ · (the sum of the squared coefficients)</b></p><p>The model no longer says only "make the error small", it says "make the error small <b>but do not grow the coefficients either</b>". λ is the bargaining power between those two wishes.</p><p>To see what happens with correlated columns, the algebra is enough. Load the same total onto a single coefficient and its square is large; split it in two and the sum of the squares <b>halves</b>. Because the penalty sums squares, it <b>rewards sharing</b>.</p><p>The result: ridge splits the weight between correlated features and the coefficients become stable. It zeroes none of them and shrinks all of them.</p>',
  'ridge:3': '<p>This step shows one of the most counter-intuitive ideas in the course: <b>making a model deliberately worse in order to make it better.</b></p><p>As λ grows the model fits the training data worse; the training error rises. And yet the test error falls.</p><p>The reason is the bias-variance trade-off. The penalty adds a little <b>bias</b> to the model (it can no longer seek the best fit) but in return erases much more <b>variance</b> (the coefficients become less sensitive to changes in the data). The total error falls.</p><p>If λ grows too far it turns around: the penalty crushes the real signal too, the model pulls everything towards zero and the error rises again. So there is a <b>sweet spot</b>, and finding it is what this whole family is about.</p>',
  'ridge:4': '<p>How will you find the right λ? The answer in this step is short but requires discipline.</p><p>First eliminate the wrong routes. <b>You cannot look at the training error</b>, because it is always smallest at λ=0; that criterion always takes you to the unpenalised model and erases the whole benefit of ridge.</p><p><b>You cannot look at the test set either</b>; the moment you do, it stops being an honest measurement.</p><p>One legitimate route remains: <b>cross validation</b>. Split the training data into folds, measure the error on the unseen folds for every λ, and pick the best. So λ is not <i>learned</i> from the data, it is <i>searched for</i> with the data.</p><p>There is one more condition, and skipping it is a common mistake: because the penalty is applied at the same magnitude to all the coefficients, the features must be <b>scaled first</b>. A feature measured in metres and one measured in kilometres cannot take the same penalty.</p>',

  'lasso:1': '<p>Ridge shrank the coefficients but killed none of them. Lasso changes that with a single modification: it uses <b>the absolute value instead of the square</b> in the penalty.</p><p>That small looking difference changes the behaviour fundamentally, and the reason is hidden in the <b>derivative</b>.</p><p>The derivative of β² at zero is <b>zero</b>. So as a coefficient approaches zero the penalty\'s pushing force weakens; this is why ridge takes coefficients close to zero but never quite there.</p><p>The derivative of |β| at zero is <b>constant</b>. The penalty pushes with the same force right to the last moment and slams the coefficient to exactly zero.</p><p>The result: lasso produces <b>sparse</b> solutions. Some coefficients become exactly zero and those features leave the model.</p>',
  'lasso:2': '<p>Making a coefficient exactly zero is not just a mathematical detail; in practice it means <b>feature selection</b>.</p><p>A feature whose coefficient is zero contributes nothing to the model\'s output, that is it has been dropped. So lasso does two jobs at once: it regularises and it tells you which variables are needed.</p><p>The data in this step was generated deliberately, so <b>the right answer is known</b>: which features are genuine and which are noise is fixed in advance. That lets us compare lasso\'s selection against the truth rather than judging it by eye. You can only measure a method honestly in a setup where you know the right answer.</p><p>Its behaviour on correlated columns also differs from ridge: where ridge <b>shares</b> the weight between the two, lasso <b>picks</b> one and discards the other.</p>',
  'lasso:3': '<p>Now the real question: which is better?</p><p>The answer depends on the criterion, and that is one of the recurring themes of this course. If you want only <b>predictive power</b>, on correlated data ridge is usually ahead, because it shares the information rather than throwing it away.</p><p>But lasso gives you something else in return: <b>a short, defensible model.</b> A model that keeps twelve of two hundred features is far easier to defend in front of a regulator than a list of two hundred items.</p><p>So the choice is not technical but contextual: are you saying "give me the best prediction" or asking "which variable matters"?</p><p>There is also a method that combines the two: <b>elastic net</b> uses both penalties. It selects a correlated group together and discards the noise as well.</p>',

  'norm-l1l2:1': '<p>This lesson shows <b>why</b> the previous two behaved as they did. The answer is not in the algebra but in the <b>geometry</b>.</p><p>First the point of view has to change. Instead of "minimise RSS + λ·penalty" you can equally say: <b>minimise RSS, subject to the penalty not exceeding a certain budget.</b> Those two statements are two faces of the same problem; every value of λ has a corresponding budget.</p><p>Written as a budget, <b>a region</b> appears: the area the coefficients have to stay inside. And the shape of that region depends on the penalty. <b>The L2 budget is a circle</b> (a fixed sum of squares) and <b>the L1 budget is a diamond</b> (a fixed sum of absolute values).</p><p>The solution is here: the constant error curves (ellipses) widen around the unpenalised solution, and <b>the point where they first touch the budget region</b> is the solution you are after.</p>',
  'norm-l1l2:2': '<p>Now the whole matter comes down to one observation: <b>a diamond has corners and a circle does not.</b></p><p>The corners of the diamond sit on the axes. Being on an axis means <b>the other coefficient is exactly zero</b>.</p><p>As an ellipse shrinks towards the diamond it most probably hits a <b>corner</b> rather than an edge, because a corner is pointed and much easier for the ellipse to touch. That is precisely why lasso produces exact zeros.</p><p>A circle has no corner. Wherever the ellipse touches it, that point is almost never exactly on an axis. This is why ridge\'s coefficients shrink but never vanish.</p><p>And the difference grows with dimension: in high dimensions the number of corners of the L1 budget rises rapidly and every one of them is on an axis.</p>',
  'norm-l1l2:3': '<p>Now we can turn the choice into a rule.</p><p><b>L2 (ridge):</b> shrinks all the coefficients and discards none. Shares the weight across correlated features. It has a closed form, is fast, and works even when there are more features than examples.</p><p><b>L1 (lasso):</b> zeroes coefficients out and selects features. Picks one member of a correlated group and discards the rest. It has no closed form; it needs an algorithm such as coordinate descent.</p><p>The decision rule is one sentence: <b>L1 if you expect sparsity, L2 if you do not.</b></p><p>"Expecting sparsity" means this: there are many features but only a few of them really matter. Genetic data is the classic example: tens of thousands of genes, a few hundred patients, and a handful of genes thought to be related to the disease. If all the features contribute a little and your only concern is prediction, L2 suits better.</p>',

  'yanlilik:1': '<p>This lesson splits a model\'s error into two separate parts. The distinction looks abstract but in practice it directly answers the question "what should I do".</p><p>Until now you always had a single training set. But that set was a random sample; had different data arrived on another day you would have a different table.</p><p>In this step <b>many training sets</b> are drawn from the same process and a separate model is fitted to each. Two questions become askable:</p><p><b>Bias:</b> how far is the <b>average</b> of those models from the truth? That is, is the model family flexible enough to do this job?<br><b>Variance:</b> how different are the models <b>from each other</b>? That is, how much does the result depend on which data you happened to get?</p><p>They are different questions and their treatments differ too.</p>',
  'yanlilik:2': '<p>When you raise the model complexity the two numbers move in <b>opposite directions</b>, and that is not a preference but a mathematical necessity.</p><p>As flexibility grows the model family widens and becomes able to imitate the true relationship: <b>the bias falls</b>.</p><p>But a flexible model also fits the noise of the particular examples it got. Given another sample it would draw a completely different curve: <b>the variance rises</b>.</p><p>You cannot shrink both at once. All you can do is find the point where the <b>sum</b> is smallest. That is the mechanism underneath the U curve you saw on Track 0; there you saw the curve, here you see the two components that make it.</p>',
  'yanlilik:3': '<p>The expected test error consists of exactly three parts:</p><p style="text-align:center"><b>error = bias² + variance + noise</b></p><p>The first two are under your control. You can move both by changing the model family, adding a penalty or collecting data.</p><p>The third is not. <b>Noise</b> is the randomness that comes from the data itself and that no model can explain: measurement error, unrecorded variables, genuine chance. It forms a <b>floor</b>: whatever model you build, your error cannot go below it.</p><p>Its practical value is large. It is where the answer to "can I make my model better" comes from on a project: if your error is already near the noise floor, what you need is not a better model but <b>better measurement</b> or <b>more data</b>. Time spent on the model will be wasted.</p>',
  'yanlilik:4': '<p>We could do the decomposition above because we knew the true function and could draw as much data as we liked from the same process. <b>In real life you have neither.</b></p><p>But you can diagnose, because the two illnesses have different symptoms.</p><p><b>High bias:</b> the training error and the test error are both high and <b>close to each other</b>. The model cannot even learn the data. Treatment: a more flexible model, better features.</p><p><b>High variance:</b> the training error is low and the test error high. There is a <b>gap</b> between them. Treatment: more data, a penalty term, a simpler model.</p><p>So <b>the gap between the training and the test error is the measurable trace of variance.</b> The ridge and lasso lessons were exactly the cure for that second illness: they add a little bias and erase a lot of variance.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:1': '<p>In the k-NN lesson we relied on an assumption: <b>points that are close together have the same label.</b> This lesson shows what happens to that assumption in high dimensions.</p><p>Our intuition developed in two and three dimensions. There the difference between "near" and "far" is clear. As the dimension grows that difference is <b>erased</b>.</p><p>The reason: every new dimension adds its own contribution to the distance between two points. As the number of dimensions grows those contributions accumulate and converge to an average; in the end all the points start to look <b>roughly equally</b> far apart.</p><p>For k-NN the implication is severe: if everybody is at the same distance, "the nearest k neighbours" means almost <b>k random points</b>. The notion of nearness loses its meaning and the model loses the assumption it was built on.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:2': '<p>Let us see the same problem from a second angle: how "local" is a neighbourhood?</p><p>k-NN, kernel smoothing and the deep branches of a decision tree all rest on the same logic: <b>look at the nearby points.</b> So how wide does a region have to be to cover a small share of the data, say a tenth?</p><p>In one dimension the answer is intuitive: a tenth of the axis. But as the dimension grows the share the region must cover on every axis <b>grows fast</b>, because volume grows exponentially with dimension and a small box covers nothing in a huge space.</p><p>In high dimensions "10% of the data" means scanning almost the entire space. So <b>nothing called locality is left</b>. Local methods stop being local and their bias grows.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:3': '<p>The third angle is geometry\'s most counter-intuitive result: <b>in high dimensions the volume escapes to the surface.</b></p><p>Go a little inwards from every edge of a cube and define an "inner core". In two dimensions the core holds most of the volume. As the dimension grows the core\'s share <b>erodes exponentially</b>, because it is multiplied by the same ratio in every dimension.</p><p>The result: in a high dimensional dataset almost every point is at an extreme value on at least one axis. So <b>in practice there is no such thing as "an average example".</b></p><p>The effect on the model is direct: every new example most probably falls outside the training data. The model constantly has to <b>extrapolate rather than interpolate</b>, and extrapolation is always riskier.</p>',
  'boyut-laneti:4': '<p>Everything you have read so far is true, and yet machine learning works with data of thousands of dimensions. How?</p><p>Because <b>real data is not spread uniformly through the space.</b> Images of handwritten digits with 784 pixels are not everywhere in a 784 dimensional space; almost none of the random pixel heaps look like a digit. Real images gather on a much lower dimensional surface. That is called the <b>manifold assumption</b>.</p><p>So the curse is <b>a curse of the true dimension, not the measured one.</b> In this experiment the points were spread uniformly on purpose, so you are seeing the curse in its heaviest form.</p><p>The practical job follows: find that low dimensional surface. PCA, autoencoders, embeddings, feature selection with lasso; they all do the same job.</p><p>And do not forget: <b>collecting more data is no remedy here.</b> The number of examples needed to keep the same density grows exponentially with the dimension; no budget can keep up.</p>',

  'hiper-arama:1': '<p>How do you choose hyperparameters? The first method that comes to mind is a <b>grid search</b>: pick a few values for each setting and try every combination.</p><p>It sounds systematic but there is a hidden waste in it, and to see it you need to know what real hyperparameter surfaces look like.</p><p>Bergstra and Bengio showed in 2012 that on these surfaces the settings are <b>not equally important</b>. Usually one or two settings determine the result and the effect of the rest is negligible. In neural networks the learning rate is like that.</p><p>The grid\'s problem is exactly here: on a k×k grid <b>you train k² models but only try k different values of the setting that matters.</b> The remaining trials are the same important value tried again and again with different values of an unimportant setting.</p>',
  'hiper-arama:2': '<p>A random search trains the same number of models but does not trap the points in a grid.</p><p>The consequence is direct: <b>make n trials and you try n different values of the setting that matters.</b> On a grid that number is only √n.</p><p>The core of the idea: <b>you do not know in advance which setting matters.</b> A grid has to allocate equal resolution to every setting despite not knowing. A random search never has to make that call; whichever setting turns out to matter, it has tried n different values of it.</p><p>This is why, on the same compute budget, a random search typically gives a better result. The conclusion runs against intuition but the reason is geometry rather than luck.</p>',
  'hiper-arama:3': '<p>The grid\'s second problem is more insidious: <b>raising the budget does not guarantee a better result.</b></p><p>The reason: the new grid may not contain the good points of the old one. A denser grid tries different values and none of them may land in the good region. So a grid\'s performance depends not on the budget but on <b>the luck of alignment</b>.</p><p>A random search does not depend on that kind of luck. Every new trial is an independent sample; so as the budget grows its curve rises smoothly and never goes backwards.</p><p>That is exactly Bergstra and Bengio\'s practical advice, and it still holds: <b>random instead of grid.</b></p>',
  'hiper-arama:4': '<p>A random search solves <i>where</i> to look but still has one waste: <b>it gives every trial equal resources.</b></p><p>Yet a bad configuration usually reveals itself as bad very early. Training it to the end is compute thrown away.</p><p><b>Successive halving</b> fixes that: train all the candidates on a small budget, keep the best half, train the rest for longer, eliminate again. The total budget stays the same while the resources shift to the good candidates. <b>Hyperband</b> generalises the idea by also searching for the answer to "how early should I eliminate".</p><p><b>Bayesian optimisation</b> is a third level: it builds a surface model from the points tried and puts the next trial where it is most informative. That is, it uses what it has learned.</p><p>All three share the same condition: the choice must be made by <b>cross validation</b>, and the test set looked at only at the very end.</p>',

  'softmax:1': '<p>The last layer of a classification network produces one number per class. They are called <b>logits</b> or raw scores and they are not probabilities: they can be negative, exceed 1, and sum to anything.</p><p>For probabilities we need two conditions: every value between 0 and 1 and a total of exactly 1. <b>Softmax</b> does that by exponentiating every score and dividing by their sum.</p><p>Exponentiating serves two purposes. It turns negative numbers positive, and it <b>magnifies the differences</b>: a difference of 1 unit between scores turns into a ratio of about 2.7 in probability.</p><p>One important property: softmax <b>does not disturb the ordering</b>. The largest score gets the largest probability. That is where the name comes from; it is a "soft maximum", picking the largest while leaving a share to the others.</p>',
  'softmax:2': '<p>The standard loss for classification is <b>cross entropy</b>, and it looks at something surprisingly narrow: <b>only the probability you gave the correct class.</b></p><p>How you split the probability among the other classes does not matter at all. The loss is computed from one number: −log(the probability of the correct class).</p><p>The behaviour of the logarithm is decisive here. If you gave the correct class a high probability the loss is near zero. As the probability shrinks the loss grows fast and <b>goes to infinity</b> as it approaches zero.</p><p>So cross entropy <b>punishes being confident and wrong mercilessly.</b> That is not a coincidence but comes from information theory: −log p measures "how surprised am I when this event happens". If a model gives something a very low probability and it happens, the model was very surprised and it pays for it.</p>',
  'softmax:3': '<p>Why not use squared error (MSE)? At first sight it looks reasonable: the square of the difference between prediction and truth.</p><p>The answer is hidden not in the <b>size</b> of the loss but in its <b>derivative</b>. What determines learning is not the loss itself but its gradient.</p><p>When softmax and cross entropy are combined the gradient comes out remarkably clean: <b>prediction minus truth</b>. The more wrong the model is the larger the gradient, so learning is strongest exactly where it is needed most.</p><p>When softmax and MSE are combined the gradient is multiplied by the derivative of softmax. That derivative <b>goes to zero</b> when the model is confident (when the probability approaches 0 or 1). The result is perverse: when the model is confident and wrong the gradient is nearly zero, so it <b>learns least at the moment it is most wrong.</b></p><p>This is why classification uses cross entropy. The right question is not "which loss is larger" but <b>"which loss teaches better"</b>.</p>',
  'softmax:4': '<p>One more setting can be added to softmax: dividing the scores by a <b>temperature</b> (T) first.</p><p>Its effect: as T shrinks the differences grow and the distribution piles onto the highest score; the model looks very confident. As T grows the differences erode and the distribution flattens; the model looks hesitant.</p><p>The critical point: <b>the ordering never changes.</b> Temperature changes not what the model says but <b>how confident it looks</b>.</p><p>That setting has two well known uses. In language models the temperature is used like a <b>creativity dial</b>: a low T almost always picks the same word, a high T gives irrelevant tokens a chance too. And in <b>knowledge distillation</b> T is raised to get the "soft" answers of the teacher model, because a soft distribution carries information about the similarity between classes.</p>',

  'dagilim-kaymasi:1': '<p>This lesson deals with the most common way models collapse in production, and the setup has to be read carefully.</p><p>The true rule is a <b>curve</b>. But the training data is gathered in only a narrow part of that curve. In that narrow region the curve looks almost straight, so a linear model does the job perfectly well.</p><p>The model is not wrong. It is just <b>right for the world it has seen.</b></p><p>The hidden assumption: the data arriving in production will come from the same place. That assumption has a name (the i.i.d. assumption) and it is machine learning\'s quietest and most frequently broken one. It is written nowhere, it gives no warning, and it sits under every model.</p>',
  'dagilim-kaymasi:2': '<p>Now the production data shifts: the incoming examples move outside the training region, towards where the curve bends.</p><p>The linear boundary cannot follow the truth there and the accuracy collapses. What to note: <b>not a single parameter of the model changed.</b> Same code, same server, same model file. The only thing that changed is who turned up.</p><p>So what broke is not the model but <b>the model\'s assumption about the world</b>.</p><p>This situation is called <b>covariate shift</b>: the distribution of the inputs changed but the true relationship between input and label stayed the same.</p><p>In real life its counterpart is very concrete: a campaign brings in a new customer base, a sensor ages, a competitor changes its prices, a pandemic changes habits. The model stays the same and the world moves.</p>',
  'dagilim-kaymasi:3': '<p>So how will you notice this in production? The answer is harder than you would think, because <b>you cannot measure accuracy in production.</b></p><p>Accuracy needs the true label. And the label either arrives far too late (you learn months later whether a loan was repaid), never arrives (you can never know why a user did not click), or is expensive (expert labelling).</p><p>But there is a signal that needs no labels: <b>the distribution of the incoming inputs.</b> Feature means, standard deviations, category distributions, missing value rates. All of these are computed instantly and for free.</p><p>And the critical point: input shift raises the alarm <b>before</b> the accuracy collapses. This is why serious monitoring systems watch the input first; accuracy is also computed retrospectively as labels arrive, but a system that relies on it alone is blind.</p>',
  'dagilim-kaymasi:4': '<p>Shift is not one thing; there are three kinds and <b>their treatments differ</b>. So the diagnosis comes first.</p><p><b>Covariate shift:</b> the distribution of the inputs P(x) changed but P(y|x) is the same. That is what you saw in this lesson. Treatment: collect data from the new region or reweight the examples.</p><p><b>Label shift:</b> the proportion of the classes P(y) changed; the fraud rate rose, say. Treatment: update the threshold and the class weights. Most of the time there is no need to retrain the model.</p><p><b>Concept shift:</b> the relationship itself P(y|x) changed. The same input now gives a different result. Treatment: retrain with new labelled data. There is no other remedy.</p><p>In adversarial environments (spam, fraud, security) concept shift is the <b>rule</b> rather than the exception, because on the other side there is somebody trying to beat the model. There retraining is not maintenance but part of the system.</p>',

  'ozellik-onemi:1': '<p>"Which variable is the model looking at?" is a question both customers and regulators ask. The answer is not as easy as it seems.</p><p>The first method that comes to mind is to look at the coefficients: a large coefficient means an important feature, right?</p><p>No. There are three separate traps. <b>One:</b> a coefficient changes with <b>scale</b>; change the same feature from metres to kilometres and the coefficient grows a thousandfold while the importance has not changed. <b>Two:</b> in non-linear models there is no such thing as a coefficient. <b>Three:</b> and the most insidious, a coefficient <b>depends on the model</b>.</p><p>You will see the third in this step: two models fitted to the same data contradict each other about which variable matters. Neither is lying. Because <b>a coefficient is a property of the model, not of the data.</b></p>',
  'ozellik-onemi:2': '<p>There is a model-independent measure that gets past the coefficient\'s problems: <b>permutation importance</b>.</p><p>The idea is simple and elegant: <b>shuffle</b> a feature\'s values across the rows, then look at how much the test error degrades. Shuffling destroys the information in that column without removing the column. If the error degrades a lot, the model was leaning on that feature.</p><p>It has two advantages over the coefficient: it is unaffected by scale and it works with every kind of model, in a tree as well as in a neural network.</p><p>But what you will see in this step: <b>permutation importance depends on the model too.</b> The same feature gets very different importance in different models.</p><p>We changed the measure and the problem did not go away, because what we are measuring is still <b>what the model leans on</b>. "Which variable really determines the data" is a completely different question, and permutation importance does not answer it.</p>',
  'ozellik-onemi:3': '<p>With correlated features permutation importance has a particular trap.</p><p>If two columns carry almost the same information and you shuffle one, the model turns to the other and takes the information from there. The error barely degrades. The same happens in the other direction. The result: <b>each hides behind the other</b> and measuring them one at a time makes both look less important than they are.</p><p>The way to see it is to shuffle them <b>together</b>. Shuffled together, the degradation comes out noticeably larger than the sum of the individual degradations; the difference is exactly the amount of "hiding".</p><p>The practical rule follows: <b>shuffle correlated features as a group.</b> The question "how important is the income group" is both more meaningful and more stable than "how important is monthly income".</p>',
  'ozellik-onemi:4': '<p>The most important sentence of this lesson is here, and it is the rule most often violated in practice.</p><p><b>Feature importance answers "what is the model leaning on". It does not answer "what would I have to change in reality for the outcome to change".</b></p><p>The second is a <b>causal</b> question, and causality cannot be extracted from data alone. A feature may be the cause of the outcome or merely a <b>symptom</b> of it. Both get high importance in a model, and the importance measure cannot tell them apart.</p><p>The classic example: a hospital model assigning high importance to "how many visits were received in the patient\'s room". That does not mean increasing visits will make the patient better; the number of visits is most probably a symptom of the severity of the illness.</p><p>One more technical warning: permutation produces data points that never occur in reality. With correlated columns that means questioning the model in a region it was never trained on, and its predictions there are unreliable.</p><p>Three practical rules: say which model you computed the measure with, measure correlated features as a group, and run a separate study if you are going to make a causal claim.</p>',
  'fisher-lda:1': '<p>Dimensionality reduction is essentially a problem of <b>choosing a direction</b>.</p><p>Reducing two dimensional data to one means picking a line and <b>projecting</b> every point onto it. Every point goes from two numbers to one: its position along that line.</p><p>But which line? There are infinitely many options and the choice changes the result completely. A good direction separates the two classes; a bad one stacks them on top of each other and the information is lost irrecoverably.</p><p>The question of this lesson: <b>on what basis do we choose the direction?</b> There are two different answers and both look reasonable. Understanding the difference means understanding the basic distinction between supervised and unsupervised methods.</p>',
  'fisher-lda:2': '<p>The first answer is PCA\'s, and it asks one thing: <b>in which direction does the data spread most?</b></p><p>That is a reasonable criterion, because the most spread direction preserves the most information. PCA is unsupervised; it <b>never looks</b> at the labels.</p><p>The problem is exactly there. A data cloud can be long for two reasons: because the classes are far apart, or because each class is very spread out <b>within itself</b>. PCA cannot tell those apart, because telling them apart would require looking at the label.</p><p>What you see in this step is the second case: the length of the cloud comes not from the difference between the classes but from the noise within them. PCA finds the most spread direction and in that direction the two classes overlap completely.</p><p>The lesson: <b>the direction of most spread is not the direction that separates best.</b></p>',
  'fisher-lda:3': '<p>Fisher asks a different question and brings the labels into play: <b>how far apart are the class means and how narrow is the spread within each class?</b></p><p>The measure is a ratio: <b>J = (the squared difference of the means) / (the sum of the within-class spreads)</b>.</p><p>What is in the numerator and denominator matters. We want the numerator large: the class centres far apart. We want the denominator small: each class a narrow cloud within itself. So the winning direction is the one where "the means are apart but the clouds are narrow".</p><p>That is fundamentally different from PCA\'s measure. PCA looks at the <b>total</b> spread; Fisher divides the <b>between-class</b> spread by the <b>within-class</b> spread. The result: Fisher can pick a direction very different from PCA\'s, even nearly perpendicular to it.</p><p>It also has a closed form: w ∝ S<sub>W</sub><sup>−1</sup>(m₁ − m₂), the inverse of the within-class scatter matrix times the difference of the means.</p>',
  'fisher-lda:4': '<p>The two are not rivals; they answer different questions.</p><p><b>PCA is unsupervised.</b> It needs no labels. It suits compressing data, reducing noise, visualisation, or situations where there are no labels at all.</p><p><b>LDA is supervised.</b> It wants labels and targets discriminability directly. It is usually better for dimensionality reduction before classification.</p><p>LDA has two practical limits. First: it can produce at most <b>the number of classes minus one</b> dimensions; with two classes you get only a single direction. Second: in very high dimensions the within-class scatter matrix becomes singular and cannot be inverted.</p><p>The standard fix for both is the same and widely used in practice: <b>go down to a reasonable dimension with PCA first, then apply LDA.</b></p>',

  'uretici-ayirici:1': '<p>There are two fundamentally different ways of looking at the same classification problem, and the difference is in <b>which question the model asks</b>.</p><p>The <b>discriminative approach</b> asks one thing: where is the boundary that separates the classes? It learns P(class | data) directly and takes no interest in where the data came from. Logistic regression is like that.</p><p>The <b>generative approach</b> asks a bigger question: <b>how does each class produce its data?</b> That is, P(data | class) and P(class) are learned and then inverted with Bayes\' rule to classify. Naive Bayes is like that.</p><p>At first sight the generative approach looks like unnecessary work: why learn the whole distribution when finding the boundary would do? This lesson\'s answer: with little data that "unnecessary work" turns into an <b>advantage</b>.</p>',
  'uretici-ayirici:2': '<p>As the amount of data grows the winner changes, and that is a general result rather than a coincidence.</p><p>With little data the <b>generative model leads</b>. The reason: it has strong assumptions, so it has fewer parameters to learn and draws conclusions quickly from few examples.</p><p>As data grows the <b>discriminative model overtakes</b>. Its assumptions are weaker; at the start that is a disadvantage, but as data becomes plentiful it can go lower because it is not limited by a wrong assumption.</p><p>Ng and Jordan showed this as a general result in 2001: <b>the generative model converges faster but converges to a higher error level; the discriminative model starts slowly but goes lower.</b></p><p>So the two curves cross. Where the crossing is depends on the problem, but that it exists is general.</p>',
  'uretici-ayirici:3': '<p>Naive Bayes plateauing past a point is a concrete example of the bias-variance lesson.</p><p>The "naive" in its name comes from one very strong assumption: <b>given a class, the features are independent of each other.</b></p><p>That assumption is almost never true in real data; columns are related. What happens when it is false? The model can never see its own error, because by construction it cannot represent that relationship.</p><p>So adding data does not fix the error; it only <b>teaches the wrong model more precisely</b>. The error sticks at a floor and stays there.</p><p>That is exactly <b>bias</b>: the error that comes from the model\'s own constraint and does not close with data. Naive Bayes is low variance and high bias; logistic regression is high variance and low bias.</p>',
  'uretici-ayirici:4': '<p>The choice is not made on accuracy alone, because a generative model gives you things beyond accuracy.</p><p><b>It can generate new data.</b> Because P(data | class) is modelled you can sample from that distribution. A discriminative model cannot; all it has is a boundary.</p><p><b>It can cope with missing values.</b> If a feature is absent a generative model marginalises it out and carries on.</p><p><b>It notices outliers.</b> If the probability of an incoming example is low it can say "this does not look like the world I have seen". That connects directly to the monitoring problem from the distribution shift lesson.</p><p><b>It can be trained per class.</b> When a new class is added you build only that class\'s model and leave the others alone. In a discriminative model the whole boundary has to be retrained.</p><p>The decision rule: generative if there is little data, a frequently changing class list, outlier detection or missing features; discriminative if there is plenty of data and the sole aim is the highest accuracy.</p>',

  'spline:1': '<p>The best known way of making a curve flexible is to raise the polynomial degree. But a polynomial has a property that turns into a flaw: <b>a polynomial is a global model.</b></p><p>Global means this: change one coefficient and the <b>whole</b> curve changes. A bend at the right end of the curve also affects the behaviour at the left end.</p><p>The practical consequence is annoying. You raise the degree to catch a sharp peak in the middle of the data; as the degree rises the curve starts making unwanted oscillations at the <b>edges</b>. So fitting one region breaks another.</p><p>This is a classic problem shown by Runge in 1901: with equally spaced points the oscillation at the edges grows as the degree rises. Look only at the average error and you will miss it; you have to look at the <b>worst deviation</b>.</p>',
  'spline:2': '<p>A spline does the same job with a completely different logic: <b>pieces instead of one curve.</b></p><p>The curve is cut at points called <b>knots</b> and a separate cubic polynomial is fitted to each piece. Where the pieces meet they are joined so that the curve and its derivatives stay continuous, producing one smooth curve.</p><p>The critical difference: <b>a spline is a local basis.</b> A knot\'s coefficient affects only its own region. Adding a knot in the middle does not break the edges.</p><p>So on the same parameter budget a spline can put the flexibility <b>where it is needed</b>, while a polynomial has to spread the budget everywhere at once.</p><p>The cubic degree is not a coincidence: it is the lowest degree with a continuous second derivative, that is the cheapest curve that looks smooth to the eye.</p>',
  'spline:3': '<p>Splines are not magic; they are subject to the same bias-variance trade-off.</p><p>Past a certain point adding knots stops paying off. The reason is familiar: the <b>noise floor</b>. The model starts chasing the noise in the data rather than the true curve.</p><p>So the number of knots is a <b>hyperparameter</b> and its right value is chosen by cross validation rather than by eye.</p><p>There is also an alternative approach: a <b>smoothing spline</b>. It puts a knot at every data point but applies a <b>penalty</b> to the curvature. That is, instead of limiting the number of knots it limits how much the curve is allowed to bend.</p><p>The penalty coefficient λ plays exactly the same role as the λ in the ridge lesson. The same idea in different clothes: making flexibility expensive instead of forbidding it.</p>',

  'ozellik-muh:1': '<p>This lesson shows how to change the result without changing the model at all: <b>by changing the columns you give it.</b></p><p>A linear model, as the name says, can only build <b>linear combinations</b> of the columns you give it: a·x₁ + b·x₂ + c. It can add and scale, and nothing else.</p><p>So <b>it cannot derive relationships like a product, a ratio or a square root by itself.</b> If the true rule is the product of two features and you did not supply that product as a column, the relationship is somewhere the model cannot reach. However much data it gets, it will not find it.</p><p>Adding that column, on the other hand, brings the problem within the model\'s reach at the cost of a single parameter. That is where the power of feature engineering comes from: <b>making the problem fit the model instead of making the model bigger.</b></p>',
  'ozellik-muh:2': '<p>Some numbers are not numbers. The hour, the month, the day of the week, an angle, a wind direction: all of these are <b>cyclic</b>.</p><p>Give the hour as an integer between 0 and 23 and you have told the model: "there is a distance of 23 units between 23 and 0." In reality there is one hour between them. The model cannot correct that false information, because that is the definition it was given.</p><p>Adding a polynomial term (hour + hour²) partly helps, because now a peak or a trough can be built. But the two ends of a parabola know nothing of each other; the break at midnight remains.</p><p>The right solution is to encode the hour on a <b>circle</b>: sin(2π·hour/24) and cos(2π·hour/24). Now 23 and 0 really are close and the cycle closes.</p><p>The general principle here: <b>how you encode a feature is a claim you make to the model about that feature.</b> A wrong encoding is a wrong claim.</p>',
  'ozellik-muh:3': '<p>Scaling is not a "tidying step"; for some models it is a <b>requirement</b>.</p><p>The reason: in a model that looks at distance, the contribution of two columns is proportional to the magnitude of their units. If "number of children" is between 0 and 4 and "income" is in the tens of thousands, the distance between two points is determined almost entirely by income. You give two columns and the model sees one.</p><p><b>Standardisation</b> fixes that: it shifts and divides every column so its mean is 0 and its standard deviation 1. Now every column contributes equally to the distance.</p><p>Do not see this as an "improvement". Before scaling, the model was <b>answering the wrong question correctly</b>: "who is the nearest neighbour in income?"</p><p>Which models are affected? Everyone that looks at a distance or a penalty term: kNN, k-means, SVM, PCA, ridge and lasso. Ridge\'s λ·Σw² term also distributes the penalty according to the units of the columns, so an unscaled ridge is meaningless.</p>',
  'ozellik-muh:4': '<p>There is a fair objection: trees and gradient boosting can discover interactions on their own. So why bother?</p><p>The answer is not "can it discover" but <b>"at what cost does it discover"</b>. Because tree splits are perpendicular to the axes, a tree can only approximate a product surface with rectangular steps. It spends data and depth on every step and still cannot catch the exact product.</p><p>So <b>a flexible model is no substitute for the right feature; it builds an expensive approximation of it.</b> Given the right feature, the model expresses it exactly with a handful of parameters.</p><p>In the other direction the tree wins: <b>scaling is completely unnecessary for a tree.</b> A split looks for a threshold, not a distance; scale a column by a factor of a thousand and the threshold scales too and the result does not change.</p><p>The rule that follows: <b>which preprocessing is needed depends on the model.</b> There is no universal checklist; scaling is mandatory for kNN and pointless for a tree.</p>',

  'gam:1': '<p>An additive model is an intermediate form between a linear model and a black box: <b>y = a₀ + f₁(x₁) + f₂(x₂) + …</b></p><p>Every feature has its own curve. More <b>flexible</b> than a linear model, because the curves do not have to be straight. More <b>constrained</b> than a black box, because the features cannot mix: the effect of x₁ is independent of x₂.</p><p>Solving those curves at once is hard. <b>Backfitting</b> does this trick: hold all the other curves fixed, subtract their contribution from y, and fit a single curve to what remains. Then move to the next. And repeat.</p><p>That way a multidimensional fitting problem is broken into <b>one dimensional fitting steps</b>. Because every round has to reduce the remaining error, the process settles into a decreasing sequence and converges quickly.</p>',
  'gam:2': '<p>The real gain of the additive structure is not in accuracy but in <b>readability</b>.</p><p>Because the effect of x₁ is independent of x₂, the question "what happens when x₁ rises" has <b>a single answer</b>, and that answer can be drawn as a curve. The model holds no hidden behaviour anywhere else.</p><p>So <b>these few plots are the entire model.</b> In a black box that is not possible: there a feature\'s effect varies with the values of the other features, so it cannot be told with a single curve.</p><p>And this is not the price of flexibility. Fit a linear model to the same data and you get a far worse result; so the gain comes from the <b>curves</b>, not from interaction. An additive model gives you the flexibility you need and withholds the flexibility you do not.</p><p>The parameter count is small too: two curves and an intercept. Every coefficient is part of a curve and the curve can be drawn directly.</p>',
  'gam:3': '<p>This step shows numerically a recurring idea of the course: <b>the right assumption is free accuracy.</b></p><p>If the data really was generated additively, the additive model <b>carries that information from the start</b>. So it can spend its data on building a smooth curve.</p><p>A tree tries to build the same smooth curve out of axis aligned steps and spends data on every step. Despite being more flexible it falls behind, because flexibility has a price: variance.</p><p>So <b>flexibility is not a virtue in itself.</b> If the assumption a model carries fits the data it brings a gain; if it does not it brings a loss. A correct constraint lowers variance for free, because the bias paid in return is zero.</p><p>In the next step we measure the reverse: what happens when the assumption breaks?</p>',
  'gam:4': '<p>Now the data changes: the output comes entirely from an <b>interaction</b>, with no additive part at all.</p><p>Here the additive model does not merely fall behind, it <b>drops below the mean</b>. The reason is instructive: look at x₁ alone and the average of the output is flat, and the same for x₂. That is, <b>there is nothing to learn in either marginal distribution.</b></p><p>The model still has to fit flexible curves and the only material it has is noise. The result is memorisation.</p><p>In the previous step the opposite happened. The same two models, the same order, the reverse result. The only thing that changed was <b>the structure of the data</b>. That is why "which model is better" is meaningless on its own.</p><p>The route used in practice is somewhere in between: adding <b>a few selected interaction terms</b> to the additive model by hand. That way readability is largely preserved while the known interactions are captured.</p>',
  'pekistirmeli:1': '<p>In every lesson so far there was a <b>right answer</b>: a y for every x. In clustering there were no labels, but at least all the data was in front of us from the start. Here neither holds.</p><p>In reinforcement learning an <b>agent</b> moves in an environment. It makes a move, the environment changes, and occasionally it receives a <b>reward</b>. Nobody tells it "that move was right".</p><p>The real difficulty is that the reward is <b>delayed</b>. You get a reward when you reach the goal, but which move do you credit it to? You made dozens of moves and any of them may have contributed. This is called the <b>credit assignment problem</b>.</p><p>Q-learning\'s answer is elegant: write on every move the <b>discounted value of the best move that follows it</b>. That way the reward seeps backwards from the goal step by step and eventually reaches the starting cell.</p>',
  'pekistirmeli:2': '<p>The agent starts out knowing nothing: it believes every move has value zero.</p><p>Now think: what happens if at every step it does the move it <b>knows to be best</b>? Because all the values are equal it picks the first one, then the first again, forever. It never sees the goal. Because it never sees the goal no value is ever updated. And because the values are never updated it keeps doing the same move.</p><p>The only thing that breaks that vicious circle is making a <b>random move</b>. With some probability (ε) the agent abandons what it knows and tries something at random. Once it reaches the goal even a single time, the reward signal starts to flow.</p><p>This is called the <b>exploration-exploitation dilemma</b> and it sits at the centre of reinforcement learning: do you do the best thing you know, or do you look to see whether something better exists? You cannot do both at once.</p>',
  'pekistirmeli:3': '<p>Exploration is necessary but not free. As ε grows the agent makes more mistakes during training and falls into more pits.</p><p>The surprise here: <b>even at very high exploration the learned policy is not damaged.</b> The agent can learn the right path while wandering around drunk.</p><p>The reason is that Q-learning is <b>off-policy</b>. Its update rule uses not the move actually made but the <b>best</b> move available from that state. So even while behaving randomly, the agent learns the answer to "what would have happened had I behaved optimally".</p><p>The price is not in the learned policy but in <b>the bill paid while learning</b>. In a simulation that is free; on a real robot it means broken parts, and in a live recommendation system lost customers. This is why in practice ε is usually started high and decayed over time.</p>',
  'pekistirmeli:4': '<p>γ (the discount factor) sets the <b>present value</b> of a future reward. Every step backwards multiplies the reward by γ.</p><p>That has a direct consequence: if the goal is k steps away, the value of the starting cell is roughly <b>γ<sup>k</sup></b>. Because γ is less than 1 that shrinks exponentially.</p><p>So γ determines <b>how far the agent can see</b>. If γ is small, a distant reward becomes invisible from the starting point; the signal is lost on the way and the agent can never learn a long term plan.</p><p>This is why in long horizon problems (chess, robot walking) γ is set at 0.99 or above. In short horizon ones (an ad click) even 0.9 can be too much.</p><p>A small note of honesty: the measured values come out slightly above the theoretical γ<sup>k</sup>. That is called <b>maximisation bias</b>: because the max in the update rule picks the largest of a set of noisy estimates, it drifts systematically upwards.</p>',

  'a-yildiz:1': '<p>In the previous lesson the agent did not know the map and learned it by trying. Here the situation is reversed: <b>the map is fully known.</b> Where the walls are, where the goal is, what every move costs; all of it is given.</p><p>If the map is known there is no need to learn. The problem is different: <b>find the shortest path while doing the least work.</b></p><p>That distinction matters and is often confused in practice. Trying to learn a known model is trying to estimate something that can be computed; it is both slow and unnecessary.</p><p><b>Dijkstra</b> gives the simplest answer: expand by distance from the start, that is spread equally in every direction. It guarantees the shortest path. But note: it knows where the goal is and <b>never uses</b> that information. The price is walking almost the whole map.</p>',
  'a-yildiz:2': '<p>The opposite extreme: do not care about the past at all, look only at the <b>estimated remaining distance</b> to the goal.</p><p>That estimate is called a <b>heuristic</b>. It is a cheap-to-compute guess, such as the distance straight to the goal ignoring the walls.</p><p>Greedy search is very fast because it runs straight at the goal. But it <b>has no guarantee</b>, and the reason is this: it never asks "what have I spent to get here". It can mistake a long detour that heads towards the goal but ends in a dead end for something cheap; it goes in and has to come back.</p><p>So at one extreme there is a method that counts all the cost but does not see the goal, and at the other a method that sees the goal but does not count the cost.</p>',
  'a-yildiz:3': '<p>A*\'s idea is to give up neither. One line:</p><p style="text-align:center"><b>f(n) = g(n) + h(n)</b></p><p><b>g(n):</b> the cost really spent from the start to here. What Dijkstra looks at.<br><b>h(n):</b> the estimated cost remaining from here to the goal. What greedy looks at.</p><p>The result does noticeably less work than Dijkstra while <b>still guaranteeing</b> the shortest path.</p><p>But the guarantee has a condition: the heuristic must <b>never overestimate the real remaining cost</b>. That is called <b>admissibility</b>. An overestimating heuristic makes you discard a path that was actually good, and the guarantee is lost.</p><p>A limiting case makes the idea clear: take h(n) = 0 and A* becomes exactly Dijkstra. So Dijkstra is an A* without a heuristic.</p>',
  'a-yildiz:4': '<p>If you want to trust the heuristic more you put a weight in the formula: <b>f(n) = g(n) + w·h(n)</b>.</p><p>As w grows the search runs at the goal more aggressively and expands fewer cells. In return you lose the optimality guarantee.</p><p>But <b>the bound on the loss is known</b>, and that is what makes the method useful in engineering terms: weighted A* finds a path at most <b>w times</b> longer than the shortest. So you are not taking an open-ended risk but making a measured trade.</p><p>In practice that trade is almost always accepted. A character in a game taking a path a few steps longer is invisible; exceeding the per-frame budget is not. This is why game engines usually set w between 1.2 and 2.</p><p>The general idea here recurs in the course: <b>give up on finding the exact optimum and settle for an approximation whose bound you know.</b></p>',

  'taylor:1': '<p>The gradient descent step looks simple: <code>x ← x − η·f\'(x)</code>. But there is a silent assumption behind that rule, and this lesson brings it into the open.</p><p>The assumption: <b>for a small enough step the function can be treated as flat.</b> That is, the tangent line at the point the model stands on can stand in for the function itself nearby.</p><p>In mathematics that is the first order form of the Taylor expansion: f(x + d) ≈ f(x) + f\'(x)·d. Gradient descent looks at that line and takes a step downhill.</p><p>While the step is small the assumption works very well: the tangent and the real curve are almost on top of each other.</p><p>From this comes a useful view: <b>the learning rate is really a trust radius.</b> It is the answer to "how far out do I trust this flat approximation?"</p>',
  'taylor:2': '<p>As the step grows the tangent breaks away from the curve and the prediction fails.</p><p>The real danger here is not the size of the error but its <b>direction</b>. The linear prediction says "the error will fall a lot"; in reality the model has leapt onto the opposite slope of the valley, and higher up at that. So the model <b>thinks it took a good step but did the opposite.</b></p><p>That is exactly why training blows up when the learning rate is too large: a step is taken into a region where the linear approximation is invalid, from there another invalid step is taken, and the values grow by compounding.</p><p>How the error grows is also known: roughly with the <b>square</b> of the step. The reason is the first discarded term of the Taylor series. So double the step and the error quadruples; that is why raising the learning rate becomes dangerous so quickly.</p>',
  'taylor:3': '<p>The way to improve the approximation is to add one more term: the <b>curvature</b>.</p><p>f(x + d) ≈ f(x) + f\'(x)·d + ½·f\'\'(x)·d². Now we are fitting a <b>parabola</b> rather than a line. The second derivative f\'\'(x) says how fast the surface bends.</p><p>The gain is not just "a bit better" but better <b>in kind</b>. The error of the linear approximation grows with the square of the step; the error of the second order approximation grows with its <b>cube</b>. For small steps a cube is far smaller than a square.</p><p>The practical meaning: if you account for the curvature you can step <b>much further</b> at the same error level. The radius of validity of the approximation widens.</p><p>But a parabola is not valid forever either. It collapses too, just later.</p>',
  'taylor:4': '<p>If the parabola approximation is good, why descend step by step? <b>We can jump straight to the vertex of the parabola.</b></p><p>The minimum of a parabola is where its derivative is zero, and the algebra is one line: <b>d = − f\'(x) / f\'\'(x)</b>. That is the <b>Newton step</b>.</p><p>What is striking: <b>there is no learning rate here</b>. The size of the step is set by the curvature itself. Where the curve is sharp it takes a small step, where flat a large one.</p><p>The result is dramatic: Newton <b>converges quadratically</b>, that is the number of correct digits roughly doubles at every step. It finishes in a handful of steps what gradient descent does in dozens.</p><p>So why is it not used in deep learning? Because in many variables f\'\'(x) becomes the <b>Hessian matrix</b>; for p parameters it is p×p and inverting it takes p³ operations. With millions of parameters it neither fits in memory nor can be computed. So in practice methods that <b>approximate the curvature cheaply</b> are used: Adam, RMSProp, L-BFGS.</p>',

  'hessian:1': '<p>In the Taylor lesson the curvature was a single number. In many variables there is <b>a separate curvature in every direction</b>, and together they form a matrix: the <b>Hessian</b>.</p><p>You can see its concrete meaning on the loss surface. If the curvature is equal in all directions the contour lines are <b>circles</b>. If one direction is much steeper than another the contours become <b>ellipses</b> and then a narrow canyon.</p><p>The ratio of the largest curvature to the smallest is called the <b>condition number</b> (κ) and it says with one number how distorted the surface is.</p><p>The critical observation here: <b>the gradient always points in the steepest direction, but the steepest direction is not the direction of the goal.</b> On a circle they are the same. In a narrow canyon the gradient looks at the steep wall while the goal is far away along the canyon.</p>',
  'hessian:2': '<p>Watching what happens in the canyon is instructive: the model <b>zigzags</b> from one wall to the other in the narrow direction while crawling along the long direction it should be travelling.</p><p>The reason is geometric. The gradient is large in the steep direction and small in the flat one; so most of the step goes sideways rather than forwards and is undone at the next step.</p><p>The measurable consequence: <b>the number of steps needed is directly proportional to the condition number.</b> That holds even with the best learning rate; it is not something tuning can fix.</p><p>And the most important inference: this slowness comes not from <b>the difficulty of the model but from the shape of the loss surface</b>. The same problem, given with differently scaled features, is learned far more slowly. This is the real reason for scaling features; it is not a cosmetic step but an intervention that lowers κ directly.</p>',
  'hessian:3': '<p>You cannot raise the learning rate as far as you like, and where the limit is is known <b>exactly</b>.</p><p>With a loss surface of this form the update shrinks the parameter by a fixed factor at every step. To converge, the absolute value of that factor has to be below 1, and that gives a single condition: <b>η has to stay below an upper bound set by the largest curvature.</b></p><p>Just below the limit the model converges. Exactly at it, it oscillates at constant amplitude, neither approaching nor moving away. Above it, it diverges and the values blow up.</p><p>The bitter part appears here: the limit is set by the <b>largest</b> curvature while the speed of convergence is set by the <b>smallest</b>. So the narrowest direction caps the learning rate while the widest direction slows the progress. If κ is large you are squeezed from both sides.</p>',
  'hessian:4': '<p>There are three ways of coping with an ill-conditioned surface and all three are used in practice.</p><p><b>1 · Fix the surface.</b> Scale the features. That equalises the diagonal of the Hessian and lowers κ directly. In deep networks batch norm does a similar job inside the layers.</p><p><b>2 · Add momentum.</b> If you accumulate the steps as you go, the movements in the zigzag directions cancel out while the movement along the canyon accumulates. The result: the number of steps needed grows with <b>√κ</b> instead of κ. At large κ that is an enormous gain.</p><p><b>3 · Give every direction its own step size.</b> Adam and RMSProp do exactly that: they keep an average of the squared gradient and divide every parameter by its own scale. That is a <b>cheap approximation</b> to the diagonal of the Hessian; it gives most of the benefit of the full Hessian without computing it.</p><p>That is the answer to why modern optimisers exist.</p>',
  'gauss-surec:1': '<p>Every model you have seen so far said a single y when given an x. But saying "0.7" and saying "around 0.7, but I am not sure" are not the same thing.</p><p>A <b>Gaussian Process</b> returns not a single number for every x but a <b>distribution</b>: a mean and a standard deviation. So it tells you both its prediction and how much it trusts that prediction.</p><p>To understand how it works one assumption is enough: <b>x values that are close together have y values that are close together.</b> When you add an observation the model learns that point, and the information is <b>local</b>: the uncertainty falls only around that point and distant regions are unaffected.</p><p>This is from the same family as k-NN\'s assumption. The difference: a GP builds it as a probability model, which is why it can give a numerical answer to "how sure am I".</p>',
  'gauss-surec:2': '<p>This step shows the model\'s most valuable behaviour: <b>saying that it does not know.</b></p><p>Over the data points the uncertainty is almost as small as the measurement noise; the model is confident there. Beyond where the data ends the band opens up fast.</p><p>But honesty is needed here, and the lesson provides it: <b>a wide band is not a guarantee that the truth lies inside it.</b> When the data runs out the GP returns to its own <b>prior</b>, and the prior here is "the mean is zero"; if the true function does something else the model can miss it, and it does.</p><p>So an uncertainty estimate is in the end also a <b>model</b>, and if the prior is wrong it is wrong too.</p><p>Still the difference is large. The polynomial in the overfitting lesson gave a confident and wrong number where the data ended. A GP at least <b>declares its own unreliability as you move away.</b> Being able to say "I do not know" is better than giving a wrong answer.</p>',
  'gauss-surec:3': '<p>The heart of a GP is the <b>kernel</b>: the function that says how "similar" two points are considered to be. Its most common form, the RBF kernel, has a single setting: the <b>length scale</b> (l).</p><p>That setting really answers one question: <b>what distance counts as "near"?</b></p><p><b>If l is small</b> the model treats every point as alone. It does not believe neighbours say anything about each other, so it generalises nothing and the uncertainty stays high everywhere.</p><p><b>If l is large</b> everything depends on everything. The model becomes very smooth and dangerously <b>overconfident</b>: it lowers its uncertainty while its prediction may be off.</p><p>The warning here matters: <b>confident and wrong</b> is possible in a GP too. An uncertainty measure is not magic protection; a badly tuned kernel produces badly tuned confidence.</p>',
  'gauss-surec:4': '<p>Uncertainty is not a nice extra; for some problems it is the <b>only solution</b>. Because it is the answer to "where should I look".</p><p><b>Bayesian optimisation.</b> In the hyperparameter lesson you saw random search and successive halving. The third route is to build a GP and say "try where the uncertainty is high and the prediction is good". If every trial is expensive (training a model from scratch, a lab experiment that takes three days) that pays off greatly.</p><p><b>Active learning.</b> If labelling is expensive, you have the example the model is least sure about labelled.</p><p><b>Shift detection.</b> If the uncertainty on an incoming example is abnormally high, the model is telling you that the example does not belong to the world it was trained on.</p><p>There is a price: a GP\'s cost grows with the cube of the number of examples, because it needs the inverse of an n×n matrix. Past a few thousand examples it cannot be used directly; there sparse GP approximations or ensemble/dropout based uncertainty estimates with deep networks take over.</p>',

  'bayes-reg:1': '<p>How do you choose model complexity? This lesson shows a route that uses no validation set. But first it has to be clear why the most obvious route does not work.</p><p>The first thought is to look at the training error. That criterion is useless, and the reason is not an observation but <b>a mathematical necessity.</b></p><p>Every curve that degree d can fit is among the curves that degree d+1 can fit. The best option in a larger set cannot be worse than the best in a smaller one. So the training error <b>has to fall</b> with complexity.</p><p>That is, the training error gives you no information about complexity; it always says "more complex". We need a separate signal.</p>',
  'bayes-reg:2': '<p>The Bayesian question is different: <b>before seeing the data, how much probability would this model family have given to the data we have?</b></p><p>That number is called the <b>marginal likelihood</b>, or the <b>evidence</b> for short. The critical detail: it does not fix the weights at their best value, it <b>averages over all possible weights.</b></p><p>The penalty comes from there and the intuition is beautiful: <b>probability sums to 1.</b> A complex model can produce far more possible datasets, so it has to spread that one unit of probability over a very wide area. The share falling on the data we actually observed shrinks. A simple model spreads it over a narrow area; if the data is in that narrow area it gets a large share.</p><p>So <b>Occam\'s razor here is not a preference but the result of a calculation.</b> We are not saying "the simple one is preferred"; we are computing that the simple model gives the data a higher probability.</p><p>And all of this is done without spending a single validation point.</p>',
  'bayes-reg:3': '<p>To understand the power of a method you need to know where it is decisive and where it is undecided.</p><p>Evidence is <b>overwhelming when rejecting an inadequate model</b>: the difference comes out very large in log units and leaves no room for argument.</p><p>But it <b>stays undecided among adequate models</b>: the difference between them is small and decides nothing.</p><p>That is not a flaw but <b>the right behaviour.</b> With a small number of noisy points there really is no information to separate two sufficiently flexible models. Evidence does not invent it. A method that did invent it would be selling you a certainty that does not exist.</p><p>You saw the same honesty in the Gaussian Process lesson: the band opened up when the data ran out. Here the evidence flattens out when the data is not enough. <b>A method\'s resolution is only as good as the data allows.</b></p>',
  'bayes-reg:4': '<p>Cross validation does the same job. The choice depends on which assumption you accept.</p><p><b>Evidence wins</b> when there is little data: every example you set aside for validation both weakens the training and makes the measurement from those few examples very noisy. Evidence uses all the data. And if training is expensive, evidence needs one training run where k-fold cross validation needs k.</p><p><b>Cross validation wins</b> when you do not trust the prior: evidence depends on p(w), and if the prior is wrong the evidence can be wrong too. Cross validation makes no such assumption. And for models whose likelihood cannot be computed (random forests, gradient boosting) evidence is undefined.</p><p>How much the dependence on the prior bites in practice is <b>measurable</b>: change the prior and see whether the choice changes. That check has been done in this step.</p><p>And a warning that applies to both: <b>they only choose among the candidates you supply.</b> If all of them are bad, they pick the least bad one and do not tell you.</p>',

  'kisit:1': '<p>There is no model to train in this lesson and no data. Only <b>rules</b>. And this is a completely different class of problem from machine learning.</p><p>Writing a problem as a <b>constraint satisfaction problem</b> takes three parts. <b>Variables:</b> what we will assign values to. <b>Domains:</b> which values each variable can take. <b>Constraints:</b> which combinations are forbidden.</p><p>The brute force solution is perfect but the numbers become impossible fast; you saw that in the combinatorics lesson.</p><p>The rescuing idea: <b>check the constraints during generation, not at the end.</b> If a half finished assignment already violates a constraint, every combination that would come from that branch is eliminated in one move. Millions of possibilities fall at once.</p><p>This is called <b>backtracking</b>, and the gain comes neither from a model nor from data but from <b>the structure of the problem</b>.</p>',
  'kisit:2': '<p>Plain backtracking has one blindness: when it assigns a value to a variable it <b>does not notice which options that makes impossible later</b> until it gets to that variable.</p><p><b>Forward checking</b> fixes that. After every assignment the domains of the remaining variables are reviewed and the values that have become impossible are deleted.</p><p>The real gain is here: if a variable\'s domain empties completely, it is clear that there is no solution on that branch <b>before ever reaching it</b>, and the branch is abandoned immediately. The subtree that would have been walked for nothing disappears entirely.</p><p>The idea is not confined to this lesson. The principle "propagate the consequences of a choice at once and give up early if a contradiction appears" stands in the same place from Sudoku solvers to compiler type checking, from timetabling to SAT solvers.</p>',
  'kisit:3': '<p>Now an even more surprising lever: <b>the order in which you handle the variables.</b></p><p>So far we went in order, left to right. There was no reason for that, only habit.</p><p>The <b>MRV</b> (minimum remaining values) rule says: handle the variable with the <b>smallest domain</b>, that is the most constrained one, next.</p><p>The intuition may seem backwards but the logic is this: that variable is going to make the solution hard anyway. Leave it to the end and you only get stuck there after making all the easy choices in front of it, and you have to redo all those easy choices. Try it early and you discover the dead ends <b>while the tree is small</b>.</p><p>The gain can be larger than what you get from changing the algorithm. But note: this is <b>a heuristic, not a theorem.</b> It gives no guarantee; it just works very well in practice.</p>',
  'kisit:4': '<p>Nothing was learned in this lesson. No training set, no loss function, no generalisation. The solution found is <b>definitively correct</b>: it either satisfies the constraints or it does not.</p><p>Machine learning does the exact opposite: because we do not know the rule it extracts an approximate rule from examples, and its output is never certain.</p><p><b>The constraint approach is the right tool</b> if the rules are exactly known and the validity of a solution can be checked: shift rosters, class timetables, production sequencing, circuit layout, package dependency resolution. A package manager resolving version conflicts is exactly this.</p><p><b>It is the wrong tool</b> if the rule cannot be written. You cannot write "is there a cat in this photo" as a constraint.</p><p>A note of honesty: the speedups here do not change the difficulty of the problem. Constraint satisfaction is NP-complete in general. The gain you see comes from this family of problems being tractable; on another set of constraints the same heuristic may gain nothing.</p>',

  'konu-kesif:1': '<p>You have thousands of documents and none of them is labelled. The automatic answer to "what are these about?" is built like this: turn every document into an <b>embedding vector</b>, group the ones that are close together, and call the groups that emerge "topics".</p><p>That is the whole of topic discovery, and when it works it is very valuable.</p><p>But there is something here that has to be told apart carefully: <b>clustering finds proximity, not meaning.</b></p><p>Clusters matching topics is not the success of the clustering algorithm but of the <b>embedding</b>. If the embedding did not separate two topics, no clustering algorithm can. So the chain is only as strong as its weakest link, and that link is usually not the clustering but the representation.</p>',
  'konu-kesif:2': '<p>How will you find the answer to "how many topics are there?" The most natural reflex is to look at how "pure" the clusters found are: the share of each cluster\'s dominant topic.</p><p>That criterion fails for two separate reasons.</p><p><b>First, purity grows with k.</b> At the extreme, put every document in its own cluster and the purity is at its highest by definition. So optimising purity takes you to "every document is its own topic".</p><p><b>Second and more fundamental, purity needs labels.</b> To find the dominant topic of every cluster you have to know the true topic of the documents. If you are doing topic discovery you do not have those labels; if you did there would be no need for discovery.</p><p>This is the fundamental difficulty of unsupervised learning: <b>to measure the accuracy you need exactly the thing you are looking for.</b> In this lesson purity is used as an evaluation tool; that is, we audit the algorithm with information you would not really have.</p>',
  'konu-kesif:3': '<p>Without labels two measures remain and they behave very differently.</p><p>The <b>within-cluster sum of squares</b> measures every point\'s distance to its own centre. It <b>always falls</b> as k grows, because more centres always cover better. Picking the smallest value means k = n. This is why an "elbow" is sought in practice, the point where the fall slows; but the elbow is judged by eye and is often debatable.</p><p>The <b>silhouette</b> compares every point\'s closeness to its own cluster with its closeness to the nearest other cluster. That one has a <b>peak</b>, so it really can suggest a k.</p><p>But as the structure weakens the silhouette gets it wrong too, and <b>there is nobody to tell you it got it wrong.</b></p><p>The real lesson: <b>choosing k is not a calculation but a decision.</b> The silhouette, the elbow and stability analysis help; none of them guarantees anything. The last step is always to look at the clusters and judge whether they make sense.</p>',
  'konu-kesif:4': '<p>The most dangerous side of topic discovery is here: <b>it fails silently.</b></p><p>The algorithm always returns k clusters. The clusters always look consistent enough to be given a name. Nothing on screen looks wrong. There is no signal telling you whether the structure was really there.</p><p>The human side does not help either. Chang and colleagues\' classic 2009 study showed that the outputs of topic models that looked statistically best were not the ones humans found most interpretable. So <b>being able to name a cluster is not proof that the cluster is real</b>; people can find a name even for random groups.</p><p>The practical safeguards: run with different seeds and see whether the clusters stay <b>stable</b>, <b>read</b> random documents from a few clusters and confirm they really are on the same topic, and <b>change the embedding</b> to see whether the result changes.</p>',
  'adillik:1': '<p>This lesson treats a contested subject as <b>arithmetic</b> rather than opinion. So the setup has to be read carefully.</p><p>There are two groups. The model is <b>identical</b> for both: the same score distribution, the same discriminative power, the same threshold. Group membership never enters the model. The only difference is the <b>base rate</b> in the groups: how common the event being sought is.</p><p>The result will be: some fairness measures come out exactly equal in the two groups and some do not. And that is not a flaw in the model.</p><p>The reason is what you saw in the probability lesson: <b>precision (PPV) depends on the base rate.</b> Sensitivity and the false alarm rate are class conditional and unaffected by the base rate; but "the rate at which you are right when you say positive" is affected directly.</p><p>The sentence that follows matters: <b>saying "the model does not look at the group" does not mean it is fair.</b> You have to say on which measure it is fair.</p>',
  'adillik:2': '<p>Say you picked a measure and want to equalise it across the two groups. The only way to do that is to apply <b>different thresholds</b> to the groups.</p><p>This step measures the price of that intervention: equalising one measure breaks another. And the damage grows as the gap in base rates grows.</p><p>Past a certain point something harsher happens: <b>no threshold suffices.</b> The reason is algebraic; the values a measure can take have a lower bound, and if the target value is below that bound then whatever threshold you choose you cannot reach it.</p><p>This is the core of the COMPAS controversy. One side said "this measure is equal, the model is fair" while the other said "that measure differs, the model is not fair". <b>Both were right</b> and satisfying both at once was not possible.</p>',
  'adillik:3': '<p>Everything you have seen so far comes out of a single equation (Chouldechova, 2017):</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">FPR = p · (1 − FNR) · (1 − PPV) / [ (1 − p) · PPV ]</p><p>This is not an approximation but an <b>identity</b>: it holds exactly for every base rate and every threshold.</p><p>The equation has four quantities: the base rate p, FNR, PPV and FPR. <b>Fix three and the fourth is determined by itself.</b> You have no choice left.</p><p>From that follows directly: <b>if two groups have equal FNR and equal PPV and different base rates, their FPRs cannot be equal.</b></p><p>That is not a design choice, a data quality problem or an engineering shortcoming. It is an <b>algebraic necessity.</b> Kleinberg and colleagues proved the same result in a more general form in 2017: calibration and error rate balance can only hold together with different base rates if the classifier is perfect.</p><p>So <b>there is no single thing called a "fair model".</b></p>',
  'adillik:4': '<p>The impossibility result does not say "do not pursue fairness". It says: <b>you have to make a choice and you have to write your choice down explicitly.</b></p><p><b>1 · Ask which error costs whom.</b> A false positive is severe if it declares an innocent person a suspect. A false negative is severe if it sends a sick person home. That question chooses the measure, not the mathematics.</p><p><b>2 · Ask where the difference in base rates comes from.</b> Throughout this lesson we took that difference as data. In reality it is often past inequality itself. Fixing the model does not fix that inequality; it only chooses how it will be transmitted.</p><p><b>3 · Question the label itself.</b> The label "reoffended" actually means "was caught again", and the rate of being caught varies by group. If what you measure is not what you want to measure, none of the fairness measures will save you.</p><p><b>4 · Write it down.</b> Model cards exist for exactly this: how it was measured on which groups, which definition was chosen, and what was not measured. Transparency here is not an alternative to fairness but a <b>precondition</b> for it.</p><p>One last distinction: equality between groups and individual justice are not the same. Even if all the group averages are equalised, individual decisions being justifiable is a separate requirement.</p>',

  'leaderboard:1': '<p>This lesson shows a situation where the numbers are correct and the conclusion is still wrong.</p><p>We set up a thought experiment: <b>every model on the leaderboard is really the same</b>. None is better than any other. Every model\'s observed score deviates a little up or down through random measurement error, because the test set is finite.</p><p>Then what do we do? <b>We pick the highest score.</b></p><p>And picking the highest score is <b>picking the luckiest measurement error.</b> Coming first is about the error deviating in the right direction, not about being good.</p><p>This is called the <b>winner\'s curse</b> and it is a known, unavoidable side effect of multiple comparisons. Nobody cheated, no model was overfitted, there is no leakage. The only source of the inflation is <b>the act of selection itself.</b></p><p>The practical consequence: the number at the top of a leaderboard <b>is not</b> an unbiased estimate of that model\'s real performance. It is biased upwards, and the bias grows with the number of models.</p>',
  'leaderboard:2': '<p>The second question matters more: is the winner really the best model?</p><p>The answer depends on one thing: <b>how large is the true difference relative to the measurement noise?</b></p><p>If there is really no difference between the models, the probability of the winner being best is exactly <b>1/N</b>; that is, no different from picking at random. As the true difference grows that probability rises.</p><p>The threshold is this: <b>if the true difference is smaller than the measurement noise, the ranking carries no information.</b> However carefully you look, the ranking is essentially a coin flip.</p><p>Computing the measurement noise is not hard either: the standard error of an accuracy p on n examples is √(p(1−p)/n). If the gap between first and second is below that magnitude, the ranking between them is meaningless.</p><p>This is why serious comparisons report a confidence interval or a statistical test rather than a single score.</p>',
  'leaderboard:3': '<p>Since the source of the inflation is measurement noise, growing the test set should be the fix. It is, but <b>more slowly than you would think.</b></p><p>The reason is familiar: the binomial standard error falls with <b>1/√n</b>. So to halve the error you have to quadruple the test set. To cut it to a tenth, multiply by a hundred.</p><p>And the inflation <b>does not go to zero</b>. However large a test set you build, the score of the leader picked from among many models stays somewhat inflated.</p><p>The second variable is more annoying still: <b>you do not control the number of models.</b> On a public leaderboard you cannot know how many people made how many attempts, and the inflation grows with that.</p><p>The real danger is one step further: submitting to the same test set again and again and changing the model according to the result each time slowly turns that test set into a <b>training set</b>. Dwork and colleagues\' work on adaptive data analysis addresses exactly this problem.</p>',
  'leaderboard:4': '<p>The reading rules that follow from the three measurements:</p><p><b>1 · Read the interval, not the leader.</b> A single score is not unbiased. If no confidence interval is given, compute it roughly: the standard error of an accuracy p on n examples is √(p(1−p)/n).</p><p><b>2 · Ask how many models competed.</b> The inflation grows with the number of models; on a table with hundreds of submissions the top places are indistinguishable.</p><p><b>3 · Ask how many submissions were made.</b> Repeated submission slowly turns the table into a training curve.</p><p><b>4 · A new test set is the strongest evidence.</b> Recht and colleagues collected a brand new test set for ImageNet in 2019 and the accuracy of every model dropped noticeably.</p><p>But the same study also strikes a balance: <b>the ranking was largely preserved.</b> So the absolute numbers were inflated while the relative ranking was more robust.</p><p>The conclusion: leaderboards are not worthless. <b>They exaggerate the absolute numbers but still carry information about distant rankings.</b> What is unreliable is comparing top places that are close together.</p>',

  'aktif-ogrenme:1': '<p>Labelled data is expensive: an expert doctor, an experienced engineer, hours of human effort. That raises a reasonable question: <b>which examples should I have labelled to gain the most?</b></p><p>Two strategies are compared. <b>Random:</b> pick from the pool at random. Simple and unbiased. <b>Uncertainty sampling:</b> train the current model, pick the example it is least sure about (nearest the decision boundary), have it labelled, repeat.</p><p>Intuition favours uncertainty sampling: labelling an example the model is already sure about adds no information, while one it is unsure about teaches a lot.</p><p>The intuition turns out right, but <b>the gain should not be exaggerated</b>: it is not a magical leap but a saving of roughly a factor of two. And there is a <b>ceiling</b>: because the data is noisy, no method can go above a certain accuracy. Active learning gets you to that ceiling faster; it does not raise the ceiling.</p>',
  'aktif-ogrenme:2': '<p>Understanding where the gain comes from is essential to using the method correctly. And the answer is different from what you would expect.</p><p>Uncertainty sampling picks points near the decision boundary. And the decision boundary is <b>where the two classes meet</b>. So selecting from there naturally brings in roughly equal numbers from both classes.</p><p>That is, active learning also works, unintentionally, as a <b>class balancer</b>. On imbalanced data the positive share it collects rises far above the share in the pool. That is why the gain grows on imbalanced data.</p><p>But the same mechanism carries an important warning: <b>the labelled set is no longer a representative sample of the pool.</b> Use those labels for another purpose (estimating the class ratio, training another model) and you get a biased result. This is one of the least discussed costs of active learning.</p>',
  'aktif-ogrenme:3': '<p>Now the case where the method fails: the <b>cold start.</b></p><p>The thing measuring the uncertainty is <b>the model itself</b>. If the model was trained on a handful of examples its uncertainty estimate is nearly random.</p><p>The result is a vicious circle: the model mistakes a wrong region for "uncertain", asks for examples from there, those examples feed its own error, and the selection locks onto the wrong region. In that situation uncertainty sampling is <b>worse than random</b>.</p><p>As the budget grows it reverses, because the model improves and the uncertainty estimate becomes meaningful.</p><p>This is why in practice active learning is <b>never started from scratch</b>. The standard approach is to make the first round random, build a reasonable starting model, and only then switch to uncertainty.</p><p>A second safeguard: use criteria that also consider <b>diversity</b> rather than pure uncertainty. Pure uncertainty can pick very similar examples from the same region one after another; if you are selecting in batches that is a serious waste.</p>',
  'aktif-ogrenme:4': '<p>The conditions for use that follow from the measurements:</p><p><b>1 · Labelling really has to be expensive.</b> The gain is about a factor of two. If the cost per label is low, that saving does not pay for the complexity of setup and maintenance.</p><p><b>2 · The pool has to be large and varied.</b> If there is nothing to choose from, a selection method gains nothing.</p><p><b>3 · Class imbalance grows the gain</b>, because selecting near the boundary balances the labelled set.</p><p><b>4 · The starting model has to be reasonable.</b> On a cold start the method does harm.</p><p>And a hidden cost: <b>the collected label set is biased.</b> If you change the model (moving from logistic regression to a neural network, say) those labels were selected according to the <i>old</i> model\'s uncertainty and may not be the most informative set for the new one. Lowell and colleagues showed that this is a serious obstacle in practice.</p><p>In short: active learning is not magic but a measured saving. If your cost per label is high it is worth it; if not, random sampling gives both a simpler and a more honest dataset.</p>',

  'automl:1': '<p>AutoML automates model and hyperparameter selection. It really works, but this lesson measures <b>where the gain comes from</b>, and the answer runs against intuition.</p><p>The setup: a model family, two hyperparameters, and a <b>search space</b> made of all their combinations. Plus a reasonable <b>default</b> configuration, that is the model you would build with no search at all.</p><p>What you will see in this step: the great majority of the configurations in the space are <b>worse</b> than the default. Only a small minority are better.</p><p>That has nothing to do with the search; it has to do with <b>the space itself</b>. A random search will spend most of its budget trying options worse than the default. That is not an algorithm problem but a problem of <b>who defined the space</b>.</p>',
  'automl:2': '<p>Now a random search is run and the result is instructive.</p><p>A single trial is noticeably <b>worse</b> than the default. An expected outcome, since most of the space is bad anyway.</p><p>What is interesting: it takes the search several trials even to <b>catch</b> the default. That is, all the early trials go towards recovering the point you would have had with no search at all.</p><p>Everything after that is profit, but the gain is <b>small</b>. A search of thirty trials brings a modest improvement over not searching.</p><p>This explains why AutoML tools start with reasonable defaults and predefined "portfolios". Searching randomly from scratch wastes most of the budget; starting from known good points removes that waste.</p>',
  'automl:3': '<p>Now one thing changes: <b>the search space is narrowed.</b> The algorithm is the same, the budget is the same, only the region searched is restricted to the part known to be good.</p><p>The result is striking: <b>a single random trial</b> in the narrow space beats what dozens of trials reach in the wide space.</p><p>The sentence that follows is the least discussed side of AutoML: <b>the search space matters more than the search algorithm.</b></p><p>Bergstra and Bengio showing that random search beats grid search was important, but both search <b>inside the same space</b>. Narrowing the space beats both.</p><p>A note of honesty: in this lesson the narrow space was chosen by looking at the previous step\'s measurement, that is narrowed "by looking at the answer". In real life that information comes from domain knowledge, previous work or a small pilot run. Wherever it comes from, <b>it is the most valuable input.</b></p>',
  'automl:4': '<p>Where the measurements place it:</p><p><b>AutoML is a good baseline producer.</b> With no human expert around, a tool that starts with reasonable defaults and portfolios quickly gives a usable model. The strength of tools like AutoGluon comes largely from this and from the ensembling they do at the end.</p><p><b>It is no substitute for domain knowledge.</b> You measured it: the information that narrows the space gains more than the search itself. And that information is not in the tool but in you.</p><p><b>The validation set gets used up.</b> Trying hundreds of configurations on the same validation set is <b>exactly</b> the winner\'s curse from the leaderboard lesson: the validation score of the chosen configuration is biased upwards. So an AutoML result must be verified on a separate test set.</p><p><b>The real bottleneck is usually not model selection.</b> In most projects feature engineering, data quality and the definition of the problem are far more decisive than hyperparameter choice. AutoML does none of those.</p>',
  'noron:1': '<p>"Neural network" sounds like something complicated. But the building block is simple enough to write on one line.</p><p>A neuron takes an input vector of numbers and produces a single number. It does that with three operations:</p><p><b>1 · Weighted sum.</b> Each input is multiplied by its own <b>weight</b> and everything is added up: w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … The weight determines how much say that input has in the decision. Large means important, near zero means all but ignored, negative means it pushes the other way.</p><p><b>2 · Add the bias.</b> A constant is added to the sum. This shifts the <b>threshold</b> of the decision: it makes the neuron easier or harder to trigger whatever the inputs are. It plays the same role as the b in the line equation y = ax + <b>b</b>.</p><p><b>3 · Activation.</b> The resulting number is passed through a function. This lesson uses the sigmoid: it squashes any number into the range 0 to 1, making the result readable as a probability.</p><p>That is all: <b>multiply → sum → add bias → squash.</b> Note that the first two steps are exactly linear regression. The only thing separating a neuron from it is the non-linear squashing in step three, and as you will see shortly all the power comes from there.</p>',
  'noron:2': '<p><b>All the knowledge</b> a neuron has is its weights and its bias. It has no other memory, no other rule, no other intuition.</p><p>In this step you turn those numbers by hand. So you feel two things:</p><p><b>The sign of the weight sets the direction.</b> A positive weight says "as this input grows, let the output grow"; a negative one says the opposite. Pull a weight negative and the neuron has learned a nonsensical rule, and it <b>does not object</b>. A neuron does not know what is reasonable; it computes whatever you give it. The meaning is not in the numbers but in the training process that tunes those numbers from data.</p><p><b>The bias alone can flip the decision.</b> A large enough negative bias makes the neuron say "no" for every input without touching the weights at all. That is why the bias is not decoration but a real parameter; it is learned along with the weights during training.</p><p>A matter of scale: this single neuron has a handful of parameters. Large language models contain hundreds of billions of parameters of the same kind. <b>The difference is not in the complexity of a single neuron but in the number and the wiring.</b></p>',
  'noron:3': '<p>Now you prove you understood it: you write the forward pass yourself.</p><p>The <b>forward pass</b> is the one-way computation from input to output. That is exactly what "the network makes a prediction" means; when you use a trained model, the forward pass is the only thing running.</p><p>It will be three lines of code:</p><p><b>1 · z = sum of w · x.</b> Each weight is multiplied by its input and the results summed. This operation is the <b>dot product</b>, and nearly all the computation in a neural network is this. GPUs are so useful precisely because they can do millions of dot products at once.</p><p><b>2 · z += b.</b> Add the bias.</p><p><b>3 · a = σ(z).</b> Pass through the activation.</p><p>The next question is unavoidable: where do those weights come from? The answer is in the next lesson: <b>backpropagation</b>. The error is measured at the output and distributed backwards, via the chain rule, to every weight as its own share.</p>',

  'backprop:1': '<p>Training a network consists of <b>two separate passes</b>, and it matters not to confuse them.</p><p><b>Forward pass:</b> the input moves layer by layer, each neuron computes its weighted sum and activation, and a prediction comes out at the end. The prediction is compared with the true label and a single number is obtained: the <b>loss</b>.</p><p><b>Backward pass:</b> the real question is: <i>if I changed this weight a little, how much would the loss change?</i> That is ∂L/∂w. This derivative is needed for every weight in the network.</p><p>The naive way would be to nudge each weight one at a time and recompute the loss; with millions of parameters that is impossible. Backpropagation solves it with the <b>chain rule</b>.</p><p>The chain rule is the rule for differentiating composed functions, and a network is exactly a composed function: layer on layer. Once the derivative at the output is known, the derivative of the previous layer follows from it by multiplication. So the error <b>flows backwards</b> and all the gradients are computed in a single pass, at a cost of the same order as the forward pass.</p><p>Then gradient descent takes over: every weight takes a small step in the direction opposite its gradient. <b>Forward → loss → backward → update.</b> That is all a training step is.</p>',
  'backprop:2': '<p>Now that loop repeats thousands of times and you are watching a network that really works. The numbers are not an animation; the network is training in your browser, on your machine.</p><p>Watch three things:</p><p><b>The loss falls and the accuracy rises, but not in a straight line.</b> It goes up and down as it advances. That is normal; each step is a small correction in a single direction and the surface is bumpy.</p><p>The decision boundary starts out nearly random, because the weights are initialised randomly. Over time it settles onto the shape of the data. <b>Nobody told the network what the shape was.</b> The network was only given examples and how wrong each prediction was; the shape emerged as a by-product of reducing that error.</p><p>This is the core promise of neural networks: you do not write the rule, the rule comes out of the data. And the price is right there too: <b>the network will not tell you why that rule is the rule.</b></p>',

  'cnn:1': '<p>Feeding an image to a plain neural network is a bad idea for two reasons.</p><p><b>1 · The parameter count explodes.</b> In a dense layer every pixel connects to every neuron. Even on a tiny image that means tens of thousands of weights; on a real photograph, millions. For the first layer alone.</p><p><b>2 · Neighbourhood information is lost.</b> To a dense network an image is an unordered list of numbers. There is no difference between two adjacent pixels and two pixels at opposite corners. Yet nearly all the meaning in an image is <b>local</b>: edges, corners and textures all arise from the relationship between neighbouring pixels.</p><p><b>Convolution</b> solves both at once. A small <b>filter</b> (a 3×3 table of numbers, say) is slid over the image. At each position the pixels beneath it are multiplied elementwise and summed, producing a single number.</p><p>Two properties follow:</p><p><b>Parameter sharing:</b> the same 9 numbers are used across the whole image. However large the image gets, the filter\'s parameter count does not change.</p><p><b>Translation equivariance:</b> if the filter has learned to find an edge, it finds that edge <b>wherever</b> in the image it appears. A dense network would have to learn every position separately.</p>',
  'cnn:2': '<p>A single step of convolution is this: place the filter at a position, multiply it elementwise with the pixels beneath, sum them all, write the result into the output map, shift the filter by one pixel and repeat.</p><p>That is <b>multiply, sum, shift, repeat.</b> Two nested loops; conceptually no more complicated than a sorting algorithm.</p><p>What comes out is called a <b>feature map</b>. Every number in the map answers the question "how much of the pattern this filter looks for is present here". A high value means a strong match.</p><p>The magic is not in the numbers inside the filter but in two things:</p><p><b>1 · The filters are learned.</b> In classical image processing, edge-detecting filters were designed by hand. In a convolutional network those 9 numbers are learned by backpropagation like any other weights. The network decides for itself which pattern to look for.</p><p><b>2 · Layers stack.</b> The first layer finds simple things like edges and colour transitions. The second finds combinations of those edges (corners, curves), later ones larger parts. Depth composes simple patterns <b>hierarchically</b>. Meaning in an image being built this way is the real reason convolutional networks work.</p>',
  'optimizer:1': '<p>Gradient descent tells you a direction: the steepest descent. But <b>how far and how</b> to move in that direction is a separate question, and the optimizer is exactly what answers it.</p><p>The problem is this: a loss surface is often like a <b>valley</b>; very steep in one direction, nearly flat in another. Plain gradient descent oscillates in the steep direction and crawls in the flat one. Raise the learning rate and the oscillation blows up; lower it and progress stops.</p><p>Three methods are compared:</p><p><b>SGD:</b> θ ← θ − η·g. It looks only at the current slope and has no memory.</p><p><b>Momentum:</b> it carries part of the past steps (v ← βv + g, θ ← θ − ηv). Like a ball rolling downhill: speed accumulates along the valley while opposing side-to-side oscillations cancel each other. So it both accelerates and settles.</p><p><b>Adam:</b> adds one more thing to momentum. It tracks the past gradient magnitude of each parameter separately and scales the step <b>per parameter</b>. A parameter that keeps getting large gradients takes smaller steps, one that always gets small gradients takes larger ones. That is what makes a big difference when parameters live on very different scales.</p><p>A warning: the learning rate <b>does not mean the same thing</b> in these three methods. Because Adam already normalises the step, its typical η is far smaller than SGD\'s. When you change optimizer you have to search for the learning rate again.</p>',

  'aktivasyon:1': '<p>This is the most basic justification for deep learning, and it is shown with one line of algebra.</p><p>A layer does this: z = Wx + b. A linear operation. Let us stack two layers with no activation between them:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">W₂(W₁x + b₁) + b₂ = (W₂W₁)x + (W₂b₁ + b₂) = W\'x + b\'</p><p>The result is again <b>a single linear layer</b>. Three layers, a hundred layers, it makes no difference: the composition of linear operations is linear.</p><p>So without activations <b>depth gains you nothing</b>. Add as many parameters as you like and the model can still only draw a line (or a plane).</p><p>The activation is the <b>non-linear</b> link placed between layers, and it breaks that chain. The moment it breaks, the layers no longer collapse into each other; each layer genuinely transforms the previous one\'s output into something new. That is the one thing that makes deep learning possible.</p><p>Which function to use is a second question (sigmoid, tanh, ReLU, GELU), and as the next step shows, that choice decides whether the network can be trained at all.</p>',
  'aktivasyon:2': '<p>To see why the choice of activation is vital you have to look at the multiplicative structure of backpropagation.</p><p>As the gradient flows from output to input it is <b>multiplied at every layer by that layer\'s activation derivative</b>. In a ten-layer network the first layer\'s gradient contains the product of the ten derivatives it met on the way.</p><p>A simple consequence follows: <b>if the derivative is consistently below 1, the product decays exponentially.</b> Think of 0.25 raised to the tenth power. The gradient of the early layers goes to zero in practice, meaning those layers never learn. This is the <b>vanishing gradient</b> problem.</p><p>The derivative ceilings of the functions summarise the whole matter:</p><p><b>sigmoid</b> · its derivative is at most 0.25. Unusable in a deep network; today it is used only in the output layer, to produce a probability.</p><p><b>tanh</b> · its derivative reaches 1 and it is zero centred. Noticeably better; still common in RNNs.</p><p><b>ReLU</b> · on the positive side the derivative is exactly 1, so the gradient passes without decaying at all. That is why it is the default for deep networks. The price is that the derivative is 0 on the negative side: a neuron that keeps receiving negative input is never updated again (a "dead neuron").</p><p><b>GELU</b> · a smooth version of ReLU with no sharp corner at zero. The standard in Transformers.</p><p>A significant part of why deep learning was stuck until the 2010s was exactly this; the spread of ReLU was one of the steps that removed the obstacle.</p>',

  'mlp:1': '<p>This lesson corrects a common intuition.</p><p>Asked how a neural network separates data shaped like two nested rings, most people say "it draws a curved boundary". <b>That is not what happens.</b></p><p>The network\'s final layer is, from beginning to end, <b>a simple linear classifier</b>: it draws a straight boundary and can do nothing else. What changes is not the boundary but the <b>space</b> the boundary is drawn in.</p><p>Every hidden layer moves the points around: it stretches, rotates and folds the space. Two classes that were interleaved in the input space drift apart after a few transformations and become <b>separable by a straight line</b>. Only then does the final layer step in, and it has an easy job.</p><p>That is why the output of the hidden layers is called a <b>representation</b>. Another name for deep learning is representation learning: the model does not learn to classify but learns the coordinate system that makes classification easy.</p><p>So why is depth needed? In theory it is not: the <b>universal approximation theorem</b> says a single sufficiently wide hidden layer can approximate almost any continuous function to any desired accuracy. But "sufficiently wide" can be astronomical.</p><p>The gain from depth is not expressive power but <b>efficiency and hierarchy</b>: doing the same job with far fewer neurons, by combining simple pieces stage by stage. The theorem says a function can be represented; it does not say it can be <b>found</b> by gradient descent.</p>',

  'regular:1': '<p>You know overfitting as a definition; now you will see <b>the moment it happens</b>.</p><p>The experiment is deliberately set up to produce overfitting: a small and noisy training set, far more parameters than that warrants, and a separate, clean validation set. The model has more than enough capacity to memorise the data.</p><p>As training proceeds two curves are watched:</p><p><b>The training loss</b> almost always falls. That is not surprising; the optimisation is trying to do exactly that. Looked at alone, the model seems to keep improving.</p><p><b>The validation loss</b> falls first, reaches a bottom, then <b>starts to rise</b>.</p><p>That bottom is where the model has finished extracting generalisable pattern from the data and moved on to <b>memorising the noise</b>. The training loss is still falling, but everything gained from then on is specific to that training set.</p><p>This is the operational definition of overfitting: <b>the two curves diverging.</b></p><p>And the natural fix is right there: <b>early stopping</b>. Keep the weights from the moment the validation loss was lowest and throw away the rest. In practice it is done with a patience counter: if the validation loss does not improve for N epochs, stop and restore the best saved weights. It is free, easy to implement and usually the highest-return regularisation there is.</p>',
  'regular:2': '<p>Early stopping tunes "when to stop". The other methods tune <b>how free</b> the model is.</p><p><b>Weight decay (L2 regularisation)</b> adds the magnitude of the weights to the loss function:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">L = L_data + λ · Σw²</p><p>In the gradient update this means the weights are pulled slightly towards zero at every step. The effect is a bargain: if the model wants to grow a weight, it has to lower the data loss enough in return. That keeps the model away from the extreme weights it would need to fit noise.</p><p>λ is a dose, and both ends are bad: too small and nothing changes, too large and the model can no longer fit the data at all, that is <b>underfitting</b> begins. So it is not chosen without measuring.</p><p>Two more methods sit alongside it:</p><p><b>Dropout:</b> at every training step a random fraction of the neurons is temporarily switched off. The model cannot rely on any single neuron and is forced to spread the information out. It is active only during training; at prediction time all the neurons run.</p><p><b>Data augmentation:</b> generating variations of existing examples that do not change their meaning (rotation, cropping, adding noise). Unlike the others it <b>adds real information</b> instead of constraining the model: the knowledge that "this transformation does not change the label". It is the most effective method on images.</p><p>A technical note: if you use Adam, prefer <b>AdamW</b>. The classical L2 penalty interferes with Adam\'s per-parameter scaling and does not work as expected; AdamW separates the two.</p>',
  'batchnorm:1': '<p>As a signal moves layer by layer through a deep network, its scale does not stay fixed. Every layer multiplies its input by a weight matrix, and that multiplication either grows or shrinks the signal a little.</p><p>An effect that looks negligible in one layer is <b>repeated as many times as there are layers</b>. So the scale accumulates multiplicatively. A signal multiplied by 0.9 at every layer has all but vanished after twelve layers; one multiplied by 1.3 is pinned to the ceiling at the same depth.</p><p>There are two failure modes and both kill the gradient:</p><p><b>Vanishing:</b> the activations approach zero. There is no information left to carry, and in backpropagation the gradient is zero too.</p><p><b>Saturation:</b> the activations stick to the flat region of the function (the ±extremes in sigmoid/tanh). There the derivative goes to zero, so again no gradient passes.</p><p>The only thing changed in this step is <b>the scale of the initial weights</b>. The architecture, the data and the number of layers are fixed. Only that one number varies, and the state of the signal in the final layer changes completely.</p><p>Smart initialisation (Xavier, He) mitigates the problem by choosing that scale well. But it is not a permanent fix: <b>as training proceeds the weights change</b> and the balance set up at the start breaks again. The next step fills exactly that gap.</p>',
  'batchnorm:2': '<p>The idea is one sentence: <b>if the scale degrades along the way, fix it again at every layer.</b></p><p>Batch normalization computes, at every layer, the mean and variance of the activations in the current mini-batch and standardises the activation:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">ẑ = (z − μ) / √(σ² + ε)</p><p>So the signal enters every layer at roughly the same scale. The scale of the initial weights becomes almost irrelevant, because the degradation is undone immediately at every layer.</p><p>But it does not end there. Raw normalisation deprives a layer of something: <b>the freedom to choose its own scale and centre</b>. In some cases a layer\'s output needs to be small, or shifted. So two <b>learnable</b> parameters are added:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">y = γ·ẑ + β</p><p>γ and β are learned by backpropagation like any other weights. So the network can <b>partly or fully undo</b> the normalisation when it needs to. It becomes a default starting point rather than a constraint.</p><p>The practical gains: insensitivity to the initial scale, the ability to train with a higher learning rate, faster convergence.</p><p>It has one limit: BN computes its statistics <b>over the batch</b>. If the batch is small the estimates are noisy; and with sequences there is no natural batch axis because lengths differ. That is why Transformers use not BN but <b>layer normalization</b>, which takes its statistics over the features of a single example.</p>',

  'embed:1': '<p>A model does not understand words; it only processes numbers. So how are words turned into numbers?</p><p>The crudest way is <b>one-hot</b>: give each word an index and set only that index to 1 in a vector the size of the vocabulary. It has two problems: the vector is enormous and sparse, and worse, <b>every word is equally far from every other word</b>. The distance from "cat" to "dog" is the same as from "cat" to "mathematics". So it carries no information about meaning.</p><p>An <b>embedding</b> instead represents each word with a low-dimensional dense vector and <b>learns</b> that vector from data.</p><p>The principle the learning rests on comes from linguistics: <b>"You shall know a word by the company it keeps."</b> (Firth, 1957). Words that occur in the same contexts should be similar.</p><p>Methods like skip-gram turn this directly into a prediction task: given a word, predict the words around it. To be good at that task the model is forced to give similar vectors to words that occur in similar contexts. The semantic structure emerges as a <b>by-product</b> of that task.</p><p>Similarity is usually measured by <b>cosine similarity</b>: the cosine of the angle between two vectors. It compares direction rather than length, because meaning is encoded in direction.</p><p>The embeddings on this page were really trained, and no category label was ever given. Yet words in the same category ended up near each other. <b>Nobody said "these are animals";</b> the structure came out of the context statistics.</p>',
  'embed:2': '<p>Embeddings sit somewhere more central today than you might think:</p><p><b>Semantic search:</b> the query and the documents are embedded into the same space and the nearest vectors are retrieved. Shared meaning is caught even with no shared words.</p><p><b>RAG:</b> is built on exactly this mechanism; document chunks are embedded, the question is embedded, the nearest ones are added to the context.</p><p><b>Recommenders:</b> users and items are embedded into the same space and proximity becomes a recommendation.</p><p><b>Transformers:</b> every token is first turned into an embedding vector; attention operates on top of those.</p><p>There are three serious traps:</p><p><b>1 · Context independence.</b> In classical methods like word2vec each word has <b>one</b> vector. The word "bank" cannot distinguish a riverbank from a financial institution; both are crushed into a single vector. BERT and everything after it fixed this: the embedding now varies with the sentence, that is it is <b>contextual</b>.</p><p><b>2 · Bias.</b> An embedding learns the co-occurrence statistics of the data. If the data carries social bias, the embedding encodes it faithfully and every system using that embedding carries it along. That is not a bug but a direct consequence of the method, which is why it has to be measured.</p><p><b>3 · Mismatched models.</b> Embed the query with one model and the documents with another and the result is garbage. And it is <b>silently</b> garbage: the system keeps working, only what it retrieves is irrelevant. A language mismatch gives the same outcome.</p>',

  'transfer:1': '<p>The most useful idea in practical deep learning is this: <b>do not start from scratch.</b></p><p>A network\'s early layers learn general, task-independent features (edges and textures in images, syntactic patterns in text). The later layers are task-specific. So the hard work, that is <b>learning a representation</b>, need not be redone if it has been done once with a lot of data.</p><p><b>Transfer learning</b> is exactly that: take the body of a model trained on plentiful data and put a small head for the new task on top of it.</p><p>In this step three strategies are compared <b>with the same small number of examples</b>:</p><p><b>From scratch:</b> start from random weights and train everything.</p><p><b>Frozen transfer:</b> freeze the pre-trained hidden layers and train only the final layer.</p><p><b>Fine-tuning:</b> start from the pre-trained weights but leave every layer free.</p><p>The expectation: with little data, training from scratch is hopeless, because a handful of examples cannot tune a whole hidden layer. But it is more than enough to tune <b>a few weights in the final layer</b>. The critical point: for transfer to work the source and target tasks have to be <b>related</b>; features inherited from an unrelated source gain you nothing.</p>',
  'transfer:2': '<p>The result in this step runs against intuition: fine-tuning comes out <b>worse</b> than frozen transfer. Yet fine-tuning has more freedom.</p><p>The reason is exactly that freedom.</p><p>When you start updating all the weights with very few examples, the gradients carry the noise of those few examples. The <b>well-learned features in the hidden layers get damaged</b>: the model sacrifices what it learned from plentiful data in order to fit a handful of examples.</p><p>This is called <b>catastrophic forgetting</b> and it is the known risk of fine-tuning on small data.</p><p>In practice the decision goes like this:</p><p><b>Little data →</b> freeze the body and train only the head. Few parameters are trained, so the overfitting risk is low.</p><p><b>Medium data →</b> unfreeze gradually: train the head first, then open the upper layers, leaving the lower ones frozen. The lower layers hold the most general features and are touched last.</p><p><b>Plenty of data →</b> open everything, but with <b>discriminative learning rates</b>: a small rate for the lower layers and a larger one for the upper. That preserves the general features while the task-specific layers adapt freely.</p><p>The general rule: pre-trained weights are free knowledge, and you decide how much freedom to allow <b>according to how easily you could destroy that knowledge</b>.</p>',
  'ilkleme:1': '<p>Before you can train a network you have to give the weights a starting value. You cannot set them all to zero (then every neuron computes the same thing and receives the same gradient, so the network collapses into a single neuron), so random numbers are used. The question is: <b>how large should those random numbers be?</b></p><p>This lesson shows how decisive that one number is at depth.</p><p>The mechanism is multiplicative. The signal is multiplied by the weights at every layer; if the weights are on average a little large the standard deviation of the signal <b>grows</b> slightly at every layer, if a little small it <b>shrinks</b>. Over twenty layers that ratio is raised to the twentieth power.</p><p>The consequence of exponential behaviour: a change of <b>a few times</b> in the scale turns into a difference of <b>orders of magnitude</b> at the final layer. Keep it too small and the signal falls to zero fast; make it larger and it explodes fast. The healthy region in between is narrow.</p><p>And both extremes end training: if the signal died in the forward pass the returning gradient is zero too; if it exploded the gradient overflows numerically.</p><p>So initialisation is not a detail but a decision that determines whether the network can be trained at all, <b>before training even starts</b>.</p>',
  'ilkleme:2': '<p>The right scale is not guessed; it is <b>derived</b> by looking at what happens in a single layer.</p><p>A neuron multiplies fan_in inputs by weights and sums them. In a sum of independent terms <b>the variances add</b>, so the variance of the output is roughly fan_in · σ² · (input variance).</p><p>We want the scale of the signal preserved from layer to layer, that is we want that factor to be 1:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">fan_in · σ² = 1  →  σ² = 1 / fan_in</p><p>This is <b>Xavier (Glorot) initialisation</b> and it is the right answer for symmetric, zero-centred activations.</p><p>But there is ReLU. ReLU zeroes every negative output, that is it <b>throws away roughly half</b> the signal. The discarded half also cuts the variance roughly in half. In a ReLU network initialised with Xavier every layer shrinks the signal a little, and at depth that accumulates.</p><p>The compensation is simple: put the lost half back at the start, that is double the variance:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">σ² = 2 / fan_in</p><p>This is <b>He initialisation</b>, and in terms of standard deviation it is exactly <b>√2 times</b> Xavier. A factor that looks negligible in one layer turns into a difference on the order of 2^10 over twenty layers.</p><p>One thing to watch: the correct scale <b>depends on fan_in</b>. Giving every layer a fixed standard deviation becomes wrong the moment the layer width changes.</p>',
  'ilkleme:3': '<p>This step corrects an important misconception: <b>not exploding does not mean being healthy.</b></p><p>The output of tanh is by definition between −1 and 1. However large a scale you give it, the signal <b>cannot explode</b> in the forward pass; the function clips it. Looking at the forward numbers, everything seems fine.</p><p>The price is paid in the backward pass.</p><p>When tanh saturates, that is when its output approaches ±1, <b>its derivative goes to zero</b>. Because backpropagation multiplies the gradient by that derivative at every layer, a gradient passing through saturated units decays layer after layer. Over a twenty-layer product that means the gradient vanishes in practice.</p><p>So the same wrong scale shows two different faces with two activations:</p><p><b>ReLU:</b> explodes noisily. Numbers overflow, the loss becomes NaN. The failure is visible.</p><p><b>tanh:</b> saturates silently. The numbers look reasonable, the loss does not fall, and it is not obvious why.</p><p>The outcome is the same: <b>the gradient does not reach its target.</b> So you cannot judge an initialisation by whether the forward numbers "look reasonable"; you have to look at the saturation rate and at the gradient itself.</p>',
  'ilkleme:4': '<p>Everything measured so far belonged to the network\'s <b>initial moment</b>; not a single training step had been taken. This step tests how much difference those measurements make in real training.</p><p>The setup is controlled: the same network, the same data, the same learning rate, the same number of steps. <b>The only thing that changes is the initial scale.</b></p><p>Four behaviours are expected:</p><p><b>Very small scale:</b> because the signal died in the forward pass, the gradient comes back as zero. The loss does not move. The network is not broken, it simply <b>never learns</b>. The code runs without error, which makes this hard to diagnose.</p><p><b>Xavier (slightly small for ReLU):</b> something happens, but slowly. The √2 lost at each layer accumulates with depth.</p><p><b>He:</b> this is where the real difference is. A markedly lower loss on the same budget.</p><p><b>Very large scale:</b> activations and gradients overflow, the loss becomes NaN and there is no coming back.</p><p>The lesson to take: <b>initialisation is not a hyperparameter but a matter of correctness.</b> You cannot compensate for it by searching over the learning rate, because the problem is not in the size of the step but in the gradient that computes the step.</p><p>In practice modern libraries apply the correct initialisation by default according to layer type. The point of this lesson is to show <b>why</b> that default is what it is, and what you are risking when you change it by hand.</p>',

  'patlayan:1': '<p>When people talk about the gradient they usually mean a single number: "the gradient norm". This step breaks that habit.</p><p>The gradient depends on the <b>batch</b> it was computed on. Different batches give different gradients. So the gradient norm is not a number but a <b>distribution</b>.</p><p>In recurrent structures that distribution is especially skewed. The reason is structural: the same weight matrix is applied <b>over and over</b> across time steps, so the gradient contains repeated products of the same matrix. The exponential nature of products gives the distribution a <b>heavy tail</b>: most batches are entirely ordinary, but a small minority are orders of magnitude above the median.</p><p>The practical consequence: training may be going perfectly well on average and <b>a single batch</b> can arrive and push the weights into an unusable state. Anyone watching the mean gradient norm would never see it coming.</p><p>The choice of activation affects the tail directly. Because tanh saturates, its derivative is bounded by a ceiling, so it <b>cuts the tail by itself</b>. ReLU imposes no bound on the positive side; it passes both signal and gradient through as they are, so the tail stays open.</p><p>That is why "is this training stable" is answered with <b>extreme percentiles</b>, not with the mean.</p>',
  'patlayan:2': '<p>If spiky gradients are breaking training, the first fix that comes to mind is to shorten the step: lower the learning rate.</p><p>It works, but it is the wrong tool for two reasons.</p><p><b>1 · It makes everyone pay.</b> The problem is in a small minority of batches. When you lower the learning rate <b>every</b> batch slows down. So the healthy 99% is punished for the problematic 1% and training takes needlessly long.</p><p><b>2 · You have to go below the stability limit.</b> If the learning rate is not small enough, a single spiky step throws the weights into a high-loss region and there is no guarantee of returning from it.</p><p>The real measurement in this step shows something subtler: <b>unstable training does not merely give a bad result, it gives a non-reproducible one.</b></p><p>The experiment: one of the initial weights is nudged by an <b>imperceptibly small</b> amount and the same training is run again. In the stable setting the final loss barely changes. In the unstable setting the result ends up somewhere entirely different.</p><p>That is the definition of chaotic dynamics: <b>sensitive dependence on initial conditions.</b> And in practice it means: the result you saw in that run is a property neither of the model nor of the settings, it is the outcome of a single accident. Run the same code again and you see a different number.</p><p>The right tool has to target the <b>tail</b>, not the step.</p>',
  'patlayan:3': '<p><b>Gradient clipping</b> targets exactly the tail and consists of a single rule:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">if ‖g‖ &gt; τ then  g ← g · τ / ‖g‖</p><p>That is, if the norm of the gradient exceeds a threshold, the gradient vector is <b>rescaled</b> so its norm lands exactly on that threshold.</p><p>Two properties to notice:</p><p><b>The direction is preserved.</b> Because the vector is multiplied by a scalar, its direction does not change. The model still goes the right way, it just covers a shorter distance on that step. (Value clipping, which clips each component separately, does not do this and distorts the direction too; norm clipping is preferred.)</p><p><b>Steps below the threshold are untouched.</b> Typical batches keep taking their full step. That is the difference from lowering the learning rate: the penalty applies only to the problematic minority.</p><p>Two gains are expected:</p><p><b>Better loss:</b> you get the speed of a high learning rate while being protected from the damage of exploding steps.</p><p><b>Reproducibility:</b> this is the one that really matters. Because the giant steps that feed the chaotic behaviour are cut, the same run starts giving the same result. For a result to be scientifically usable it first has to be reproducible.</p><p>That is why clipping is an all but standard component of RNN and Transformer training.</p>',
  'patlayan:4': '<p>Clipping is not free and does not always help.</p><p>Every time the rule fires it erases the gradient\'s <b>magnitude information</b>. Gradient magnitude is a meaningful signal: if you are in a steep region you should take a large step. When clipping trims that information the model cannot take the step it should.</p><p>If there is a real explosion the trade pays off: you lose the information of a few steps and save the training. But <b>if there is no explosion</b> you only lose.</p><p>The measurement in this step sets up exactly that case: in a setting where stability was already achieved, the same threshold <b>does harm</b>. Because the threshold is lower than it needs to be, it touches a non-negligible share of the steps and slows the model down every time it does.</p><p>Two practical rules follow:</p><p><b>1 · The threshold is chosen by measuring.</b> The right method is to look at the distribution of gradient norms over a few hundred batches and put the threshold where the tail begins. That is, typical steps should pass untouched and only the extremes should be cut.</p><p><b>2 · Clipping treats the symptom, not the cause.</b> What produces the heavy tail is the exponential nature of repeated multiplication. There are structural fixes that actually reduce it: <b>residual (shortcut) connections</b> that open an additive path for the gradient, <b>gates</b> that learn how much information to carry (LSTM/GRU), and normalisation layers. Clipping goes alongside those, not instead of them.</p>',
  'kisayol:1': '<p>There is a common assumption about deep networks: the deeper it gets the more powerful the model, but the greater the risk of <b>overfitting</b>. This lesson shows that assumption is incomplete.</p><p>What is measured here is the <b>training loss</b>, not the test loss. That distinction is critical: overfitting means the model fits the training data <i>too well</i>. If a model cannot even fit its own training data, there is no generalisation problem; there is an <b>optimisation problem</b>.</p><p>And that is exactly what is observed: with the same data, the same initialisation and the same budget, the training loss gets <b>worse</b> as layers are added.</p><p>The reason this looks illogical: a deep network is mathematically <b>more powerful</b> than a shallow one. If it set all the extra layers to the identity function (the function that passes its input through unchanged) it would do exactly the same job as the shallow network. So the set of solutions a deep network can reach <b>contains</b> the shallow network\'s.</p><p>So the problem is not what the model can do but <b>whether training can get there.</b> Gradient descent cannot find that reachable solution.</p><p>This is exactly the <b>degradation problem</b> He and colleagues pointed to in the 2015 ResNet paper. And the fix is in the same paper: stop making the identity function something the network has to learn and <b>put it into the architecture</b>.</p>',
  'kisayol:2': '<p>The change is one line. Where a layer was:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">h ← F(h)</p><p>it is written as:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">h ← h + F(h)</p><p>The difference looks small but it changes the layer\'s <b>job</b>. The layer no longer produces a new representation replacing the input; it produces a <b>correction</b> to be added to the input. That is why these blocks are called <b>residual</b>: what is learned is the residual between the identity and the target.</p><p>The gain comes from this: it is now easy for a layer to "do nothing". Pushing F towards zero is enough; the weight matrix does not have to be tuned to exactly the identity. So <b>unnecessary layers can switch themselves off cheaply</b> and depth stops being a burden.</p><p>What the measurement should show is the relationship between depth and loss being <b>reversed</b>: the loss that got worse with depth in the plain network gets better with depth in the residual one.</p><p>But an honest note is needed: <b>a shortcut gains you nothing in a shallow network</b> and may even leave it slightly behind. A shortcut pays the price of depth; with no depth there is no price to pay. This is the general rule of architectural decisions: every technique solves a problem, and if that problem is absent it only adds overhead.</p>',
  'kisayol:3': '<p>To see why the shortcut works you have to look at backpropagation.</p><p>In a plain layer, as the gradient travels backwards it is multiplied at every layer by that layer\'s Jacobian (its derivative matrix). Thirty-two layers, thirty-two multiplications. If the average magnitude of those multiplications is below 1 the gradient decays exponentially; if above, it explodes. Both are a <b>multiplicative</b> fate.</p><p>In a residual layer the derivative takes the form:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">I + J</p><p>Here <b>I is the identity matrix</b> and it comes precisely from writing h + F(h). The consequence: a copy of the gradient passes backwards <b>multiplied by nothing</b>. Whatever the layer does, however broken F is, that copy reaches the first layer intact.</p><p>So a shortcut opens an <b>additive motorway</b> for the gradient. The layers still use the multiplicative path, but it is no longer the only path.</p><p>What the measurement should show: in the plain network the gradient norm at the first layer <b>shrinks</b> as depth grows; in the residual network the same change in depth <b>grows</b> it. The same change, opposite directions.</p><p>The importance of this idea is not limited to ResNet. Every sublayer of a Transformer is wrapped the same way; it is one of the reasons hundred-layer language models can be trained at all.</p>',
  'kisayol:4': '<p>Writing a shortcut is not enough by itself, and the reason is the same calculation as in the initialisation lesson.</p><p>When you write h + F(h) the variance of the output becomes the <b>sum</b> of the input\'s variance and F\'s variance. So every layer grows the variance a little. And that growth accumulates multiplicatively: after thirty-two layers the signal can explode <b>before a single training step is taken</b>.</p><p>So while the shortcut solves a gradient problem it creates a forward-pass problem. The fix is to <b>start the network close to the identity</b>. There are three standard ways and all three have the same purpose:</p><p><b>1 · Branch damping:</b> use a small coefficient, h + α·F(h). At the start the block behaves almost like the identity.</p><p><b>2 · Zero-initialise the last layer:</b> set the final weight inside the block to zero and F produces exactly zero at the start, so the block is exactly the identity. As training proceeds it grows its own contribution.</p><p><b>3 · Normalisation:</b> put layer norm/batch norm inside the block to stop the variance accumulating layer by layer. The <b>pre-norm</b> arrangement in modern Transformers does this and is markedly more stable than post-norm in deep models.</p><p>The shared idea in one sentence: <b>a deep network should start training like a shallow one</b> and learn to use its depth over time.</p>',

  'havuzlama:1': '<p>A convolutional layer produces a feature map the same size as the image. If every layer has to carry that many values the computation is both expensive and wasteful: values at neighbouring positions are very similar, so the information is heavily repeated.</p><p><b>Pooling</b> is a simple summary rule: divide the map into k×k windows and take <b>a single number</b> from each window.</p><p><b>Max pooling</b> takes the largest value in the window, answering "how strong was the trace of the pattern I am looking for in this region".</p><p><b>Average pooling</b> takes the window\'s mean, preserving overall intensity.</p><p>The reduction in size is always <b>the area of the window</b>: a 2×2 window cuts the number of values to a quarter, a 4×4 window to a sixteenth.</p><p>The distinguishing property of pooling is that it is <b>parameter-free</b>. It contains no weights to learn; it is a fixed rule. So it shrinks the computation without growing the model and adds nothing to the overfitting risk.</p><p>But what it really contributes is not size reduction; you will measure that in the next step.</p>',
  'havuzlama:2': '<p>The real justification for pooling is <b>resistance to shifts</b>.</p><p>The idea: max pooling asks "what is the largest value in this window". If the image shifts by one pixel and the largest value <b>stays inside the same window</b>, the output does not change at all. So pooling <b>does not care</b> about the exact position of the pattern within the window.</p><p>This is measurable: shift the image by one pixel, recompute the representation and look at the relative difference between the two. 0 means "did not change at all", 1 means "changed completely".</p><p>The expectation: without pooling even a one-pixel shift changes the representation significantly, because edge maps are sparse and the activations move. As the window grows the change shrinks.</p><p>You are also testing a common claim: "max pooling is always more invariant than average". That is <b>not true</b>. Which is more stable depends on the window size and the structure of the data; neither has a general advantage on its own. Not accepting rules like this without measuring them is the general stance of this course.</p>',
  'havuzlama:3': '<p>The resistance has a <b>limit</b>, and that limit is lower than intuition would guess.</p><p>You would expect a k×k window to withstand shifts of up to k pixels. The reality is worse than that.</p><p>The reason: pooling windows sit on the image via a <b>fixed grid</b>. The windows do not move with the shift. When a feature moves, what matters is not where it is inside the window but <b>which window it falls into</b>. The moment the feature crosses into the neighbouring window the invariance ends, and that can happen well before the shift reaches the window size.</p><p>So what pooling provides is not full invariance but a <b>partial, grid-dependent</b> resistance. It works for small shifts and breaks down quickly for medium ones.</p><p>This is a known weakness of convolutional networks: true translation invariance does not come from the architecture. That is why <b>data augmentation</b> (random cropping and shifting) is still necessary in practice; the model learns part of its robustness from the data rather than the architecture.</p>',
  'havuzlama:4': '<p>If growing the window increases resistance, why not take the largest one?</p><p>Because what is gained and what is lost are <b>the same thing</b>.</p><p>Pooling gains resistance by not caring about position within the window. The larger the window, the larger the share of positional information that is erased. With a large enough window the representation says nothing about <b>where</b> in the image the object is: the same object at different positions becomes two indistinguishable representations.</p><p><b>Shift invariance and positional sensitivity are two ends of the same trade-off.</b> Increasing one decreases the other, and that is not an implementation flaw but the definition itself.</p><p>Which one you want depends on the task:</p><p><b>Classification:</b> for "is there a cat in this image" position is irrelevant. Invariance is a gain.</p><p><b>Detection and segmentation:</b> for "<i>where</i> is the cat" position is exactly the answer. Invariance is a loss.</p><p>That is why modern architectures moved away from aggressive pooling. Downsampling has largely been handed to <b>strided convolution</b>: it also shrinks the map, but it <b>learns</b> how to summarise instead of following a fixed rule. Segmentation architectures use skip connections from early layers to recover the resolution that was lost.</p>',

  'rnn:1': '<p>Every network so far expected a <b>fixed-size</b> input. But a sentence can be 5 words or 50; a time series can be 100 measurements or 10,000. How do you feed that to a fixed-input network?</p><p><b>The RNN answer:</b> do not read the sequence all at once, read it step by step. At each step take the next input and update the <b>state</b> you hold:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">h ← tanh(W h + U x)</p><p>Here h is the summary of everything read so far; that is what we call "memory".</p><p>The critical point: <b>W is the same matrix at every step.</b> We are not adding new layers, we are applying the same layer over and over. This is <b>parameter sharing</b>, the time-axis counterpart of the idea in convolution.</p><p>It has two consequences:</p><p><b>1 · The parameter count is independent of sequence length.</b> The same model processes a sequence of 2 steps or 2000.</p><p><b>2 · The entire past is compressed into a single fixed-size vector.</b> However long the sequence gets, h does not grow.</p><p>The second is the source of both the power and the limit: you have to fit an unbounded past into a fixed-size container. In the next step you will measure where that limit lies.</p>',
  'rnn:2': '<p>It is easy to say "it has memory"; here we measure it.</p><p>The task is deliberately plain: T random numbers are shown and at the end <b>the first one</b> is asked for. Everything in between is a distractor. The model has to do one thing: carry the first number all the way to the end.</p><p>The metric is the <b>explained ratio</b>: 1 means perfect, 0 means "the same as always saying the mean", a negative value means worse than saying the mean. And the measurement is made on a <b>separate test set</b>; memorising the training set is very easy in this task, so training success proves nothing.</p><p>The expected behaviour: nearly perfect on short sequences, a fast collapse as the sequence lengthens, and dropping below zero past a certain point.</p><p>At the moment of collapse, the thing to look at is this separation: <b>low training loss but high test loss.</b> That shows not that the model lacks capacity but that the information it must carry <b>cannot reach</b> the end. The model tries to cope with a task it cannot solve by memorising.</p><p>So the problem is not the kind that a "bigger model" fixes. The reason is in the next step.</p>',
  'rnn:3': '<p>Now we set training aside and look at <b>the network itself</b>. The question: how sensitive is the output to the input t steps earlier? That is, if I nudged that input, how much would the output change?</p><p>This is a derivative and can be computed directly; it <b>requires no training</b>.</p><p>The expected result is nearly <b>a straight line</b> on a logarithmic scale. A straight line means the decay is <b>exponential</b>: at every step backwards the sensitivity is multiplied by a fixed ratio.</p><p>The reason is the chain rule. Going t steps back, the gradient contains t multiplications of the same matrix (and of the tanh derivative). If that ratio is below 1, the result goes to zero exponentially as t grows.</p><p>Given a ratio you can work out the horizon in your head: if the ratio is r, sensitivity halves in log(0.5)/log(r) steps and falls to one per cent in log(0.01)/log(r) steps. So <b>the horizon is a direct consequence of the ratio.</b></p><p>The most important point: this measurement is made on an <b>untrained</b> network. So the decay is not the result of bad training, a wrong learning rate or insufficient data; it is a property of <b>the structure itself</b>. Training longer does not fix it.</p>',
  'rnn:4': '<p>The source of the decay is clear: there is a <b>multiplication</b> at every step and most of the factors are below 1. So the direction of the fix is clear too: open <b>a path the information can travel without being multiplied</b>.</p><p>That sentence should sound familiar. You measured exactly this in the shortcut connections lesson: writing h + F(h) puts the derivative into the form I + J and a copy of the gradient passes through unmultiplied. There it was done along <b>depth</b>; here it has to be done along <b>time</b>.</p><p>There are two answers:</p><p><b>LSTM and GRU:</b> add a <b>cell</b> alongside the state and multiply it not by a matrix but by a <b>gate</b> between 0 and 1. When the gate is near 1 the cell is carried through almost unchanged, that is the per-step decay ratio approaches 1. The network decides for itself how much information to carry. The next lesson measures this.</p><p><b>Attention:</b> a more radical fix. Instead of carrying information step by step, every step <b>looks directly at every past step</b>. Whatever the distance, the path length is 1; there is no chain of multiplications to decay.</p><p>And the price is right there: because every step looks at every step, the cost grows with the <b>square</b> of the sequence length. The whole context-length debate in modern language models originates in exactly this trade.</p>',

  'lstm:1': '<p>In an RNN the state was <b>overwritten</b> at every step: h ← tanh(W h + U x). Anything surviving from the old state depended on surviving multiplication by W, and that multiplication usually shrank it.</p><p>LSTM adds a second path. It places a <b>cell</b> alongside the state and updates it like this:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">c ← f · c + i · g</p><p>Here <b>f is the forget gate</b>, a number between 0 and 1 (it comes from a sigmoid). Note: the cell is <b>not multiplied by a matrix</b>, only by this scalar gate.</p><p>The consequence: if f were 1 the cell would be carried through unchanged; the per-step decay ratio would be exactly 1. This is the time-axis counterpart of the <b>additive path</b> idea from shortcut connections.</p><p>The gates are learned, so the network decides for itself what to keep and how much. The other two gates: <b>i (input)</b> sets how much of the new information is written, <b>o (output)</b> sets how much of the cell is exposed.</p><p>An important practical detail: the forget gate\'s <b>bias</b> is initialised positive. That way the gate opens near 1 at the start of training, the cell begins carrying information from the outset and the model gets a chance to learn long dependencies. If the bias starts at zero the gate is half closed and the decay returns on the very first step.</p>',
  'lstm:2': '<p>Now <b>exactly the same</b> measurement as in the RNN lesson is made: the sensitivity of the output to the input t steps earlier, on an untrained network, averaged over many sequences. Same task, same settings; the only thing that changes is the architecture.</p><p>That is what makes the comparison fair: because the measurement is independent of training, an explanation like "the LSTM was trained better" is ruled out. What is measured is a property of the <b>structure</b> directly.</p><p>When reading the plot, look at the <b>slope</b> of the curve, not its height. On a logarithmic axis:</p><p><b>A straight line going down</b> = exponential decay. The per-step ratio is below 1.</p><p><b>A flattening line</b> = the decay has stopped. The per-step ratio has approached 1, that is past information is not lost with distance.</p><p>The expectation: the RNN curve descends steadily, while the LSTM curve flattens as the forget gate\'s bias increases. And the gap grows by <b>orders of magnitude</b> at distant steps, because the effect is exponential.</p><p>The real lesson here: the forget gate\'s bias is not a minor tuning knob but a parameter that <b>sets the network\'s memory horizon</b>.</p>',
  'lstm:3': '<p>A sensitivity measurement describes the structure, but leaves one question open: how much does this difference <b>move on a real task</b>?</p><p>The same task as in the RNN lesson is run: show T random numbers, ask for the first at the end. Same data, same budget, same learning rate.</p><p>Two results are expected, and both are instructive:</p><p><b>On long sequences the LSTM wins.</b> At the point where you measured the RNN\'s horizon ending, the RNN drops below zero while the LSTM holds a meaningful score. The difference in decay translates directly to the task.</p><p><b>On short sequences the RNN can win.</b> And that should not be ignored. The reason is simple: the LSTM\'s four gates mean four weight matrices, roughly <b>four times the parameters</b> at the same hidden size. On short sequences, where decay is not a problem, the LSTM\'s extra capacity is not a gain but <b>slower learning on the same budget</b>.</p><p>The general rule follows: <b>gates are not free.</b> With no long dependency there is no problem for the LSTM to solve, and only its cost remains. This is the same conclusion as in the shortcut connections lesson: every mechanism pays the price of a specific problem, and with that problem absent it is only overhead.</p>',
  'lstm:4': '<p>LSTM largely solves the decay but has <b>three costs</b>, and all three appeared in this lesson.</p><p><b>1 · Parameters.</b> Four gates, four weight matrices: roughly four times the parameters at the same hidden size. On short sequences that is a direct disadvantage.</p><p><b>2 · Sequentiality.</b> This is the heaviest one. Because the cell is updated step by step, an LSTM <b>cannot process a sequence in parallel</b>: a 1000-step sequence means 1000 sequential steps. GPUs accelerate parallel work; a sequential dependency is exactly what cannot be parallelised. So an LSTM cannot use the power of the hardware.</p><p><b>3 · The path is still long.</b> Even with the gate fully open, information still has to be <b>carried</b> across t steps. The decay slows down but the distance does not shrink.</p><p><b>Attention</b> solves all three at once: every step looks directly at every past step, so the path length between any two positions is <b>1</b>; and because all those lookups are independent of each other, the computation <b>parallelises</b>.</p><p>The price paid in return: because every step looks at every step, the cost grows with the <b>square</b> of the sequence length. In an LSTM the cost was linear in length.</p><p>The whole of the next route is built on this trade.</p>',
  'otokodlayici:1': '<p>Every model so far had a <b>label</b>: the right answer. But labelled data is expensive and unlabelled data is plentiful. Can anything be learned from unlabelled data?</p><p>The autoencoder\'s answer is surprisingly simple: <b>let the network\'s target be its own input.</b> Ask the output to equal the input.</p><p>Put that way it sounds pointless; what does copying the input teach? That is exactly why a <b>bottleneck</b> is placed in the middle. The data is first compressed into a <b>code</b> of lower dimension than the input, then expanded back from that code.</p><p>The bottleneck makes copying <b>impossible</b>. Because the network cannot carry all the information, it is forced to decide what to keep and what to discard. And that decision answers the question of which part of the information carries the structure of the data.</p><p>There are two parts: the <b>encoder</b> (input → code) and the <b>decoder</b> (code → reconstruction). The loss is usually squared error: how different the reconstruction is from the original.</p><p>To interpret the error you need a baseline: the <b>total variance</b> of the data. If the bottleneck had zero dimensions, that is if no information passed at all, the best the network could do would be to output the mean for every example, and the error would come out exactly at the total variance. The measured error is read against that number.</p>',
  'otokodlayici:2': '<p>This step shows a surprising equivalence.</p><p>Remove all the non-linear layers from the autoencoder. Two matrices remain: one that compresses, one that expands. The loss is still squared error.</p><p>Trained with gradient descent, this network finds <b>the same subspace as PCA</b>. So a linear autoencoder trained with squared error does nothing different from principal component analysis.</p><p>That is not a coincidence. The defining property of PCA is exactly this: <b>the linear subspace of a given dimension that minimises squared reconstruction error.</b> And that is exactly what the linear autoencoder is minimising. Two different routes to the same problem.</p><p>The way the two are computed is entirely different: PCA is solved directly from the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix; the autoencoder converges iteratively by gradient descent. That the results coincide is therefore a good <b>verification</b>.</p><p>A small distinction: the linear autoencoder finds the same <b>subspace</b> but not the ordered, orthogonal axes PCA gives. The basis it finds is a rotated version; equivalent for reconstruction, not for interpretation.</p><p>The conclusion matters: the autoencoder\'s advantage <b>does not come from "being a neural network".</b> Wherever the gain comes from, it must be where we drop linearity.</p>',
  'otokodlayici:3': '<p>Now a <b>non-linear</b> layer is added to the encoder and the decoder. Same bottleneck, same data, same budget.</p><p>To see where the gain comes from you have to look at how the data was generated: it comes from a few hidden parameters, but through a <b>curved transformation</b>. So the points do not sit on a flat plane in the high-dimensional space but on a <b>bent surface</b>.</p><p>PCA can only find <b>flat</b> subspaces. Forced to represent a curved surface with a flat plane, some error is unavoidable.</p><p>A non-linear autoencoder can <b>follow</b> the surface. This is the core idea of manifold learning: high-dimensional data usually lives on a low-dimensional, curved manifold.</p><p>One more expected behaviour: <b>the gain shrinks as the bottleneck widens.</b> The reason is simple. Give it enough dimensions and a flat subspace approximates the curved surface well too; the curvature stops being an obstacle. The value of non-linearity shows up <b>when the compression is tight</b>.</p>',
  'otokodlayici:4': '<p>So far we have been trying to lower the error. So how would we get the lowest error of all?</p><p>By opening the bottleneck to <b>the same size as the input</b>. Then the network can learn the identity function and the error goes to zero.</p><p>And what it has learned is <b>nothing</b>. A representation that passes the input through unchanged is the input. With no compression there is no decision; with no decision nothing has been learned.</p><p>The lesson here is general and shows up everywhere in machine learning: <b>reconstruction error is not a goal but a pressure.</b> What is valuable is not that the error is low but that it is low <b>under a constraint</b>. Remove the constraint and the metric loses its meaning.</p><p>That is why every autoencoder variant in practice adds a constraint to close off the easy solution:</p><p><b>Denoising:</b> noise is added to the input but the target is the clean input. Copying is no longer enough; the network has to know the structure of the data.</p><p><b>Sparse:</b> the bottleneck may be wide but only a few units are allowed to be active at once.</p><p><b>Variational (VAE):</b> a probabilistic structure and a regularisation term are added to the code, so the code space becomes both regular and sampleable.</p><p>All three have the same purpose: <b>to make the network\'s job harder.</b></p>',

  'hesap-cizge:1': '<p>You have seen backpropagation as a formula; now you will see <b>how it is implemented</b>.</p><p>A computer does not see a mathematical expression as one piece. It makes every elementary operation (multiply, add, sine, exponent) a <b>node</b> and draws arrows between them showing the flow of data. The structure that results is a <b>computation graph</b>.</p><p>This graph is not merely a visualisation aid; libraries like PyTorch and JAX genuinely build it while the code runs and compute the derivative over it.</p><p>There is one structure in the graph to watch for: <b>a node can have more than one outgoing arrow.</b> That is, the same intermediate value can be used in several places in the expression.</p><p>The counterpart in calculus is the multivariable chain rule: <b>if there is a dependency on a variable along several paths, the contributions from those paths are summed.</b> In backpropagation this is implemented as "gradients arriving at a node are added together".</p><p>The detail looks minor but it is exactly the most common bug in hand-written backpropagation code: counting only one of a shared node\'s contributions.</p>',
  'hesap-cizge:2': '<p>Backpropagation walks the graph <b>from the end to the start</b>. The final node is given a derivative of 1 (the derivative of f with respect to itself), then each node passes back the share falling on its own inputs using its local derivative rule.</p><p>This is called <b>automatic differentiation</b>, and it should not be confused with two other things:</p><p><b>It is not symbolic differentiation.</b> There is no formula growing anywhere; it works on numbers.</p><p><b>It is not numerical differentiation.</b> That is, it makes no approximation like (f(x+h) − f(x)) / h.</p><p>Because automatic differentiation applies the chain rule exactly, it is <b>exact</b>: the result matches the value from a hand-derived formula to floating-point precision.</p><p>In this step three routes are compared: automatic differentiation, a hand-derived closed-form formula and a numerical derivative by finite differences.</p><p>The expected result: the difference between automatic differentiation and the hand-derived formula is <b>exactly zero</b>; the difference against the numerical derivative is not zero, because finite differences carry their own error. As h shrinks the truncation error falls but the <b>rounding error</b> grows; so a numerical derivative has a precision ceiling it cannot beat.</p><p>So the three-way comparison here uses the numerical derivative not as a referee but as an <b>independent check</b>.</p>',
  'hesap-cizge:3': '<p>A derivative can be carried in two directions through a graph, and which you choose sets the cost.</p><p><b>Forward mode:</b> goes from start to end. Each pass carries the derivative along <b>a single input</b> direction. With P parameters you need P passes.</p><p><b>Reverse mode:</b> goes from end to start. Each pass carries the derivative along <b>a single output</b> direction. With M outputs you need M passes.</p><p>The rule follows: <b>forward mode wins when there are few inputs, reverse mode when there are few outputs.</b></p><p>In neural network training the situation is extreme: the number of inputs (parameters) is in the millions and the number of outputs (the loss) is <b>one</b>. So reverse mode gives the gradient of every parameter in a single backward pass. Doing the same work in forward mode would take as many passes as there are parameters.</p><p>That is why what deep learning uses is called <b>reverse-mode automatic differentiation</b>; "backpropagation" is its neural-network-specific name.</p><p>This does not make forward mode useless: when the number of inputs is small (in the sensitivity analysis of a physical model with a few parameters, say) forward mode is cheaper. The right choice is made by looking at the <b>ratio</b> of inputs to outputs.</p>',
  'hesap-cizge:4': '<p>Reverse mode makes the computation cheap but the price is <b>memory</b>.</p><p>The reason: the backward pass needs the <b>intermediate values</b> computed in the forward pass. The derivative of a product needs the value of both factors; the derivative of an activation needs that layer\'s output.</p><p>So every intermediate value from the forward pass has to be <b>kept</b> until the backward pass arrives. Forward mode does not have this problem, because it carries the derivative along with the forward pass and never has to hold the past.</p><p>The consequence: memory grows linearly with the network\'s <b>depth</b> and with the <b>batch size</b>. In large models the most common cause of an out-of-memory error is not the parameters themselves but the stored intermediate activations.</p><p>The standard fix is <b>gradient checkpointing</b>: instead of storing every intermediate value store only some of them, and <b>recompute</b> the ones in between when the backward pass needs them.</p><p>Placing checkpoints at intervals of √L brings memory from O(L) down to O(√L); in return the forward pass is roughly done once more, so the total computation rises by about 1.3×.</p><p>This is a clean example of the <b>compute-versus-memory trade</b> that recurs throughout engineering, and it is the standard tool for fitting large models onto the hardware you have.</p>',

  'mdn:1': '<p>Every regression model so far carried an implicit assumption: each input has <b>one</b> correct answer.</p><p>That assumption breaks very often.</p><p>There are <b>several joint angles</b> that bring a robot arm\'s tip to a given point. There may be several physical states explaining one measurement. A sentence has several valid continuations. These are called <b>inverse problems</b>, and the multiplicity is not noise but the <b>structure</b> of the problem.</p><p>The data in this step is built exactly that way: for every x there are two valid answers and both are equally correct.</p><p>Now ask the critical question: what is the <b>average</b> of those two answers? Because the branches are symmetric the average is right in the middle, that is where <b>none</b> of the valid answers are.</p><p>And this is not a quirk of one example. <b>The mean of a distribution need not be a typical member of it.</b> In any bimodal distribution the mean falls into the gap between the modes.</p><p>That observation is what the whole of the next two steps rests on.</p>',
  'mdn:2': '<p>An ordinary regression network is trained on this data: input x, output a single number, loss squared error.</p><p>The result is the empty region right between the two branches. The model never comes close to a valid answer at any x.</p><p>But <b>the model is not making a mistake.</b> On the contrary, it is doing perfectly what was asked of it.</p><p>The reason lies in the definition of squared error. The prediction that minimises squared-error loss is mathematically the <b>conditional mean</b> E[y | x]. That is not a tendency but a provable result. The model finds exactly the conditional mean; because in a two-branch distribution that mean is in the gap, the prediction is in the gap too.</p><p>This shows one of the most instructive classes of error in machine learning: <b>you get whatever the loss function asks for.</b> Ask for squared error and you get the mean. Ask for absolute error and you get the <b>median</b> (which, on symmetric two-branch data, is also in the gap).</p><p>So the model is not flawed; <b>the question is flawed.</b> Asking for a single number on a problem with several answers makes the answer impossible from the start.</p><p>The right question is: predict not a single number but <b>a distribution</b>.</p>',
  'mdn:3': '<p>This step abandons training entirely and looks straight at the probabilities. Every number here is computed <b>in closed form</b>, so none of it depends on a training accident. That matters: it makes it impossible to explain the result away as "the model was not trained well".</p><p>What is compared: the true conditional density (bimodal) against the <b>best single Gaussian</b> that can be fitted to it.</p><p>"Best" has a precise meaning here: the Gaussian with the same mean and the same variance is the best member of the unimodal family in likelihood terms. So this is <b>the fairest chance</b> that can be given to a single Gaussian.</p><p>It still does not suffice. A unimodal distribution by definition has one peak; it cannot represent a bimodal one. By growing its variance it can <b>cover</b> both branches, but that means also assigning high probability to the empty region between them. The model declares values that are never observed to be likely.</p><p>How much is lost can be reduced to one number: the <b>KL divergence</b> between the two distributions gives how many <b>nats</b> are lost per observation. Exponentiate it and it becomes a likelihood ratio directly: how many times more likely the mixture model finds the data.</p><p>And the point to underline: this loss <b>cannot be closed by training</b>. When the model family is wrong, more data, more parameters or longer training do not help.</p>',
  'mdn:4': '<p>A <b>mixture density network (MDN)</b> solves this problem: instead of a single number it predicts the <b>parameters of a mixture distribution</b>. A mean, a standard deviation and a weight for each component. So the model can say "there are two answers and these are their probabilities".</p><p>But mixture models have a difficulty of their own, and this step measures exactly that.</p><p>The problem: <b>the components are unlabelled.</b> The first component carrying the upper branch and the second the lower gives <b>exactly the same</b> distribution as the reverse. So the parameters differ and the model is identical.</p><p>This is called the <b>identifiability problem</b>: there are several parameter sets giving the same value of the likelihood function. With k components there are k! equivalent solutions arising from ordering alone.</p><p>The practical consequence: an <b>imperceptibly small</b> change in the initial weights can swap the learned component means. The learned <b>distribution</b> is correct and reproducible; the <b>individual component parameters</b> are not.</p><p>Three practical rules follow:</p><p><b>1 · Do not report component parameters on their own.</b> Evaluation should go through a quantity independent of component order: the held-out likelihood or the predicted density itself.</p><p><b>2 · Put a floor on σ.</b> Otherwise a component can collapse onto a single point, its variance goes to zero and the likelihood diverges.</p><p><b>3 · Give more components than needed and train from multiple starts.</b> The mixture likelihood is multimodal; a single run can settle in a local optimum.</p>',
  'bayes-ag:1': '<p>So far a network\'s weights have been <b>a single</b> set of numbers: training is over, these are the weights. The Bayesian view questions that certainty.</p><p>The idea: there are <b>many</b> weight sets consistent with the data. They all explain the training data about equally well but say different things away from the data they saw. So the weights should be thought of not as a single point but as a <b>distribution</b>.</p><p>Computing that distribution exactly is impractical in a network with millions of parameters. The simplest and most useful approximation is an <b>ensemble</b>: train several networks on the same data from different random starts and look at the differences between them.</p><p>The expected behaviour: <b>where there is data the members have to agree</b>, because the data pulls them to the same place. The moment the data ends the constraint is lifted and the members start to diverge.</p><p>So the <b>spread</b> among members is a cheap proxy for the question "how much information do I have here". A small spread means you are near the data, a large one means you are outside it.</p><p>This is called <b>epistemic uncertainty</b>: uncertainty from a lack of data, reducible with more data. It differs from <b>aleatoric</b> uncertainty, which comes from the noise in the data itself and does not shrink however much you collect.</p>',
  'bayes-ag:2': '<p>Uncertainty opening up when the data ends is a good sign. But the real question is harder: <b>does the band that opens cover the truth?</b></p><p>The concept that measures this is <b>calibration</b>. When you call an uncertainty estimate "±2 standard deviations", you expect the band you give to contain the true value about <b>95%</b> of the time. That comes from the definition of the normal distribution.</p><p>The measurement tests this directly: it looks at the fraction of points where the band contains the true function.</p><p>If coverage is markedly below 95% the model is <b>overconfident</b>: it gives a narrower band than it should. That is the error in the wrong direction for safety; the system claims to know where it does not.</p><p>Comparing the result against a reference is also instructive. The Gaussian Process lesson made a measurement of the same kind: the GP\'s uncertainty was also insufficient but <b>less</b> so. The reason is structural. A GP\'s uncertainty follows <b>analytically</b> from the kernel function; an ensemble\'s comes from the empirical spread of a finite number of members, and nothing makes that spread match the true posterior spread.</p><p>In short: <b>producing an uncertainty number is easy, producing a correct one is not.</b></p>',
  'bayes-ag:3': '<p>It would be comforting to think the problem lives only outside the data. This step shows it does not.</p><p>Coverage can fall short of expectation inside the data too. And in a more troubling way: even at a point where the members are almost <b>completely in agreement</b>, the ensemble mean can be markedly far from the true value.</p><p>That is, <b>the ensemble agreeing does not mean it is right.</b></p><p>The reason is hidden in the ensemble method\'s assumption. Members are expected to make different errors so that those errors cancel. But these members share <b>the same data, the same architecture, the same loss function and the same optimisation procedure</b>. The only difference between them is random initialisation and data order.</p><p>That is a weak source of independence. Bias coming from anything they share is <b>identical across all members</b> and the ensemble cannot see it; averaging cannot remove it, because the error being averaged is systematic, not random.</p><p>A second warning: <b>with few members the spread is a volatile estimate.</b> You are estimating a standard deviation from a handful of samples; the result does not improve monotonically as the member count changes. So the measured spread is itself noisy.</p>',
  'bayes-ag:4': '<p>The measured picture is clear: ensemble uncertainty gets the <b>ranking right</b> (outside the data is markedly more uncertain than inside) but not the <b>magnitude</b> (coverage far below 95%).</p><p>That directly determines which decisions it can be used for.</p><p><b>Where it works</b> (only ranking is needed):</p><p>· "Does this input look like the training distribution?" Flagging inputs where the spread is abnormally large and routing them to a human. Cheap and effective for out-of-distribution detection.</p><p>· <b>Active learning:</b> choosing the next example to label. Only the answer to "which is more uncertain" is needed here, not an absolute value.</p><p>· <b>Monitoring:</b> a rise in average spread over time is a warning that the data distribution is drifting.</p><p><b>Where it does not work</b> (an absolute value is needed):</p><p>· Numbers presented to a user as a "95% confidence interval".</p><p>· Automatic decisions that threshold on uncertainty ("if σ is below 0.3 do not ask a human").</p><p>· Probabilities feeding directly into a risk calculation.</p><p>Those need a separate <b>calibration step</b>: measure the true coverage on a held-out set and rescale the band accordingly (methods like conformal prediction do this with distribution-free guarantees).</p><p>And when reporting it, this has to be written down: <b>that calibration is valid only inside the training distribution.</b> Outside it there is no guarantee at all, and that is precisely where uncertainty is needed most.</p>',

  'enc-dec:1': '<p>The difference between encoder and decoder architectures is far smaller than most explanations imply. The layers are the same, the attention mechanism is the same, the feed-forward is the same. The only thing that changes is <b>which position can see which</b>: the attention mask.</p><p><b>Encoder (bidirectional):</b> every position sees the whole sequence, past and future. The total number of visible (query, key) pairs is n².</p><p><b>Decoder (causal):</b> every position sees only itself and what came before. A triangular mask. The number of visible pairs is n(n+1)/2.</p><p>The ratio of the two approaches <b>2</b> for long sequences. So the causal mask closes off roughly half the available information.</p><p>This is not a limit of the architecture but a <b>choice</b>, and the choice is made according to the task. The next two steps measure what each direction gains and loses.</p><p>A note: the mask\'s effect on compute cost is not as large as it looks. In practice the attention matrix is usually computed in full and then masked, so the cost is of order n² either way. The ratio here measures <b>visible information</b>, not compute spent.</p>',
  'enc-dec:2': '<p>To measure the gain from bidirectional context a special task is set up: the target at each position is a function of <b>both its neighbours</b>. So part of the answer is on the left and part on the right.</p><p>The valuable thing about this construction is that the answer is <b>computable in advance</b>. A causal model can never see the neighbour on the right; so it can <b>never</b> explain the share of the target\'s variance coming from that neighbour. That gives a closed-form <b>theoretical ceiling</b>.</p><p>Comparing against a theoretical ceiling makes the measurement much stronger. If the causal model sits on the ceiling we know: the model <b>was not trained badly</b>, it did the best it could. What is missing is not capacity or optimisation but <b>the information itself</b>.</p><p>The bidirectional model is expected to solve the same task without hitting that ceiling, because both neighbours are within its view.</p><p>The learned weights tell the same story: the bidirectional model gives the right coefficient to both neighbours, the causal model only to the one it can see.</p><p>The conclusion is general: <b>a model cannot learn information it cannot see.</b> That shows architecture choice is not a matter of "style"; the mask defines the information the model can reach.</p>',
  'enc-dec:3': '<p>Now the task changes: let the model predict <b>the current position itself</b>. That is the essence of language modelling, predicting the next token.</p><p>Two models are compared: one with a causal mask and one whose mask <b>leaks</b> (that is, keeps the value it is meant to predict inside its view).</p><p>The result is predictable: the leaky model gets a <b>perfect</b> score. Look at its weights and the reason is immediately visible; the answer itself is sitting in the input and the model has learned to copy it.</p><p>And the value of that perfect score is <b>zero</b>. At generation time the token to be predicted does not exist yet; the model cannot use that copying path and collapses completely.</p><p>This is <b>data leakage</b> in its purest form and one of the most insidious classes of bug in real projects. The signature of leakage is familiar: <b>a validation score too good to be real.</b> A feature from the future slipping into the input in a time series, or post-diagnosis measurements being used in medical data, are different faces of the same mistake.</p><p>That is why the causal mask is <b>not a constraint but a precondition for generation</b>. During training the model must have access only to the information it will have at generation time. The principle holds well beyond masking: an information asymmetry between training and use is always a bug.</p>',
  'enc-dec:4': '<p>What you measured separates three architecture families naturally.</p><p><b>Encoder-only (the BERT family).</b> Bidirectional context, n² visible pairs. For work that requires <b>understanding</b> the input: classification, tagging, extracting embeddings for search. It cannot generate, because there is no causal order.</p><p><b>Decoder-only (the GPT family).</b> Causal mask, n(n+1)/2 pairs. It can <b>generate</b>. The price is what you measured: every position sees only half the context.</p><p><b>Encoder-decoder (T5, translation models).</b> The input is encoded bidirectionally, the output generated causally. There is no constraint on the input because the whole input is known up front; on the output the constraint is required anyway. It is the natural choice when input and output are different languages or different formats.</p><p>The right question is not <b>"which is better"</b> but <b>"which constraint serves me"</b>.</p><p>A final note of honesty: the present dominance of decoder-only models <b>does not remove</b> the disadvantage you measured. The causal mask still closes off half the context. The reason for the dominance is something else: being trainable on any text with a single objective (the next token), scaling extremely well, and letting one model do many tasks through generation. So the disadvantage was not solved by the architecture, it was <b>compensated for by scale.</b></p>',

  'kuantizasyon:1': '<p>A model\'s memory footprint is the number of parameters times how many bits each is stored in. Training is usually done in 32 or 16 bits. But is that precision necessary for <b>inference</b>?</p><p><b>Quantization</b> rewrites the weights with fewer bits. The most common form is <b>post-training quantization</b>: the model is trained normally, then the weights are rounded, with <b>no retraining</b>.</p><p>The method is uniform: the range between the smallest and largest weight is divided into 2ⁿ levels and every weight is rounded to the nearest level. So a <b>scale</b> and a <b>zero point</b> are stored and everything else becomes an integer.</p><p>The gain is computed directly: against 32 bits, 8 bits is <b>a quarter</b> of the memory, 4 bits <b>an eighth</b>, 2 bits <b>a sixteenth</b>. Lower memory also relieves memory bandwidth, which is often the real bottleneck in large models.</p><p>The cost, however, is <b>not linear</b>. At high bit counts the rounding error stays below the network\'s own noise tolerance and almost nothing is lost. As the bit count falls a <b>threshold</b> is crossed and the error grows abruptly. Where that threshold sits is what determines the decision, and it varies by model and by task.</p>',
  'kuantizasyon:2': '<p>The most common cause of disaster in practical quantization can be summarised in one sentence: <b>a single outlier weight ruins the precision of everyone else in the same tensor.</b></p><p>The mechanism is purely arithmetic. Per-tensor quantization uses <b>one</b> [min, max] range and divides it into 2ⁿ levels. So the size of the rounding step depends directly on the width of the range:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">step = (max − min) / 2ⁿ</p><p>If a single weight is far larger than the rest it stretches the range by itself. The step grows and <b>every other weight</b> is coarsely rounded. The outlier itself is represented well; everyone else pays for it.</p><p>Worth noticing: the outlier weight causes almost no problem in the <b>unquantized</b> model. The model works perfectly well with it. The problem appears only together with quantization.</p><p>This is not a theoretical concern in real models. Systematic outlier activation and weight values are observed in particular layers of large language models, and they are the principal obstacle to quantization below 8 bits.</p><p>The diagnostic rule: <b>an abrupt collapse after quantization is usually the signature of outliers.</b> And the fix, as the next step shows, is not more bits.</p>',
  'kuantizasyon:3': '<p>The outlier is still there. The only thing that changes is <b>where the scale is defined</b>.</p><p>Instead of a single range for the whole tensor, <b>a separate range</b> is used for each row (or each channel). This is called <b>per-channel quantization</b>.</p><p>The logic is direct: the outlier now stretches only the range <b>in its own row</b>. Because the other rows keep their own narrow ranges, their rounding step stays small and their precision is not damaged.</p><p>The gain is striking: with an outlier present, per-channel scaling lowers the error markedly <b>without using a single extra bit</b>. So the improvement here comes not from storing more information but from <b>placing the same information more cleverly</b>.</p><p>The cost is negligible: one scale factor per row, which is nothing next to the weights.</p><p>But it has a limit worth underlining: <b>with no outlier the gain is small.</b> Per-channel scaling is not a cure-all; it is a specific fix for a specific problem (an unbalanced distribution). This is the recurring pattern of this course: every technique pays the price of a problem, and with that problem absent it only adds complexity.</p><p>There are finer versions of the same idea: group-wise scaling (splitting a row into blocks too) or storing outlier weights separately at high precision.</p>',
  'kuantizasyon:4': '<p>Let us put the three measurements together and turn them into a decision.</p><p><b>Memory</b> is directly proportional to the bit count and predictable. <b>Error</b> is not linear: negligible at high bit counts, growing fast once the threshold is crossed at low ones.</p><p>The practical rules:</p><p><b>8 bits should be the default.</b> Its cost is too small to measure and the gain is fourfold. There is not much to debate at this point.</p><p><b>4 bits is a serious option but requires measurement.</b> A setting that looks fine on a general benchmark can be unacceptable on your task. Do not decide without measuring on your own evaluation set.</p><p><b>2 bits and below require special methods.</b> Plain rounding does not work at that level; you need quantization-aware training or advanced methods that compensate for the error.</p><p>Three further warnings:</p><p><b>1 ·</b> What you measured was weight quantization. Quantizing activations is a separate and usually <b>harder</b> problem, because the range of activations varies with the input and calibration data is needed.</p><p><b>2 ·</b> A memory gain is not automatically a speed gain. Speed depends on whether the hardware can actually multiply fast at that bit width; otherwise the weights are unpacked at runtime and only memory is saved.</p><p><b>3 ·</b> Average error can be misleading. Quantization can degrade rare but critical inputs disproportionately; evaluation should use <b>the task\'s own metric</b>, not the average loss.</p>',
  'attention:1': '<p>The meaning of a word in a sentence is not determined on its own. "It" is two letters and means nothing by itself; its meaning comes entirely from the rest of the sentence. A human resolves this instantly, but a model has to <b>compute</b> it.</p><p>Older language models (RNN, LSTM) read the sentence left to right, accumulating into a state. The problem you measured in the RNN lesson kicks in exactly here: the influence of information <b>decays exponentially</b> with distance. Referring back to a distant word falls below that decay.</p><p>The 2017 paper "Attention Is All You Need" asked a different question: instead of <b>carrying</b> information step by step, let every word <b>look directly</b> at the word it needs.</p><p>That is what attention solves. Every position assigns a weight to every position in the sequence and gathers information with those weights. Whatever the distance between two words, the <b>path length between them is 1</b>. There is no chain of multiplications to decay.</p><p>The second gain is at least as important: all those lookups are independent of each other, so they can be <b>computed in parallel</b>. The sequentiality barrier you measured in the LSTM lesson disappears and modern hardware can be used at full capacity.</p>',
  'attention:2': '<p>Attention is built on three roles, all three produced from the same input by different weight matrices:</p><p><b>Query:</b> "what am I looking for?" Every position produces its own query.</p><p><b>Key:</b> "what do I have?" Every position describes how it can be found.</p><p><b>Value:</b> "what do I give if I am found?" The actual content to be carried.</p><p>The mechanism is four steps:</p><p><b>1 · Match score.</b> Every query takes a <b>dot product</b> with every key. A large dot product means the two vectors point the same way, that is "this is what I was looking for".</p><p><b>2 · Scaling.</b> The scores are divided by √d. The reason is technical but important: as the dimension grows so does the magnitude of the dot products, the softmax becomes overly sharp and the gradient all but vanishes. The division prevents that.</p><p><b>3 · Softmax.</b> The scores become weights summing to 1. So every position distributes its attention like a <b>budget</b>: looking more at one thing means looking less at another.</p><p><b>4 · Weighted sum.</b> The values are summed with those weights, giving the position\'s new representation.</p><p>In one formula: <b>softmax(QKᵀ/√d)·V</b>. Every modern language model is this block stacked dozens of times.</p>',
  'attention:3': '<p>Every word has <b>its own</b> attention map, because every word produces its own query. Change the query and where it looks changes completely.</p><p>The patterns that emerge are grammatically meaningful: pronouns look at the nouns they refer to, adjectives at what they qualify, conjunctions at the two sides they join.</p><p>And the point to emphasise: <b>none of these patterns was hand-coded.</b> Nobody told the model to make pronouns look at nouns. The model was trained with a single objective: <b>predict the next word.</b> Grammar, reference and semantic relations emerged as a <b>by-product</b> of achieving that objective.</p><p>This is one of the most striking examples of representation learning: a prediction task that is hard enough and general enough <b>requires</b> learning the structure of the language in order to be solved.</p><p>A note of honesty: it is tempting to read attention maps as an <b>explanation</b>, but care is needed. A high attention weight is not proof that the information contributed to the decision; different attention distributions can give the same output. Attention maps are a <b>hint</b> about what the model is doing, not evidence.</p>',

  'token:1': '<p>A language model cannot read text directly. The text first has to be split into pieces called <b>tokens</b>, each mapping to a number.</p><p>There are two naive routes and both are bad:</p><p><b>Character by character:</b> the vocabulary is tiny (a few hundred symbols) but the sequences get very long. Because the cost of attention grows with the <b>square</b> of sequence length, that is very expensive.</p><p><b>Word by word:</b> sequences are short but the vocabulary explodes. Worse, every word not in the vocabulary is marked <b>unknown</b> and its information is lost completely. In agglutinative languages like Turkish that is a disaster: the number of words derivable from a single root is practically unlimited.</p><p><b>BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)</b> finds the middle ground. The algorithm is surprisingly simple:</p><p>1 · Split the text into characters.<br>2 · Find the <b>most frequent</b> adjacent pair and merge it into a single symbol.<br>3 · Repeat until the desired vocabulary size is reached.</p><p>The result sits somewhere between characters and words: the vocabulary is manageable, the sequences are short, and there are <b>no unknown words</b> (at worst it falls back to characters).</p><p>An interesting side effect: the merges come out <b>meaningful</b>. Although nobody said "find roots and suffixes", merging frequent pairs naturally surfaces roots and affixes. Frequency statistics coincide with the morphology of the language.</p>',
  'token:2': '<p>Tokenization is an invisible layer but it affects <b>everything</b>.</p><p>The critical point: the merges BPE learns depend on the statistics of the <b>corpus it was trained on</b>. If a tokenizer was trained mostly on English text, it splits words in other languages inefficiently. A root that never appeared in the corpus can fall all the way down to individual letters.</p><p>That has four concrete consequences:</p><p><b>1 · Cost.</b> APIs charge per token. If text in one language takes markedly more tokens than the same meaning in English, you pay more for the same work.</p><p><b>2 · Context window.</b> The limit is in <b>tokens</b>, not words. Inefficient tokenization fits less text into the same window.</p><p><b>3 · Quality.</b> When a word is split into meaningless pieces the model has to reconstruct its meaning from the pieces. A split that keeps the root intact is far easier to learn from than one scattered across letters.</p><p><b>4 · Strange failures.</b> Some known weaknesses of language models come straight from here: counting letters, spelling a word backwards, operating at character level. The model sees tokens, not letters.</p><p>The practical consequence: if you are building a multilingual application, <b>measuring</b> how efficient your model\'s tokenizer is in the target language is a real cost item.</p>',

  'transformer:1': '<p>Attention alone is not a transformer; it is one link in a nine-step chain. The whole block is:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">x = x + attn(norm(x))<br>x = x + mlp(norm(x))</p><p>Each piece there solves a specific problem:</p><p><b>Residual connections</b> · The layer\'s output does not replace the input, it is <b>added</b> to it. This is exactly what you measured in the shortcut connections lesson: it opens a path the gradient can travel unmultiplied and makes depth possible. Those two plus signs are the reason a hundred-layer model can be trained.</p><p><b>Normalisation</b> · Applied <b>before</b> each sublayer (pre-norm). As you saw in the batch norm lesson, it keeps the scale of the signal steady layer by layer. Because the work is on sequences, <b>layer norm</b> is used rather than batch norm: the statistics come from a single token\'s features, not from the batch.</p><p><b>MLP (feed-forward)</b> · A small two-layer network running <b>separately</b> on every token. Attention moves information <b>between</b> tokens; the MLP processes each token\'s own representation. They do different jobs and both are necessary.</p><p>A surprising fact: the majority of the parameters are not in attention but in the <b>MLP</b>. The mechanism the lesson is named after uses the minority of the parameter budget.</p>',
  'transformer:2': '<p>The size of a language model is not mysterious; it is computed with a few multiplications. The parameter count of each part of the block follows directly from the architecture:</p><p><b>Attention:</b> Q, K, V and the output projection, all four d×d → <b>4d²</b></p><p><b>MLP:</b> the expanding and contracting layers → roughly <b>3·d·d_ffn</b> (d_ffn is usually around 4d, so ~8d²)</p><p><b>Normalisation:</b> a few d per layer, negligible</p><p>Roughly <b>12d²</b> per block, and <b>12d²L</b> for L layers. To that you add the input and output embeddings: <b>2·vocab·d</b>.</p><p>Two design intuitions follow:</p><p><b>d_model has a squared effect, layer count a linear one.</b> Widening a model is far more expensive than deepening it.</p><p><b>Embeddings dominate in small models and are negligible in large ones.</b> Because the vocabulary size stays fixed while d² grows, the ratio shifts quickly.</p><p>Converting to memory is direct too:</p><p><b>Inference:</b> parameter count × bytes per parameter (2 for fp16). Add the KV cache to that, which grows with the context length.</p><p><b>Training:</b> roughly <b>six times</b> that. The reason: weights + gradients + optimizer states (Adam keeps two extra states per parameter) + intermediate activations.</p><p>That factor of six is the reason methods like LoRA and quantization exist: to decouple the memory of training from the parameter count.</p>',

  'sampling:1': '<p>A language model does not <b>choose</b> a word. It produces a probability for every token in the vocabulary. What makes the choice is the <b>sampling strategy</b> outside the model, and that strategy changes the output entirely.</p><p>The raw scores the model produces are called <b>logits</b>. They are turned into probabilities by the softmax. <b>Temperature</b> is a divisor entering exactly that conversion:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">p_i = exp(logit_i / T) / Σ exp(logit_j / T)</p><p>Its effect is direct:</p><p><b>Small T (→0):</b> the differences between logits are magnified and the distribution <b>sharpens</b>. The most likely token takes almost all the probability. In the limit this is always picking the highest-probability token (greedy).</p><p><b>T = 1:</b> the distribution the model learned, as it is.</p><p><b>Large T:</b> the differences are flattened and the distribution <b>spreads</b>. At a large enough T every token is nearly equally likely, that is random text.</p><p>The right way to measure this is <b>entropy</b>: it gives how uncertain the distribution is, in bits. Low entropy means stable and repetitive output, high entropy means varied and inconsistent output.</p><p>Practical use: low temperature for factual tasks (summarising, translation, code, extraction); moderate for creative writing. Very high temperature rarely helps, because "creativity" and "randomness" are not the same thing.</p>',
  'sampling:2': '<p>Temperature alone does not solve one problem: <b>the long tail.</b></p><p>In a vocabulary of fifty thousand tokens the individual probabilities may be tiny, but <b>their sum</b> is not tiny at all. Over a long enough text a nonsensical token will eventually be picked from that tail. And in a language model an error does not just spoil that one word; every subsequent token is built on the spoiled context. The text derails from there.</p><p>Holtzman and colleagues (2020) documented the phenomenon and proposed two filters:</p><p><b>top-k:</b> keep only the k most likely tokens and zero the rest. Simple and predictable. Its problem: k is <b>fixed</b>. Where the model is very certain (only one sensible continuation) keeping 40 candidates is needless risk; elsewhere, where there really are many sensible continuations, capping at 40 is a needless constraint.</p><p><b>top-p (nucleus):</b> sort the probabilities descending and take tokens <b>until the cumulative sum passes p</b>. The number of candidates varies with the situation: a few tokens when the model is certain, many more when it is unsure. Being adaptive fixes top-k\'s fixed-count problem.</p><p>The two can be used together, and in practice they are tuned alongside temperature. The general approach: moderate temperature + top-p for creative work; low temperature for factual work, where the tail is already almost entirely cut off.</p><p>Worth underlining: these three numbers <b>are not part of the model</b>. The same model with the same prompt behaves entirely differently under different sampling settings. Failing to report the sampling settings when evaluating a model makes the result irreproducible.</p>',
  'kvcache:1': '<p>A language model produces text <b>token by token</b>. To produce each new token it has to look at every token before it.</p><p>In the naive approach the whole sequence is processed <b>from scratch</b> for every new token. But there is a large waste here: the K and V vectors of the earlier tokens come out <b>the same</b> every time, because those tokens did not change.</p><p>The <b>KV cache</b> removes that waste: the computed K and V matrices are stored. A new token computes only its own Q and looks at the stored K, V.</p><p>The difference in cost is a difference in order:</p><p><b>Without a cache:</b> processing the whole sequence at each step, that is 1+2+3+…+N ≈ <b>N²/2</b>.</p><p><b>With a cache:</b> constant work per token, so <b>N</b> in total.</p><p>For long answers that means hundreds or even thousands of times. Without a KV cache, generating long text is practically impossible.</p><p>And it also explains why generation is split into two distinct phases: <b>prefill</b> (processing the prompt in one go to fill the cache, parallel and compute-heavy) and <b>decode</b> (producing token by token, sequential and memory-bandwidth-heavy). Their performance profiles are entirely different.</p><p>The price paid in return is memory; that is exactly the subject of the next step.</p>',
  'kvcache:2': '<p>The KV cache makes computation cheap but makes <b>memory expensive</b>, and this is the real wall for long context.</p><p>The amount to store per token is computed directly:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">2 (K and V) × number of layers × dimension × bytes per number</p><p>The critical point: this amount <b>grows linearly with context length</b> and is independent of the model weights. The weights are fixed; the KV cache accumulates with every token. At a long enough context the cache takes up <b>many times</b> more space than the model\'s own weights.</p><p>And this cost is paid <b>per concurrent user</b>. Every session has its own cache. So the capacity calculation for an LLM service is: concurrent sessions × context length × KV per token.</p><p>The standard fixes:</p><p><b>GQA (Grouped-Query Attention):</b> the query heads stay numerous while the K and V heads are reduced and shared across groups. It saves memory by a direct multiple and is effectively standard today.</p><p><b>PagedAttention:</b> divides the cache into blocks like operating-system paging, preventing fragmentation and allowing sharing between sessions.</p><p><b>FlashAttention:</b> computes attention without ever materialising the attention matrix, lowering the intermediate memory requirement.</p><p><b>KV quantization:</b> storing the cache in fewer bits. The same trade as in the quantization lesson, in a different place.</p><p>So "a one-million-token context" is not so much an architectural achievement as a <b>memory engineering</b> one.</p>',

  'multihead:1': '<p>A single attention distribution can capture a single <b>type of relation</b>. Because the softmax distributes a budget, one position cannot give full weight at once to "which noun am I referring to" and "which verb am I the subject of".</p><p><b>Multi-head attention</b> solves this: instead of one big attention, several <b>parallel</b> attentions are run. Each head has its own Q, K, V projections, so each learns its own point of view. The outputs are concatenated and passed through a single projection.</p><p>In cost terms it is effectively free: the total dimension is <b>divided</b> among the heads. Eight heads means each is one eighth the size; the total parameter count and computation stay the same as with one head. What is gained is not capacity but <b>diversity</b>.</p><p>There are documented specialisations: some heads always look at the previous token (positional), some track syntactic dependencies, some resolve reference.</p><p>But an important note of honesty is needed: <b>most heads are redundant</b>. Michel and colleagues (2019) showed that a large fraction of the heads in trained transformers can be pruned without seriously hurting performance; the work is carried by a small number of specialised heads. So the clean story that "each head learns a grammatical rule" is tidier than reality.</p>',
  'multihead:2': '<p>Look carefully at the definition of attention: every token is multiplied with every token, a softmax is taken, a weighted sum is made. <b>Order appears nowhere.</b></p><p>The consequence is surprising: for pure self-attention the order of the tokens is <b>irrelevant</b>. Shuffle the input and the output is shuffled the same way but the content does not change (permutation equivariance). For language that is a disaster; "the cat chased the dog" and "the dog chased the cat" cannot be the same thing.</p><p>The fix is to add positional information <b>separately</b>. There are three generations of approach:</p><p><b>1 · Sinusoidal (2017).</b> A fixed pattern of sine and cosine waves at different frequencies is added to the embeddings. It is computed rather than learned, and generalises somewhat to lengths not seen in training.</p><p><b>2 · Learned position embeddings (BERT, GPT-2).</b> A separate vector is learned for each position. Simple and effective, but the <b>maximum length is fixed</b>: a position never seen in training has no vector.</p><p><b>3 · RoPE (Llama, Mistral).</b> Instead of adding the position, it <b>rotates</b> the Q and K vectors by a position-dependent angle. The elegant part: because the dot product of two vectors depends only on the difference of their angles, the result naturally encodes <b>relative position</b>. That makes it possible to extend the context window after training (with methods like interpolation) and is the basis of today\'s long-context models.</p>',

  'egitim-llm:1': '<p>When you talk to a chat assistant you are talking not to the result of a single training but to the product of <b>three separate stages</b>. Telling those stages apart is the key to understanding why the model does what it does.</p><p><b>1 · Pretraining.</b> An enormous amount of text, a single objective: <b>predict the next token</b>. No labels, no humans; the text itself is both input and answer. This is called <b>self-supervised learning</b>, and it scales because it removes the labelled-data bottleneck. It takes months and is very expensive.</p><p>At the end of this stage you do <b>not</b> have an assistant. A raw pretrained model is a <b>text completer</b>: ask it a question and it may well produce a list of similar questions instead of an answer, because that is how questions usually appear on the internet.</p><p><b>2 · Supervised fine-tuning (SFT).</b> Training on human-written (instruction, ideal answer) pairs. This stage teaches the model the <b>format</b>: when asked a question, give an answer.</p><p><b>3 · Learning from human feedback (RLHF/DPO).</b> Humans compare answers to the same prompt and mark which they prefer. The model is tuned to those preferences. This stage gives <b>tone, style and refusal behaviour</b>.</p><p>The difference in scale is striking: the data for the last two stages is negligible next to the first. Yet almost all of the assistant behaviour comes from there.</p>',
  'egitim-llm:2': '<p>This step corrects a common misconception: <b>alignment does not add knowledge to a model.</b></p><p>The LIMA study (Zhou et al., 2023) showed this experimentally: with a small but carefully chosen set of SFT examples you can get behaviour close to models aligned with far larger datasets.</p><p>The explanation the authors proposed is the <b>Superficial Alignment Hypothesis</b>: nearly all the knowledge and capability is acquired during pretraining; alignment only selects <b>in what form</b> the model presents that knowledge.</p><p>That has three direct practical consequences:</p><p><b>1 · If you want to add knowledge, fine-tuning is the wrong tool.</b> You cannot teach a model a fact it does not know with a few examples; and even if it appears to work, it will not recall it reliably. The tool for that is <b>RAG</b>.</p><p><b>2 · If you want style, format or domain register, fine-tuning is the right tool.</b> And it is surprisingly effective with little data.</p><p><b>3 · RLHF does not reduce hallucination.</b> This is the most critical point. RLHF optimises the answer humans <b>like</b>. And humans tend to like confident, fluent, decisive answers. The result can push the model to <b>appear</b> more certain; that is, to be persuasive even when it is wrong.</p><p>This is the classic example of the gap between an objective and a metric: what you measure is "did humans like it", what you want is "is it true". The two often coincide, but where they do not, optimisation follows the metric.</p>',

  'rag:1': '<p>A language model has two fundamental limits: it knows nothing after the cut-off of its training data, and it has never seen your private documents.</p><p><b>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</b> solves both: it makes the model <b>read</b> the relevant documents before answering. That is, it extends not the model\'s memory but its <b>context</b>.</p><p>The pipeline has six steps, and each is a distinct source of error:</p><p><b>1 · Chunking.</b> Documents are split into manageable pieces. <b>Overlap</b> between pieces is critical: without it a sentence or a definition can be cut in half and lose its meaning.</p><p><b>2 · Embedding.</b> Each chunk is turned into a vector. The warning from the embeddings lesson applies verbatim here: the query and the documents must be embedded with the <b>same</b> model, and that model must work well in the target language.</p><p><b>3 · Indexing.</b> The vectors go into a vector database. Approximate nearest-neighbour search trades some accuracy for speed.</p><p><b>4 · Retrieval.</b> The question is embedded and the nearest k chunks are pulled.</p><p><b>5 · Reranking.</b> The retrieved chunks are reordered by a stronger model that evaluates them together with the question. Retrieval is fast and coarse; reranking is slow and accurate.</p><p><b>6 · Generation.</b> The selected chunks go into the prompt and the model answers.</p><p>One important detail in the last step: models tend to miss information in the <b>middle</b> of a long context (Liu et al., 2023). So the order in which the chunks are placed matters as much as which ones are selected.</p>',
  'rag:2': '<p>When RAG breaks the first reflex is to blame the model: "it made things up". But the great majority of errors happen at <b>earlier</b> steps, and the right diagnostic order makes that easy to separate.</p><p>The single key to diagnosis is <b>recall@k</b>: prepare a set of questions, mark by hand which chunk holds the right answer for each, then ask: <b>did the right chunk come back in the top k?</b></p><p>That one number splits the problem in two:</p><p><b>Low recall@k → the problem is in retrieval.</b> The model never saw the right information. Nothing you write in the prompt and no choice of model will fix it. Where to look: chunk size, overlap, the embedding model\'s fit to the language, a mismatch between the query\'s wording and the documents\'.</p><p><b>High recall@k but a bad answer → the problem is on the generation side.</b> The model saw the right information but could not use it. Where to look: prompt design, the order of the chunks in the context, requiring citations, unnecessary noise in the context.</p><p>One common reflex and why it is wrong: <b>raising k is not a fix.</b> Retrieving more chunks may bring the right one in, but it brings far more irrelevant text with it; that raises cost and amplifies the "lost in the middle" effect.</p><p>The real gains come from two places:</p><p><b>Hybrid search:</b> combining semantic (vector) search with keyword search. Vector search catches synonyms but can miss exact terms (product codes, proper nouns, error messages); keyword search is the opposite.</p><p><b>Reranking:</b> retrieve broadly, then narrow with a good model.</p>',
  'llm-embed:1': '<p>In the embeddings lesson you saw <b>static</b> embeddings of the word2vec kind: one vector per word. This step shows where that approach collapses.</p><p>The problem is <b>polysemy</b>. A word can have several unrelated meanings. In a static embedding that word has <b>one</b> vector, and that vector is an average of the context statistics of all its meanings.</p><p>The consequence: the vector represents none of the meanings correctly. While unambiguous words separate cleanly into their own semantic clusters, the ambiguous word hangs at a point <b>moderately</b> close to all of them. This is the linguistic counterpart of the observation from the MDN lesson: <b>the average is none of the things being averaged.</b></p><p>The fix is <b>contextual</b> embeddings, and it starts with BERT. The idea: an embedding is not read from a table but <b>computed</b> by looking at the whole sentence. The same word gets a different vector in a different sentence.</p><p>What makes that possible is attention itself: every token draws information from the rest of the sentence while forming its representation. So "context sensitivity" is not an extra mechanism but a natural consequence of the transformer architecture.</p><p>Almost everything meant today by "an embedding model" is of this second kind. It is also why RAG works: a question and a document get nearby vectors when they carry the same meaning, even if the surface words differ.</p>',

  'halusinasyon:1': '<p>"The model is lying" is the wrong frame. Lying requires knowing the truth and hiding it. The model is doing exactly <b>what it was trained to do</b>; the problem is in what it was trained to do.</p><p>Recall the training objective: <b>predict the next token.</b> That objective does not say "be true", it says "produce a likely continuation".</p><p>Imagine the model is asked about a person it has never heard of. In text, what follows the pattern "X graduated from ___ University" is nearly always the name of a university, not "I do not know". The model behaves <b>consistently</b> with the distribution it was asked for and produces a fluent, likely continuation. That continuation is factually wrong but <b>statistically expected</b>.</p><p>Two important consequences follow:</p><p><b>1 · The model does not represent the boundary of its own knowledge.</b> Saying "I do not know" is likely only if saying so is common in that context in the training data. Absence of knowledge and presence of knowledge are not two distinctly flagged states inside the model.</p><p><b>2 · Lowering the temperature is not a fix.</b> Temperature adjusts the sharpness of the distribution, not its content. At T = 0 the model still makes things up; it just makes up <b>the same thing every time</b>. Consistency should not be mistaken for correctness.</p>',
  'halusinasyon:2': '<p>There is even a theoretical result that hallucination cannot be eliminated entirely. Kalai and Vempala (2024) showed that a <b>calibrated</b> language model must hallucinate at a certain rate: for facts appearing only once in the training data the model either makes something up or becomes excessively cautious, with no middle path.</p><p>So the right question is not "how do I eliminate it" but <b>"how do I reduce it and how do I catch it"</b>.</p><p><b>What works:</b></p><p><b>· RAG.</b> Making the model read the right documents before answering. Taking the information from context rather than memory narrows the room for invention.</p><p><b>· Mandatory citation.</b> Making it state which chunk of the context each claim came from. Verifiability is not correctness itself, but it makes checking possible.</p><p><b>· Programmatic verification.</b> Testing the output against a schema, a computation or a database query. Trusting code rather than the model.</p><p><b>· Semantic entropy.</b> Asking the same question several times and looking at how much the answers diverge <b>in meaning</b>. If the model is inventing, inconsistency between answers rises. Because it is measured at the level of meaning rather than words, saying the same thing differently is not penalised.</p><p><b>· Tool use.</b> Handing arithmetic, dates and search to real tools rather than the model.</p><p><b>What does not work:</b></p><p><b>· Low temperature.</b> It only makes the invention stable.</p><p><b>· Instructing it "do not make things up".</b> The model cannot follow that instruction because it does not know what it does not know.</p><p><b>· Just making the model bigger.</b> It lowers the hallucination rate but does not zero it; and a larger model invents more <b>persuasively</b>, making it harder to catch.</p>',

  'perplexity:1': '<p>A language model gives a probability distribution at every step. A good model assigns high probability to the symbol that actually comes next. The standard way to reduce that to a single number is <b>perplexity</b>.</p><p>The definition is the exponential of the average negative log likelihood. But what matters is <b>how to read it</b>:</p><p><b>Perplexity is how confused the model is, expressed as how many options it seems to be choosing among at random.</b> A perplexity of 4 means the same uncertainty as rolling a fair four-sided die. A perplexity of 1 is complete certainty.</p><p>The value of this lesson\'s setup: <b>we build the source</b>, so we <b>know its true entropy exactly</b>. That means the measured perplexity can be tested against a ceiling, which is never possible with real text.</p><p>There are three reference points and all three can be computed in advance:</p><p><b>Knowing nothing:</b> perplexity equal to the alphabet size. Complete uncertainty.</p><p><b>Knowing only symbol frequencies (1-gram):</b> a little better, because some symbols are more common.</p><p><b>Knowing the previous symbol (2-gram):</b> because the source is first order, this is the <b>best</b> achievable value. It is the source\'s <b>irreducible</b> uncertainty; no model can go below it.</p>',
  'perplexity:2': '<p>If the 2-gram sits on the theoretical floor, a longer context should be even better, right?</p><p>The measurement says otherwise: as the context lengthens the result gets slightly <b>worse</b>. The difference is small but the direction is clear and the reason is instructive.</p><p>The source is <b>first order</b>: the next symbol depends only on the previous one. So looking two or three steps back <b>carries no additional information</b>. It is a search for information that does not exist.</p><p>And searching has a price. Going to 3-grams and 4-grams, the number of contexts to estimate grows exponentially. The statistics of each context are estimated from <b>fewer examples</b>, so every count gets noisier.</p><p>This is the language-model form of the bias-variance trade-off:</p><p><b>Short context:</b> high bias (may miss information), low variance (counts are reliable).</p><p><b>Long context:</b> low bias (misses nothing), high variance (counts are noisy).</p><p>The right context length depends on the <b>actual structure</b> of the data, and longer is not always better. The smoothing and backoff techniques developed for classical n-gram models exist precisely to suppress this variance.</p><p>In modern neural models the trade-off does not disappear, it changes form: the model <b>learns</b> which part of a long context carries information and can ignore the rest.</p>',
  'perplexity:3': '<p>Now the critical question: <b>can we compare the perplexity of two models?</b></p><p>The answer, if you are not careful, is no.</p><p>The reason: perplexity is a <b>per-symbol</b> measure. Split the same text into larger pieces and you carry the same information in fewer steps; the information carried per step rises and perplexity <b>grows</b>. Same text, equally knowledgeable model, different number.</p><p>So perplexity <b>depends on tokenization</b>. Putting the perplexities of two models with different tokenizers side by side is meaningless; the lower one may not be the better model but the one that splits into smaller pieces.</p><p>The correct fix is to convert the measure into a unit independent of tokenization: <b>bits per character</b>. Divide the total negative log likelihood not by tokens but by the <b>number of characters</b>. If the text is the same the character count is the same, so the measure becomes comparable.</p><p>Other forms of the same trap: perplexity also depends on the <b>data it was measured on</b>. Two numbers measured on different test sets are not comparable. And if the test data leaked into the training data, perplexity drops artificially; in large models this is a serious and common problem.</p>',
  'perplexity:4': '<p>Perplexity measures one thing very well: <b>how well a model compresses</b> a text distribution. The measurement is robust, cheap and needs no labels. It relates directly to information theory: predicting well and compressing well are the same thing.</p><p>What it does not measure:</p><p><b>Truth.</b> The model can produce facts wrongly but <b>fluently</b>. As you saw in the hallucination lesson, fluency and truth are separate things and perplexity sees only fluency.</p><p><b>Usefulness.</b> Following instructions, hitting the requested format, not padding. None of that shows up in perplexity. Indeed an aligned (post-RLHF) model can have a <b>worse</b> perplexity than the raw model while being far better for the user.</p><p><b>Reasoning.</b> Solving a problem step by step does not reduce to single-step prediction accuracy.</p><p><b>Safety and bias.</b> Perplexity does not care what the content is.</p><p>Its correct use is clear: <b>same data, same tokenizer, one variable</b>. In that setup perplexity is a very sensitive and very cheap indicator: ideal for tracking training progress, comparing two training settings, or catching a change in data quality.</p><p>For choosing a model it is not enough on its own; it should be read alongside task-based evaluations.</p>',
  'olcek-yasalari:1': '<p>A <b>scaling law</b> is an empirical relationship describing how a model\'s loss changes with the amount of data, the number of parameters or the compute budget. The observed form is:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">L(N) = L∞ + A·N<sup>−α</sup></p><p>This is a <b>power law</b> and it is easy to recognise: plotted on log-log axes it gives <b>a straight line</b>.</p><p>The two terms of the formula are entirely different things and should not be confused:</p><p><b>A·N<sup>−α</sup> · reducible loss.</b> The error the model makes because it has not seen enough data. It goes to zero as data grows.</p><p><b>L∞ · irreducible loss.</b> The floor coming from the randomness of the data itself. <b>It belongs to the data, not the model.</b> However much data you collect and however large a model you build, you cannot go below it. It is exactly the <b>irreducible noise</b> term from the bias-variance lesson.</p><p>The value of this lesson\'s setup: because we built the source, we know the true value of L∞ <b>independently</b>. So the fitted law can be tested against a reference.</p><p>The exponent α tells you how <b>efficient</b> scaling is: large means adding data pays off quickly, small means slowly.</p>',
  'olcek-yasalari:2': '<p>What this step tests is subtle: <b>can a scaling law find a number it was never told?</b></p><p>The procedure for fitting L∞ is this: try different candidate values of L∞ and pick the one that makes the log-log plot most <b>straight</b>. The thing to note is that the true entropy is <b>never used</b> in that search; even the upper bound of the search comes only from the observed losses.</p><p>So the L∞ found is an estimate derived entirely from <b>the shape of the curve</b>. Comparing it with the true entropy, which we know independently, is a genuine test of the method.</p><p>The second observation is <b>diminishing returns</b>, and it follows directly from the power law itself. Because α &lt; 1, doubling the data does not lower the loss proportionally. Roughly: to halve the excess loss you have to grow the data by a fixed <b>multiplier</b>, and that multiplier is the same every time. So progress buys <b>linear</b> gain at <b>exponential</b> cost.</p><p>This explains the economics of large models: every new decimal place is many times more expensive than the last.</p><p>And it gets worse as you approach L∞, because the remaining room for improvement has already shrunk. Past a point, enormous resources buy a gain too small to measure.</p>',
  'olcek-yasalari:3': '<p>The real value of scaling laws is not retrospective explanation but <b>forward prediction</b>.</p><p>The practical use: make a few runs with small, cheap models, fit the curve, and predict the loss of a large model <b>you have not trained yet</b>. So you can estimate what you will get before committing to a training run costing millions. This is exactly what the GPT-4 technical report describes: the final performance was predicted from far smaller runs.</p><p>This step tests the same thing under control: the law is fitted using only <b>small-scale</b> points, then large-scale points that never entered the fit are predicted.</p><p>And a very instructive detail emerges here: <b>the exponent can be wrong while the prediction is still right.</b></p><p>The reason: at large N the term A·N<sup>−α</sup> shrinks and the result is determined almost entirely by <b>L∞</b>. So the accuracy of the prediction depends mainly on estimating the floor correctly; the error in the exponent has already faded at large N.</p><p>A practical warning follows: <b>do not trust a reported α on its own</b>. That number is sensitive to the range it was measured over, and different subsets of the same data can give different exponents.</p>',
  'olcek-yasalari:4': '<p>Scaling laws are powerful but not magic. Knowing their limits is a precondition for using them properly.</p><p><b>1 · The fit is not perfect.</b> The points sit on a line but there is scatter, and that scatter turns into uncertainty in the prediction. Reading a number from a single fitted curve as a precise forecast is wrong.</p><p><b>2 · The exponent is fragile.</b> Different subsets of the same data give different α. So claims of the form "this architecture\'s scaling exponent is X" are meaningless without the measurement range.</p><p><b>3 · The law holds only in the regime it was fitted in.</b> Extrapolation works as long as the underlying mechanism does not change. If data quality changes, if the architecture changes, or if the data runs out, the curve breaks. Indeed that is exactly what sits at the centre of today\'s scaling debate: high-quality text is not unlimited.</p><p><b>4 · A scaling law does not say what will improve.</b> What it measures is loss. It does not say which capability a lower loss will turn into; as you saw in the perplexity lesson, loss and usefulness are not the same thing.</p><p><b>5 · And most importantly: L∞ is always there.</b> Scaling lowers the reducible loss, not the irreducible one. Scale solves problems while data is the constraint; it can do nothing about the uncertainty inherent in the data.</p><p>There is also the matter of allocation: the Chinchilla study showed that within a fixed compute budget there is an optimum balance between parameters and data, and that earlier models were too large for the data they saw. So the relationship between "bigger" and "better" depends on how the budget is divided.</p>',

  'oz-gozetim:1': '<p>The idea of <b>self-supervised learning</b> is one sentence: if there are no labels, produce the label <b>from the data itself</b>.</p><p>Part of the data is hidden and the model tries to predict it. This is called the <b>pretext task</b>. In language models it takes the form "predict the next token"; in vision it takes forms like "fill in the masked region" or "pull two crops of the same image together".</p><p>The gain in scale is enormous: even a short document yields dozens of supervised examples, and none of them requires human effort. The labelled-data bottleneck disappears.</p><p>But that is not the real question. The real question is: <b>does solving this pretext task actually teach something useful?</b></p><p>The value of this lesson\'s setup is that it makes the test <b>fair</b>. The data is designed so that raw symbol counts carry <b>provably zero information</b> about the target. That is, knowing only "which symbol appears how often" makes knowing the topic mathematically impossible.</p><p>So if the pretext representation succeeds, the source of that success is beyond dispute: there is no superficial shortcut, and the knowledge can only have been acquired as a <b>by-product of solving the pretext task</b>.</p>',
  'oz-gozetim:2': '<p>The gain from pretraining is usually looked for in the wrong place. Not "the model gets better", but rather: <b>it lowers the number of labels the downstream task needs.</b></p><p>This step measures exactly that. The number of labels is increased from few to many and two representations are compared: the one learned by the pretext task, and the raw one we know carries no information.</p><p>The expected behaviour: with a good representation <b>a handful of labels</b> suffices and accuracy saturates quickly. The reason is the same as in the transfer learning lesson: the hard work, building a coordinate system in which the classes are separable, has already been done. What remains is drawing a simple boundary in that space, and very few examples are enough for that.</p><p>With the raw representation, adding labels <b>does not help</b>. Because the information is not there, no number of labels gets you anywhere. This shows an important distinction: <b>a shortage of labels and a shortage of representation are different problems</b>, and solving the first does not solve the second.</p><p>The point to emphasise: the representation was learned <b>without knowing what the topic was</b>. The pretext task never saw the information "this document is about X". Topic knowledge emerged as a <b>by-product</b> of the structure needed to predict the next symbol well.</p>',
  'oz-gozetim:3': '<p>The appeal of self-supervised learning is that "labels are free". This step makes an important correction: <b>labels being free does not mean raw data is free.</b></p><p>The experiment: the labelled data is fixed at a minimum and the amount of <b>unlabelled</b> data is varied.</p><p>The pretext representation turns out to have an <b>appetite for data</b> of its own. The reason is direct: the pretext task is trying to estimate the statistical structure in the data, and estimates made from little data are noisy. A representation learned from a noisy structure is weak.</p><p>The control group is very instructive here: the raw representation, which we know carries no information, is <b>unaffected</b> by the amount of data. That gives a rule worth remembering: <b>more data does not create a signal that is not there.</b> Data only allows an existing signal to be estimated better.</p><p>The practical counterpart: if a self-supervised pretraining is not working, two different diagnoses are possible and they call for very different things. Either the raw data is <b>insufficient</b> (fix: collect more), or the pretext task <b>never asks</b> about the information you want (fix: change the pretext task). The next step measures the second.</p>',
  'oz-gozetim:4': '<p>This step shows the most important limit of self-supervised learning: <b>what it teaches is determined by the question the pretext task asks.</b></p><p>The experiment is simple: the downstream task is changed. The new task is also readable from the data, and is in fact easier. But the pretext representation <b>cannot solve it</b>, and even falls below a baseline that learns nothing at all.</p><p>The reason is structural. The pretext task learns a <b>frequency</b> representation: how often each transition occurs. And frequency <b>contains no positional information</b>; shuffle the symbols in a sequence and the frequencies stay the same. Because the new task asks about position, that information is simply not in the representation.</p><p>This does not show the representation is "bad". The representation does well the job it was asked to do. The problem is the <b>mismatch between the pretext task and the downstream task</b>.</p><p>The practical rules that follow:</p><p><b>1 · Choosing the pretext task is a design decision.</b> The sentence "we did self-supervised pretraining" says nothing on its own; which pretext task was chosen is what matters. That masking and contrastive learning teach different features in vision is a known example.</p><p><b>2 · The fit cannot be known without measuring.</b> Whether a pretext task will help your task cannot be deduced from theory; it has to be tested with a small downstream trial.</p><p><b>3 · Always include a baseline.</b> What saved the interpretation in this step was the presence of a majority-class predictor: without it, "56% accuracy" could have looked good. No number can be interpreted without a baseline.</p>',

  'icl:1': '<p><b>In-context learning</b> is a model changing its behaviour with no weight updated, purely by looking at the examples in the prompt. That is the surprising part: no training, yet adaptation.</p><p>For this to be genuinely measurable the setup has to be fully controlled, and this lesson does that: the model has seen a certain set of tasks during pretraining and its weights <b>never change</b> thereafter.</p><p>Then the model is given a context: examples from one of those tasks, without being told which. What is measured is how much the model improves as the number of examples in the context grows.</p><p>The reference point is critical: the <b>oracle</b>, a predictor that knows from the start which task it is in. The difference between the two is the <b>excess loss</b>, and it answers the question: "what is the cost of not knowing the task?"</p><p>The excess loss going to zero means the model can infer the task <b>exactly</b> from the context. This way of measuring turns a vague observation like "the model answered better" into a precise number.</p>',
  'icl:2': '<p>The excess loss going to zero is nice, but <b>how</b> does it happen? This step shows the mechanism, and the answer runs against intuition.</p><p>The model holds a <b>belief distribution</b> over the possible tasks. Each new example in the context updates that distribution according to which task it fits better. This is exactly <b>Bayesian updating</b>.</p><p>The way to measure it is <b>posterior entropy</b>: how uncertain the distribution is. With an empty context the entropy equals the logarithm of the number of tasks, that is <b>complete uncertainty</b>: the model prefers no task. As examples are added the entropy falls, that is the model becomes confident about "which task am I in".</p><p>From this comes the lesson\'s main claim: <b>in-context learning is not learning but selection.</b></p><p>The model does not acquire a new capability. It selects, from among the behaviours it already has, the one that best fits the context. This view is known in the literature as <b>implicit Bayesian inference</b>, and it explains why few-shot prompts are sometimes miraculous and sometimes entirely useless.</p><p>It has a direct practical consequence: if a few-shot prompt is not working, <b>adding more examples</b> is usually not the fix. Either the right behaviour is not in the model\'s repertoire, or the examples are pointing at the wrong behaviour.</p>',
  'icl:3': '<p>If the "selection" claim is right it makes a clear prediction: <b>a task that is not in the model\'s repertoire cannot be selected.</b></p><p>This step tests exactly that. The model is given a context from a task it <b>never saw</b> during pretraining.</p><p>The expected and observed behaviour: there is some improvement (because one of the seen tasks resembles the new one to a degree), and then it <b>plateaus</b>. Adding more examples does not break through that wall.</p><p>That wall is the fundamental limit of in-context learning. The context is a <b>selection mechanism</b>, not a training mechanism; because the weights do not change, the set of things the model can do does not change either.</p><p>The practical counterpart is a direct decision rule:</p><p><b>If the model can already do the task but is not hitting it</b> (format, style, what kind of answer is wanted) → prompt engineering and few-shot prompting are the right tools.</p><p><b>If the model cannot do the task at all</b> (no domain knowledge, the required behaviour is not in its repertoire) → prompting will not solve it. What is needed is fine-tuning (for behaviour) or RAG (for knowledge).</p><p>This distinction saves a great deal of wasted time in real projects: months spent on prompts get nowhere if the problem is a missing repertoire.</p>',
  'icl:4': '<p>This is the most important and most uncomfortable measurement in the lesson.</p><p>On the unseen task the model was failing. But at the same time something else happens: <b>the posterior entropy still falls.</b> That is, even on a task it has never seen, the model decides "which task am I in" and becomes <b>confident</b> about its decision.</p><p>The model is confident and wrong.</p><p>The reason follows from the mechanism: Bayesian updating distributes belief only <b>among the options that exist</b>. There is no option called "none of these". As evidence accumulates the probability mass flows to the least bad option and concentrates there. So confidence indicates not that the right answer was found but that <b>the other options were even worse</b>.</p><p>This is the third time this pattern has appeared in the course: in the Gaussian Process lesson and in the Bayesian network lesson the model also came out <b>overconfident</b> away from the data. The common cause is the same: <b>a model\'s uncertainty is only meaningful within its own hypothesis space.</b> If the right answer lies outside that space, the uncertainty measure will not warn you.</p><p>The practical consequence is clear: <b>few-shot prompts cannot be evaluated by how confident the model appears.</b> A fluent, decisive and consistent answer is not proof that it is right. The only reliable route is to <b>measure</b> on a separate evaluation set; and that evaluation set must be independent of the examples you used to design the prompt.</p>',
  'cot:1': '<p>Telling a model to "think step by step" changes everything on some tasks and nothing on others. This lesson measures <b>where</b> the difference lies.</p><p>The setup is deliberately small and fully closed: a function, and the task of applying it a certain number of times. That way we can count <b>exactly</b> what knowledge is required.</p><p>Two models are compared:</p><p><b>Direct answer:</b> maps the question straight to the answer. For that it has to know <b>every one</b> of the (start, number of steps) pairs separately. It cannot know a pair it has not seen, because what it learned is a table.</p><p><b>Chain-of-thought:</b> writes the intermediate steps. For that it only needs to know the <b>single-step</b> function; the rest is applying that one step repeatedly.</p><p>That is the critical point: the chain <b>reduces a compound question to a single question asked repeatedly</b>. The number of facts to learn stops being multiplicative and becomes additive.</p><p>Worth emphasising: the gain does not come from the model "being smarter". It comes from <b>the task being decomposable</b>. The chain exploits structure that is already there.</p>',
  'cot:2': '<p>In this step the training budget is fixed and the only variable is how many steps the question requires.</p><p>The expected behaviour differs between the two models, for the same reason as before:</p><p><b>For the chain model the number of steps is not a difficulty but simply more work.</b> It reads the same single-step table eight times instead of once. What it learned is independent of the step count.</p><p><b>For the direct model every (state, step) pair is a separate fact.</b> A fixed budget covers only part of the table, and which cells get covered is <b>random</b>.</p><p>From this comes a second, general lesson about measurement: the direct model\'s curve does not show a steady decline, it <b>goes up and down</b>.</p><p>When you see a curve fluctuating, the first question to ask is: <b>is this shape a signal or is it sampling noise?</b></p><p>Here we know the answer for certain, because we built the task: the step count has no structural meaning for the direct model, so the fluctuation is <b>noise</b>. In real experiments you are never this lucky; that is exactly why you need repeated runs and confidence intervals rather than reading a trend off a single curve.</p>',
  'cot:3': '<p>The chain has a cost, and that cost is multiplicative.</p><p>Every intermediate step can be wrong. And in this task a chain that goes astray once <b>cannot</b> recover by itself: continuing from a wrong intermediate state leads to a wrong answer.</p><p>If the per-step error probability is ε, the probability that <b>all</b> n steps go right is roughly <b>(1 − ε)ⁿ</b>.</p><p>The practical meaning of that formula is far harsher than most people\'s intuition. An error rate that looks small per step can halve the accuracy after a few steps. That is, <b>a model that looks good per step can easily become unusable per chain.</b></p><p>This is the central mathematical fact about agent systems: reliability over a long chain of actions is step reliability raised to a power.</p><p>A subtlety: (1 − ε)ⁿ is actually a <b>lower bound</b>. A wrong step can by chance put the chain back on the right path. So at high error rates the measured accuracy sits slightly above the theoretical curve. The formula errs on the pessimistic side, which as a safety margin is the desirable direction.</p><p>And this motivates the next two lessons: breaking the error accumulation requires either <b>voting</b> (self-consistency) or <b>intermediate checking</b> (prompt chaining).</p>',
  'cot:4': '<p>Seeing the case where the chain helps not at all is the clearest way to know when to use it.</p><p>The task is changed in one place: the answer is no longer the result of a repeated operation but a value <b>assigned at random</b> to each question. The question looks the same; the <b>structure inside it is gone</b>.</p><p>The result is entirely different for the two models:</p><p><b>The direct model is unaffected.</b> For it the task was always memorisation; whether the thing being memorised is meaningful makes no difference.</p><p><b>The chain model collapses.</b> Writing intermediate steps gains nothing, because there is no relationship between the intermediate steps and the answer. Adding examples does not help either.</p><p>From this comes the lesson\'s main sentence: <b>a chain exploits structure that already exists in the task; it does not create structure from nothing.</b></p><p>The decision rule: <b>does the task really decompose into intermediate steps?</b></p><p><b>If it does</b> (mathematics, multi-step inference, code, logic) the chain both learns from less data and works at any length.</p><p><b>If it does not</b> (direct recall, judgements of style, atomic classification) the gain is zero while the cost of error accumulation remains. So a chain can be not merely useless but <b>harmful</b>.</p>',

  'self-cons:1': '<p>In the previous lesson you measured how error accumulates multiplicatively as a chain lengthens. <b>Self-consistency</b> offers a simple answer to that accumulation: ask the same question K times <b>independently</b> and take the most frequent answer.</p><p>The surprising part: even a model that is <b>wrong most of the time</b> can yield an almost correct system.</p><p>The reason is classic ensemble logic, but with an important difference: here the members are not different models but <b>different samples from the same model</b>. Because the temperature is above zero, each run takes a different path and makes different errors.</p><p>The gain depends on two things, both measurable:</p><p><b>1 · The quality of the model.</b> As the per-step error falls, the same number of votes buys far more. A poor model can approach where a good model gets only with many times more votes, or not at all.</p><p><b>2 · The number of votes.</b> But the return is not linear; the later steps measure exactly that.</p><p>The critical assumption here is that the trials are <b>independent</b>. When independence breaks the method fails; the third step shows exactly that case.</p>',
  'self-cons:2': '<p>Understanding why voting works is the only way to know when it will not.</p><p>The answer emerges when you look at the model\'s <b>distribution</b> of answers.</p><p>The correct answer is a single target. The wrong answers <b>spread across many different targets</b>, because the deviations are random and each run goes astray somewhere else. The result: the correct answer can have a larger share than each individual wrong answer <b>even without holding an absolute majority</b>.</p><p>And that is exactly what a plurality vote needs: the correct answer only has to be <b>first</b>, not above 50%.</p><p>From this comes the method\'s single condition: <b>the correct answer must be the model\'s most likely answer.</b> If that holds, accuracy approaches 1 with enough votes (the law of large numbers). If it does not, more votes means converging <b>more confidently on the wrong answer</b>.</p><p>The sentence to underline: <b>the power of voting comes not from the model being good but from its errors being scattered.</b> If the errors are scattered even a weak signal can be rescued; if the errors pile up, even a strong model cannot be.</p>',
  'self-cons:3': '<p>Now one thing changes: there is a <b>systematic</b> error in the model\'s knowledge. The step noise is the same, but the model consistently believes something wrong in a particular state.</p><p>The critical difference: <b>the noise differs on every run, the wrong belief is the same on every run.</b> The runs do not correct each other; they <b>converge on the same error</b>.</p><p>The result is a <b>hump</b>-shaped curve, and it is very instructive: with a few votes the accuracy first rises (the random noise is cleaned up), then <b>falls</b> as the vote count grows. Because more votes reveal the distribution\'s true peak more sharply, and that peak is now on the wrong answer.</p><p>So <b>voting does not change what the model knows, it only announces what it knows more clearly.</b></p><p>This shows why telling error types apart matters so much:</p><p><b>Random error</b> (sampling noise, carelessness) → voting cleans it up.</p><p><b>Systematic error</b> (wrong knowledge, wrong assumption, bias) → voting <b>amplifies</b> it.</p><p>And in practice they are hard to tell apart, because from outside they look identical: the model is giving a wrong answer. The way to distinguish them is repeated sampling: if the answers are scattered it is noise, if they are consistently the same wrong answer it is systematic.</p>',
  'self-cons:4': '<p>K votes means <b>K times the compute</b>. This step reads that bill.</p><p>The return is not linear but <b>diminishing</b>. The first few votes buy a lot; later ones progressively less. The reason is statistical: the probability that the plurality picks the right answer follows a <b>saturating</b> curve, and as it approaches 1 the contribution of each new vote shrinks.</p><p>The real comparison is this: <b>is it cheaper to fix the model or to buy votes?</b></p><p>The measurement is clear: a model whose per-step error is halved gives much higher accuracy at the same vote count. Bringing the worse model to the same point by adding votes costs many times more compute, and in some cases is impossible at all (the ceiling from the third step).</p><p>So <b>voting is no substitute for model quality</b>; it is a way to squeeze a bit more out of a good model.</p><p>And there is a better alternative: scoring with a <b>verifier</b> instead of a plurality vote. The difference matters: a plurality looks at what is <b>most frequent</b>, a verifier at what is <b>correct</b>. If the correctness of an answer can be checked independently (running the code, substituting into the equation, checking the source) this is both cheaper and more reliable.</p><p>The practical rule: use a verifier on tasks that are <b>hard to produce but easy to check</b>; fall back on voting when checking is hard too.</p>',
  'talimat-ayar:1': '<p>Pretraining teaches a model <b>operations</b> but gives no way to say which one you want. This step makes that gap visible.</p><p>The setup is a closed world: a few different tasks, and a pretraining corpus made of a mixture of them. The critical detail: the corpus <b>does not say which task it is</b>; only the input and the output sit side by side.</p><p>The only sensible thing a model from such a corpus can do is assume the <b>most likely</b> task in the mixture. The result: the model is very good at the dominant task and near random on the others.</p><p>And when the dominant task is changed the peak moves while <b>the average stays almost the same</b>. That is direct evidence that the observed behaviour comes not from the model\'s capability but from <b>the distribution of the corpus</b>.</p><p>From this comes the lesson\'s main sentence: a base model\'s "capability profile" is really <b>the task distribution of its pretraining corpus</b>.</p><p>And the right diagnosis is: the model does not <b>fail to know</b> how to add, it <b>assumes</b> you want addition. What is missing is not a capability but a way to say which capability you want. That is the explanation for why a raw pretrained model does not behave like an assistant.</p>',
  'talimat-ayar:2': '<p>Now an <b>instruction</b> is attached to each task and the model is shown a few examples: "with this instruction, this input gives that output".</p><p>What the model does here is critical: it <b>is not learning a new operation.</b> It is <b>eliminating</b>, from among the operations it knows, those that contradict the examples. If more than one survives it picks the most frequent one from pretraining.</p><p>That mechanism explains why the result comes so <b>fast</b>: eliminating is far cheaper than learning. A handful of examples per instruction lifts the average dramatically.</p><p>It also explains where the difficulty comes from. The difficulty of an instruction is not measured by the <b>complexity</b> of the operation but by how much it <b>overlaps</b> with other operations.</p><p>If two operations give the same answer on a large share of inputs, most of the examples shown <b>do not distinguish</b> them and cannot eliminate. A conceptually very simple operation can be the hardest instruction to learn because it sits so close to its neighbour.</p><p>The practical counterpart is direct: if an instruction is not landing, the problem is usually not its complexity but its <b>discriminativeness</b>. The examples have to be chosen so as to separate the behaviour you want from the neighbour you do not.</p>',
  'talimat-ayar:3': '<p>This step places the <b>scale</b> of instruction tuning next to pretraining, and the contrast is striking.</p><p>The examples come from a single pool and spread across the tasks; that is how real instruction-tuning datasets work. Accuracy reaches the ceiling with a very small total number of examples, and everything after that is wasted.</p><p>The comparison matters: the pretraining needed for the model to <b>learn</b> those operations is many times larger. Making them <b>addressable</b> costs very little.</p><p>This is a controlled model of the result reported in the LIMA study (Zhou et al., 2023): with few but carefully chosen examples you can get behaviour close to that from far larger alignment datasets.</p><p>Two practical consequences:</p><p><b>1 · In instruction-tuning data, coverage matters more than quantity.</b> Adding a thousand examples of the same task contributes almost nothing after the eleventh. The gain comes from adding the first example of a behaviour <b>not yet addressed</b>.</p><p><b>2 · The saturation point should be measured.</b> Continuing to add data after saturation is pure cost; excessive tuning can even do harm by narrowing the model\'s diversity.</p><p>The general frame: <b>pretraining builds the capability, instruction tuning opens a door to it.</b> Their data scales are not the same order of magnitude, and they do not need to be.</p>',
  'talimat-ayar:4': '<p>To see the method\'s limit, a task that was <b>never present</b> in pretraining is added. Everything else is the same: the same instruction format, the same example pool, the same model.</p><p>The result is two separate worlds: the old tasks reach the ceiling while the new one barely moves. Even multiplying the number of examples does not close the gap.</p><p>The reason is directly in the mechanism. On the old tasks the model was <b>eliminating among operations it knew</b>. On the new task there is <b>no correct option</b> to be left standing. One path remains: <b>memorising</b> input-output pairs. And memorisation both demands a lot of data and does not generalise to unseen inputs.</p><p>Hence the lesson\'s main sentence: <b>instruction tuning reveals a capability, it does not create one.</b></p><p>This is the sibling of the result in the in-context learning lesson. There we said "context only selects among existing behaviours"; here the same thing is done with weight updates and hits <b>the same wall</b>. Neither widens the repertoire.</p><p>A very useful diagnostic rule follows:</p><p><b>If the model answers in the right form but with the wrong content</b> → the problem is not in instruction tuning. Content problems are solved with data (retraining with new knowledge) or access (RAG, tool use).</p><p><b>If the model gives the right content in the wrong form</b> → instruction tuning or prompting is exactly the right tool, and it works with little data.</p>',

  'zincir-prompt:1': '<p>"Split a big job into separate calls" is common advice. This step shows that the advice is <b>empty on its own</b>.</p><p>The setup is simple: a job of n steps with a given per-step error probability. Two designs are compared: asking for everything in one call, and splitting into n separate calls.</p><p>If the per-step error is the same in both designs the result is <b>exactly the same</b>: in both cases the probability of finishing correctly is the same product. Splitting does not change the product.</p><p>This is the same (1 − ε)ⁿ calculation as in the chain-of-thought lesson, and it makes one thing clear: <b>splitting is not itself a gain.</b></p><p>So does splitting give nothing at all? It gives something, but not a gain, an <b>opportunity</b>: the intermediate results become <b>visible</b>.</p><p>In a single call, whatever the model did inside, you only see the result. In a split chain the output of every step is in your hands. That visibility makes two things possible: knowing <b>where</b> the error began, and <b>intervening</b> at that point.</p><p>What turns visibility into a gain is the subject of the next step: a checkpoint placed at the seams.</p>',
  'zincir-prompt:2': '<p>The same split chain, with one addition: after each step a <b>checker</b> looks at the intermediate result. If it finds an error, that step is repeated.</p><p>A checker has two numbers and both are necessary:</p><p><b>Catch rate r:</b> the probability of catching a wrong intermediate result (sensitivity).</p><p><b>False alarm rate f:</b> the probability of mistaking a correct intermediate result for a wrong one.</p><p>Expressing it as a single number ("the checker is 90% accurate") is misleading; the system\'s behaviour is set by both together.</p><p>The mechanism of the gain: the checkpoint <b>cuts the multiplicative accumulation</b> of error. If a wrong intermediate result is caught and fixed before it propagates, the following steps start from a clean input. That is, the ε in (1 − ε)ⁿ effectively shrinks.</p><p>The second expected observation is that <b>the return on retries saturates very fast</b>. The first retry brings a large jump; the second and third contribute progressively less; beyond that buys almost nothing.</p><p>The reason is simple: a step failing on both the first and the second attempt is an increasingly unlikely event. A retry targets an event whose probability is already small.</p><p>The practical consequence: <b>keep the retry budget small.</b> Paying beyond three is rarely sensible.</p>',
  'zincir-prompt:3': '<p>A checkpoint does not always help. This step gives the condition <b>exactly</b>.</p><p>The measurement shows that the value a checker adds depends on comparing the catch rate r with the false alarm rate f, and the threshold is precisely at <b>r = f</b>.</p><p>That is, a checker helps only if it <b>catches wrong more often than right</b>. At r = f the checker carries no information at all; it is the same as flipping a coin and saying "repeat". And this is not measurement noise but an <b>algebraic identity</b> that falls out of the formula.</p><p>When r &lt; f the situation is worse: because the checker discards correct results as wrong, accuracy drops <b>below</b> that of the unchecked chain. And you pay extra call charges while doing it. <b>A bad checker is worse than no checker.</b></p><p>A very important practical warning follows: if the checker and the generator <b>are not independent</b>, this condition breaks easily. When you have the same model check its own output, r falls because the model cannot see its own error; and f rises when it doubts its own correct answer.</p><p>That is why these are valuable: a different model, a different prompt, and best of all <b>programmatic verifiers</b> (running the code, validating against a schema, substituting the computation back in). Their r is high and their f near zero, because they do not guess, they <b>test</b>.</p>',
  'zincir-prompt:4': '<p>The last step reads the bill: what does accuracy cost <b>in calls</b>?</p><p>The main finding: <b>the quality of the checker matters more than the number of retries.</b></p><p>A good checker is <b>cheap</b>. If its false alarm rate is low it only repeats genuinely broken steps, so the total call count stays a little above the unchecked chain while accuracy rises markedly.</p><p>A false alarm makes you <b>pay twice</b>:</p><p><b>1 · It grows the bill.</b> Correct steps are repeated for nothing.</p><p><b>2 · It lowers accuracy.</b> When a correct intermediate result is discarded and regenerated, the new attempt may come out wrong. So the checker sometimes <b>breaks what was right</b>.</p><p>That is why looking at a single accuracy number is not enough when evaluating a checker; r and f have to be measured separately. This is the same recall-precision trade-off from the classification lesson, showing up in a different place.</p><p>The general design rule: <b>prompt chaining is not an architecture but an opportunity for control.</b> Its value is determined by what you put at the seams. With no verification at the seams, the only effect of splitting is more calls and more latency.</p>',
  'gramer:1': '<p>When you ask a model for valid JSON, valid SQL or output matching a particular shape you hit a problem: the model <b>does not know</b> the shape, because the shape is a constraint on the form of the output, not something about the statistics of language.</p><p>There are three approaches:</p><p><b>1 · Asking in the prompt.</b> "Return only JSON." It usually works, but it gives <b>no guarantee</b>. And a single malformed output breaks the downstream parser.</p><p><b>2 · Reject-and-retry.</b> Sample, and if it is invalid sample again. It gives a guarantee but its cost is the inverse of the probability of a valid output; as the shape narrows that cost explodes.</p><p><b>3 · Constrained decoding (masking).</b> At each step it zeroes the probability of tokens the grammar <b>does not allow</b> at that point, renormalises the rest and picks from among them.</p><p>The third is very powerful: it produces a <b>valid output in a single call, always</b>. Entering an invalid path is physically impossible, because the tokens that open that path can never be chosen.</p><p>A guarantee this cheap must have a price. The next step shows what it is.</p>',
  'gramer:2': '<p>The price is here: <b>constrained decoding does not sample from "the distribution conditioned on being valid".</b> Those are different things and the difference is not negligible.</p><p><b>Global conditioning:</b> "take the model\'s distribution, discard the invalid outcomes, renormalise the rest", that is p(x | x valid). That is exactly the distribution reject-and-retry gives and it is <b>unbiased</b>.</p><p><b>Local masking:</b> "at each step discard invalid continuations, renormalise the rest, pick, continue". That is what constrained decoding does.</p><p>Why does the difference arise? Because local masking <b>cannot look ahead</b>. When it picks a token at one step it does not know how much that choice narrows the valid continuations later. The model enters a path, then finds the options at the end of that path heavily pruned, and by then there is no going back.</p><p>The result is a measurable bias: some valid outputs come out more likely than they should and others less. More importantly, even the <b>ranking</b> between outputs can be reversed.</p><p>The practical counterpart matters: <b>the model\'s "most likely valid answer" and the answer found by constrained decoding need not be the same.</b> There is work aimed at reducing this gap (grammar-aligned decoding and the like), but plain masking carries the bias.</p>',
  'gramer:3': '<p>Where the bias comes from can be written in one line.</p><p>In masked decoding the total probability of the allowed tokens at each step is some number Z, and the choice is renormalised by <b>dividing</b> by it. The local probability of a sequence is the model probability multiplied by all the 1/Z factors along the path. Global conditioning, by contrast, divides by a single constant, the total probability of validity.</p><p>Simplifying the ratio of the two leaves an <b>inflation factor</b>:</p><p style="font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center">inflation = P(valid) / Π Z<sub>t</sub></p><p>Read it like this: <b>sequences along whose path a lot of probability mass is masked get inflated.</b> That is, paths the constraint forces the model onto while it naturally wanted to go elsewhere come to look more likely than they should. Paths where little is masked get deflated.</p><p>This is not an approximation but an <b>algebraic identity</b>; it holds exactly for every valid sequence.</p><p>And one result is worth underlining: the bias exists <b>even with a perfectly uniform model</b>. So what is responsible is not a bad model but <b>the structure of the grammar</b>. The more selective the constraint and the later it bites, the larger the bias.</p>',
  'gramer:4': '<p>Let us put the two methods\' bills side by side.</p><p><b>Reject-and-retry:</b> the distribution is exactly p(x | valid), so <b>zero bias</b>. Its cost is an expected number of calls equal to the inverse of the probability of a valid output. As the shape narrows or the model drifts from it, that cost explodes fast.</p><p><b>Masking:</b> always <b>one call</b>, always valid. Its cost is the bias, and that bias <b>does not disappear</b> as the model improves; it comes from the grammar.</p><p>In practice masking is used almost always, because the cost difference is enormous. But two consequences have to be known:</p><p><b>1 · Do not use probabilities from constrained decoding as confidence scores.</b> The measured probabilities are systematically distorted. The sentence "the model gave this answer 90% probability" loses its meaning under constraint.</p><p><b>2 · Separate reasoning from formatting.</b> Forcing the model into a rigid schema from the very first token also prunes the path it needs to think along. A better arrangement: reason first in a free call, then convert that result to the schema in a separate call. That way the constraint applies only to the <b>formatting</b> step and does not distort the thinking.</p><p>The general principle: <b>a constraint guarantees validity but not correctness.</b> A perfectly formed and completely wrong JSON is caught by no parser.</p>',
  'hafiza:1': '<p>First let us correct a common misunderstanding: <b>the model does not remember the conversation.</b> Every call is independent; all the model knows is what you sent it in that call.</p><p>So what we call "memory" is not a property of the model but a <b>design decision of yours</b>: which part of the past will you carry?</p><p>The most common strategy is the <b>sliding window</b>: carry the last W turns verbatim and discard the rest.</p><p>The recall curve is a <b>step</b>: everything inside the window is perfect, everything outside is zero. There is no in-between.</p><p>That has two consequences:</p><p><b>1 · Overall recall is W/T.</b> So as the conversation lengthens the same window remembers progressively less. The window is fixed while the conversation grows.</p><p><b>2 · Forgetting is sharp.</b> Information that falls one turn outside the window is lost completely; not even a partial trace remains.</p><p>And this calculation is actually <b>optimistic</b>, because it assumes "if it is in the context, it is remembered". The "lost in the middle" effect from the RAG lesson weakens that assumption as the window grows.</p>',
  'hafiza:2': '<p>The second strategy: after every turn, compress the past into a <b>summary</b> and carry only that. The carried context then stays fixed however long the conversation gets.</p><p>But compression is <b>lossy</b>. The probability that a fact survives a single round of summarisation is below 1. And the critical point: a fact is summarised <b>again</b> every turn. A fact of age y has been summarised y times, so its survival probability is ρ<sup>y</sup>.</p><p>So the summary\'s form of forgetting is <b>exponential</b>: no sharp wall but a gradual <b>fade</b>.</p><p>That gives a practical reading: every value of ρ has a <b>half-life</b>. How many turns it takes for a fact\'s chance of being recalled to halve is computed directly, and that is the summary\'s real memory horizon.</p><p>And that horizon corresponds to a particular window size. So <b>a summary is not unlimited memory</b>; it is a differently shaped limit. The window says "perfect nearby, nothing far away"; the summary says "a diminishing chance at every age".</p><p>An additional risk: because summarisation is lossy, error accumulates too. Information summarised incorrectly on one turn enters the next summaries <b>in its wrong form</b> and cannot be corrected.</p>',
  'hafiza:3': '<p>The third strategy: keep the past as it is, but on each question retrieve only the few <b>relevant</b> turns. This is RAG applied to a conversation.</p><p>This time the recall curve is <b>flat</b>. Information from thirty turns ago arrives with the same probability as yesterday\'s, because the retriever looks at <b>relevance rather than distance</b>. The only thing setting the height of the curve is the retriever\'s quality.</p><p>Putting the three shapes side by side makes the decision easy:</p><p><b>Window · step.</b> Perfect for recent events, nothing for old ones.</p><p><b>Summary · exponential.</b> A diminishing chance at every age; nothing is lost entirely but nothing is guaranteed either.</p><p><b>Retrieval · flat.</b> Independent of age; the only structure that scales.</p><p>The critical observation: as the conversation lengthens, <b>the overall recall of the window and the summary falls while retrieval\'s does not.</b> Retrieval is the only strategy that scales for long conversations.</p><p>But in practice all three are used <b>together</b>, and that is no accident: their weaknesses are in different places. The window preserves the flow and tone of recent turns, the summary holds the long-term gist, retrieval brings back distant and specific facts.</p>',
  'hafiza:4': '<p>Now let us compare the same three strategies on the <b>cost</b> axis: how many turns are you carrying per call?</p><p><b>Window:</b> recall is directly proportional to the turns carried. Double the recall means double the context, that is double the tokens, double the money and more latency.</p><p><b>Summary:</b> carries a single turn. So the context cost is constant and independent of conversation length. Getting the same recall with a window would cost far more tokens.</p><p><b>Retrieval:</b> carries a few turns, and recall depends on the retriever\'s quality rather than on the amount carried.</p><p>In recall per unit of context, the summary and retrieval are markedly more efficient than the window.</p><p>But there are <b>hidden costs</b> that do not appear on the context bill:</p><p><b>Summary:</b> requires an extra model call every turn. So latency rises and the total number of calls doubles.</p><p><b>Retrieval:</b> requires an index, embedding cost and maintenance. It also adds one more source of error: retrieving the wrong thing.</p><p>And remember the assumption under all of it: these calculations rest on "<b>if it is in the context, it is remembered</b>". That assumption weakens as the window grows, so very large windows are not as good as they look on paper.</p>',

  'cokdilli:1': '<p>A common frame is wrong: it is not that a model "does not know" a language; it is that <b>the tokenizer does not recognise it</b>. And the reason is entirely mechanical.</p><p>Recall the algorithm from the BPE lesson: at each step the <b>most frequent</b> character pair is merged. The critical point: the criterion "most frequent" is <b>blind to language</b>.</p><p>A pair\'s frequency is directly proportional to the corpus share of the language it appears in. So <b>the merge budget is allocated by corpus share</b>. If most of the corpus comes from one language, most of the early merges go to that language\'s patterns.</p><p>The result is unavoidable: the dominant language\'s words stay <b>whole</b> while minority languages\' words get fragmented.</p><p>This is not a bug or an oversight but <b>a consequence of BPE\'s definition</b>. The algorithm carries no "be fair" constraint; it only follows frequency. And frequency is determined by who filled the corpus.</p><p>A direct consequence follows: this is a <b>data decision</b>, not a matter of language ability. So it is fixable; you will see how in the last step.</p>',
  'cokdilli:2': '<p>To see the mechanism concretely, words from different languages are split with the same tokenizer.</p><p>There is an important methodological detail: the words measured are <b>not</b> in the tokenizer\'s training list. That is deliberate, because measuring a tokenizer on a word it memorised would be meaningless; there it gives a single token anyway. Real behaviour shows when generalisation is tested.</p><p>The difference you see is a real measurement but on a <b>synthetic corpus</b>. So the absolute numbers here are not the numbers of real models; what it demonstrates is the <b>mechanism</b>.</p><p>For the magnitude in real models there are separate studies: Petrov and colleagues (2023) measured that the cross-language fragmentation gap in commercial tokenizers reaches up to fifteen times for some language pairs.</p><p>Keeping that distinction matters: <b>a simulation proves a mechanism, not a magnitude.</b> The only way to learn the number that applies to your application is to measure it on your own text with your own model. That measurement takes a few minutes and is far better than a guess.</p>',
  'cokdilli:3': '<p>Fragmentation can be reduced to a single number: the <b>fertility ratio r</b>, how many times more tokens the same content costs relative to the dominant language.</p><p>Everything after that is not measurement but <b>pure arithmetic</b>. r multiplies three costs at once:</p><p><b>1 · Price.</b> If you pay per token, the same text costs r times more. r times the money for the same work.</p><p><b>2 · Context.</b> Because the window is fixed in tokens, only <b>1/r</b> of the text fits into the same window. So "a 128k-token window" is not a language-independent promise; how much text it means varies by language.</p><p><b>3 · Latency.</b> Because generation is token by token, giving the same answer takes r times longer.</p><p>All three follow from the same number, so measuring r answers three questions at once.</p><p>And the source of the cost has to be named correctly: it comes <b>not from the model not knowing the language but from the tokenizer not recognising it</b>. That distinction matters in practice, because there is nothing you can do about the former while the latter is an engineering decision.</p>',
  'cokdilli:4': '<p>Since the source of the problem is corpus balance, the fixes start there too. All three are real and all three have a price:</p><p><b>1 · Balance the corpus.</b> Artificially raise the share of minority languages when sampling for tokenizer training. XLM-R does this with an exponential resampling (Conneau et al., 2020). <b>The price:</b> some loss of efficiency in the dominant language, because the merge budget is finite and what is given to one is taken from another.</p><p><b>2 · Grow the vocabulary.</b> More merges means more pieces for everyone. <b>The price:</b> the embedding table grows (parameters and memory), and more importantly <b>the early merges still go to the dominant language</b>. So growing the vocabulary reduces the imbalance but does not remove it.</p><p><b>3 · A language-specific tokenizer.</b> Train a separate tokenizer for the target language. It gives the most efficient segmentation. <b>The price:</b> cross-language transfer is lost; the greatest advantage of a multilingual model is that languages share a common representation.</p><p>The shortest thing to do on the application side: <b>measure the tokens per word on your own text.</b> All three of your cost, context and latency estimates depend on that one number, and it is written in no documentation.</p>',

  'alan-model:1': '<p>"Should we use a general model or train one specific to our domain?" This question is usually debated as a matter of preference. It is in fact a matter of a <b>measurable threshold</b>.</p><p>The setup has two domains: a <b>general domain</b> with plentiful data and a <b>target domain</b> whose data is scarce and expensive. The relationship between them is controlled by a single number, the <b>angle</b> between their decision boundaries: zero degrees means the domains are identical, ninety degrees means entirely unrelated.</p><p>Two models are compared: the <b>specialist</b>, trained only on target-domain data, and the <b>generalist</b>, which combines both domains\' data.</p><p>The expected behaviour: with little data the generalist leads (the extra data is valuable), and as data grows the specialist takes over. The two curves <b>cross</b> at some point.</p><p>What happens after the crossing has a name: <b>negative transfer</b>. The general data pulls the model away from the target domain\'s right answer; it is no longer a help but an <b>obstacle</b>.</p><p>The right question is not "which is better" but <b>"where is the crossing and which side am I on"</b>.</p>',
  'alan-model:2': '<p>This step isolates what the threshold depends on, and the answer is a single variable: <b>the distance between the domains</b>.</p><p>The pattern is clear:</p><p><b>If the domains are identical</b> the specialist never takes over at any amount of data. That is expected, because the general data here is target-domain data verbatim. In that case building a separate specialist model is <b>wasted effort</b>.</p><p><b>As the domains grow apart the threshold falls fast.</b> With very different domains even a handful of examples is enough to put the specialist ahead, because the information the general data carries points the wrong way.</p><p>From this comes a way to test a claim heard very often in real projects: <b>"our domain is special."</b></p><p>That claim is valid only if supported by two things:</p><p><b>1 ·</b> Is the domain really distant? (Measurable; the subject of the next step.)</p><p><b>2 ·</b> Is there enough domain data to pass the threshold? (Countable.)</p><p>Both are measurable questions. And in practice the second condition often fails: the domain really is different but there is not enough data to train a specialist. In that case the right answer is not a specialist model.</p>',
  'alan-model:3': '<p>In real life you cannot measure the distance between domains directly; there is no "angle". But you can measure a <b>proxy</b>, and this step shows exactly that.</p><p>The method: take a model trained only on the general domain, which has seen <b>no examples</b> from the target domain, and test it on the target domain. The number that comes out is the <b>raw value of transfer</b>.</p><p>Reading it is direct:</p><p><b>If the result is close to the noise ceiling</b> the domains are in fact close. The general data carries almost everything about the target domain.</p><p><b>If the result is close to random</b> the domains are unrelated. The general data carries nothing and will probably do harm.</p><p>The practical value of this measurement is large, because it takes <b>a single run</b> and <b>requires no</b> labelled target-domain data (beyond a small test set). So it is the cheapest measurement you can make before deciding.</p><p>And what it tells you feeds directly into the next step\'s decision rule: if the value of transfer is high, do not build a separate model; if it is low, compute the threshold.</p>',
  'alan-model:4': '<p>Putting the three measurements together gives the decision rule:</p><p><b>1 · Test the general model on the target domain.</b> If the result is close to the noise ceiling the domain is not distant; building a separate model is probably wasted effort. Spend your time on data quality or the prompt instead.</p><p><b>2 · Count your domain data.</b> Below the threshold, use the generalist. Above it, the specialist wins.</p><p><b>3 · In the region between there is a third path</b> and it is usually the best: start from the general model and do <b>continued training on domain data</b>. Gururangan and colleagues\' (2020) work on domain-adaptive pretraining documents this.</p><p>Why the third path works well follows from this lesson\'s mechanism: training jointly makes the general data a <b>weight that keeps pulling</b> throughout training. Continued training makes it only a <b>starting point</b>. So the gain from the transfer learning lesson is preserved while the source of negative transfer is removed.</p><p>And the warning from the transfer lesson applies here too: with little data, continued training can cause catastrophic forgetting. A low learning rate, early stopping and protecting the lower layers are the standard precautions.</p>',
  'temel-model:1': '<p>The phrase <b>foundation model</b> brings scale to mind. This lesson says something different: a foundation model\'s value comes not from its size but from <b>the shared representation it finds at the intersection of many tasks</b>. And that intersection is measurable.</p><p>The setup: a high-dimensional feature space, where the answer to every task in fact lies within <b>a small shared subspace</b> of it. No single task knows this; each only learns its own full-dimensional weight vector.</p><p>Then the <b>dominant shared directions</b> of the learned vectors are extracted and compared with the true subspace.</p><p>The expected behaviour: recovery improves as the number of tasks grows. And the most important point here is why recovery is limited with few tasks: <b>this is not a training problem but an information problem.</b> Two vectors cannot span a three-dimensional space; however well you train, the information is simply not there.</p><p>This is the concrete form of the "shared representation" idea: the structure is not visible inside any single task, it appears only <b>at the intersection of tasks</b>. And that is exactly why multi-task pretraining learns something different from single-task training.</p>',
  'temel-model:2': '<p>Now a <b>new</b> task that fits the shared structure arrives and we have very few examples. Two routes: train from scratch in the full dimension, or train in the <b>small representation</b> the foundation model learned.</p><p>A methodological note: every number is the average of several independent draws of the data. A single few-shot draw would not be meaningful, because the result depends heavily on which examples came up. Reading a result from a single run when working with little data is the mistake this course warns about repeatedly.</p><p>The expected result: the model trained in the representation reaches, with <b>very little data</b>, where the from-scratch model gets with far more.</p><p>The source of the gain is clear and it is not the model\'s "intelligence": <b>the place to search has shrunk.</b> Finding the right direction in a high-dimensional space takes many examples; finding it in a few dimensions takes very few. This is the direct inverse of the calculation in the curse of dimensionality lesson.</p><p>The same mechanism appeared in the transfer learning lesson too: the hard work is learning a representation, and once done it need not be done again.</p>',
  'temel-model:3': '<p>This step shows the least discussed side of foundation models: <b>a good foundation is a floor, a bad one is a ceiling.</b></p><p>Training on a good representation beats from-scratch training at every data amount and settles on the noise ceiling.</p><p>With a weak representation the situation reverses: the model <b>plateaus at a low point</b> and adding data does not rescue it. Training from scratch on the same data does better.</p><p>The reason is in the mechanism and it is irreversible: <b>projecting onto a representation erases</b> the directions outside it. The erased information cannot be recovered by any amount of later data, because the model can no longer see those directions.</p><p>So <b>the ceiling of a frozen representation is the quality of that representation.</b></p><p>The practical counterpart is a direct design decision:</p><p><b>If you trust the representation, freeze it.</b> It gives fast, stable results from little data.</p><p><b>If you do not, unfreeze it.</b> But then the warning from the transfer lesson returns: unfreezing everything with little data leads to catastrophic forgetting. The middle path is adding a few free layers on top of the representation, or unfreezing gradually.</p>',
  'temel-model:4': '<p>The final experiment shows the limit in its sharpest form: the information carried by the new task is <b>orthogonal</b> to the subspace shared by the pretraining tasks. So the shared representation holds <b>no information at all</b> about this task.</p><p>The result is unambiguous: training in the representation stays at chance level however much data is added. Training from scratch on the same data works fine.</p><p>What is happening is simple: the projection <b>zeroes out</b> the direction carrying all of the task\'s information. The model is not losing that information, it <b>never sees it</b>. And no number of examples can teach it something it cannot see.</p><p>This is a clean example of the class of "problems not solved by adding data". The diagnostic rule: <b>if accuracy does not move at all as data grows, the problem is not data.</b></p><p>The fix changes accordingly: not adding data but <b>unfreezing or replacing the representation</b>.</p><p>The general frame: a foundation model is an enormous accelerator <b>within the area its pretraining tasks cover</b>. Outside that area it is not merely useless but an outright <b>obstacle</b>. So the real question when adopting a foundation model is not "how big is it" but <b>"what was it trained on"</b>.</p>',

  'tokenizer-fark:1': '<p>Two different models split the same text into different numbers of pieces. That is not a quality difference; where the difference comes from is written in <b>which corpus the tokenizer was trained on</b>.</p><p>In this step a real BPE tokenizer is trained and the corpus is deliberately unbalanced: mostly natural language, with numbers and code fragments far rarer. An imbalance resembling the composition of real corpora.</p><p>The unit of measure is <b>tokens per character</b>: lower is better, meaning the same text expressed with fewer pieces. And the measurement is made on words <b>not seen</b> in training, because measuring on a memorised word is meaningless.</p><p>The expected behaviour as the vocabulary grows: <b>the merge budget goes to the text type that is frequent in the corpus.</b> Natural language improves quickly, code and numbers lag behind.</p><p>Numbers have a special case: a value of 1.0 tokens per character means <b>every digit is its own token</b>. And growing the vocabulary does not fix that, because digit sequences do not repeat often enough in the corpus.</p><p>It is also worth seeing that vocabulary size is not a wish: when no frequent pair is left to merge, training <b>stops</b>. The vocabulary is as large as the corpus permits.</p>',
  'tokenizer-fark:2': '<p>Examples from three different text types are split with the same tokenizer, and the split points are very instructive.</p><p>Natural-language words separate into <b>meaningful</b> pieces: roots and affixes appear separately. The reason is not semantic but statistical: those pieces got a share of the merge budget because they are frequent in the corpus. That the morphology of a language coincides with frequency statistics is BPE\'s good fortune.</p><p>Numbers, on the other hand, are split in <b>strange places</b>. There is no mathematical meaning to a number being cut at a particular point; that split point is merely the result of that digit sequence having occurred in the corpus.</p><p>The general rule: <b>split points have a statistical rather than a semantic cause.</b></p><p>And it has a measurable cost. How numbers are split directly affects models\' arithmetic performance (Singh and Strouse, 2024). If a number is split inconsistently, the model meets a different representation each time and learning place value becomes harder. Indeed some models prefer to tokenise digits <b>one by one</b> for exactly this reason.</p><p>The conclusion: two models splitting the same text differently is not a quality difference but a <b>corpus difference</b>.</p>',
  'tokenizer-fark:3': '<p>The difference between tokenizers has four concrete consequences, all four touching everyday engineering decisions:</p><p><b>1 · Token counts do not transfer.</b> Text costing a certain number of tokens in one model can cost a very different number in another. The answer to "does it fit in the context window" varies by model and cannot be guessed.</p><p><b>2 · Price comparisons are incomplete.</b> Putting two providers\' per-million-token prices side by side assumes both split the same text into <b>the same number</b> of tokens. If they do not, the cheap-looking option can turn out expensive. The right comparison is not unit price but <b>total cost for your own text</b>.</p><p><b>3 · For number- and code-heavy work the tokenizer is a model selection criterion.</b> A model that leads on natural-language benchmarks can fall behind on numerical work because of an inefficient tokenizer.</p><p><b>4 · Chunk limits should be set in tokens.</b> A limit like "500 characters" in a RAG pipeline costs very different numbers of tokens across text types and your context budget drifts.</p><p>The shared answer to all four is the same: <b>measure.</b> With your own text and your own model\'s tokenizer it takes a few minutes and is far better than guessing.</p>',

  'llm-siniflandirici:1': '<p>Asking an LLM "is this review positive" works, and it works <b>immediately</b>. So when should you move to a small model? That is a measurable question.</p><p>The <b>shapes</b> of the two approaches are entirely different:</p><p><b>LLM:</b> its accuracy is <b>independent</b> of the number of labels. The same with zero labels as with a thousand. So it is a horizontal <b>baseline</b>: obtained immediately and it does not improve by itself.</p><p><b>Small model:</b> its accuracy <b>rises</b> with labels and settles on the noise ceiling at some point.</p><p>The two curves cross. The decision is a matter of where the crossing is.</p><p>And the measured crossings are usually <b>lower</b> than people assume. The intuition that "training a model takes thousands of labels" is often wrong for simple classification tasks: a few dozen well-chosen labels can be enough to beat an LLM prompt.</p><p>So the right reflex is not to argue about "which is better" but to <b>collect a small labelled set and draw the curve</b>. Collecting a hundred labels is usually an hour\'s work and moves the decision from guessing to measuring.</p>',
  'llm-siniflandirici:2': '<p>Accuracy is not the only axis. The <b>shapes</b> of the costs are entirely different too, and the decision is often made here.</p><p><b>LLM:</b> you pay on every call. Cost rises <b>linearly</b> with volume and never saturates. Negligible at low volume, the dominant line item at high volume.</p><p><b>Small model:</b> the cost is almost entirely a <b>one-off</b> labelling expense. After that the inference cost is incomparably lower.</p><p>The break-even point is a simple division: total labelling cost ÷ cost per call. That single formula directly answers "after how many requests does the small model pay for itself".</p><p>And two extra items should not be forgotten: the small model\'s <b>latency</b> is also far lower (a local computation instead of an LLM call over the network) and in some products that matters more than cost.</p><p>From this comes a common and well-working design pattern:</p><p><b>Start with the LLM</b> (get it working immediately, validate the product) → <b>label with the LLM</b> (collect data from production traffic) → <b>move to a small model</b> (once volume grows).</p><p>This is the practical, modern form of <b>distillation</b>: transferring a large model\'s knowledge to a small, cheap one through data.</p>',
  'llm-siniflandirici:3': '<p>There are three problems easily missed when using an LLM as a classifier:</p><p><b>1 · The scores are not calibrated.</b> Using the probability the LLM assigns to the "yes" token as a confidence score is common but dangerous. Zhao and colleagues (2021) showed that in few-shot classification these scores are sensitive even to the <b>order</b> of the examples, their format and which class comes first. They should not be used without correction.</p><p><b>2 · Class balance cannot be tuned with a threshold.</b> In a small model you can shift the threshold and pick any point you want on the recall-precision trade-off. With an LLM there is no direct way to turn that dial; you are left trying to do it indirectly and unpredictably through the prompt.</p><p><b>3 · Behaviour changes without notice when the provider updates a version.</b> Same prompt, same code, different result. A model you trained yourself does not change unless you change it. In production this is one of the most common sources of silent degradation, and the countermeasure is a <b>regression evaluation set</b> run regularly.</p><p>Against that, there are three cases where the LLM is unambiguously superior: <b>if the class definitions change often</b> (no retraining needed), <b>if there are very many classes</b> or they are open-ended, and <b>if no labels can be collected at all</b>.</p><p>The right question is not "which is better" but <b>"how stable is this job"</b>. Stable, high-volume work goes to a small model; changeable, low-volume work goes to the LLM.</p>',
/*SON*/
};
